[[17592186405039 1000 17592186405040 17592186405038] [17592186405039 1000 17592186405041 17592186405038] [17592186405039 1000 17592186405042 17592186405038] [17592186405039 1000 17592186405043 17592186405038] [17592186405039 1000 17592186405044 17592186405038] [17592186405039 1000 17592186405045 17592186405038] [17592186405039 1001 "OSU-MTH" 17592186405038] [17592186405039 1002 #uuid "3bf14d1a-f9f9-51e5-9404-d0b3fa5f729b" 17592186405038] [17592186405040 1004 17592186406336 17592186406335] [17592186405040 1004 17592186406337 17592186406335] [17592186405040 1004 17592186406338 17592186406335] [17592186405040 1004 17592186406339 17592186406335] [17592186405040 1004 17592186406340 17592186406335] [17592186405040 1004 17592186406341 17592186406335] [17592186405040 1004 17592186406342 17592186406335] [17592186405040 1004 17592186406343 17592186406335] [17592186405040 1004 17592186406344 17592186406335] [17592186405040 1006 "Differential Calculus" 17592186405038] [17592186405040 1007 "Differential Calculus introduces the mathematics of change for real-valued functions of one variable. Building on algebra and trigonometry, the course develops the concepts and methods needed to interpret derivatives, analyze functions, and solve problems involving changing quantities. Graphical, numerical, and symbolic representations connect computational techniques with mathematical reasoning and applications." 17592186406335] [17592186405040 1008 "Limits and continuity provide the conceptual framework for studying derivatives. Students connect average rates of change with instantaneous rates, then use limit laws and differentiation rules—including the chain rule—to calculate and interpret derivatives. The emphasis is on understanding what these calculations represent, rather than treating differentiation solely as algebraic manipulation.\n\nApplications connect derivatives to motion, the shape of a graph, and maximum and minimum problems. Students use calculus to analyze velocity, speed, and acceleration; investigate how functions change; and solve optimization problems. Mathematical modeling includes identifying relevant variables, making appropriate assumptions, and evaluating whether a method and its conclusions make sense in context." 17592186406335] [17592186405040 1009 "Oregon State University" 17592186405038] [17592186405040 1011 "MTH 251" 17592186405038] [17592186405040 1012 #uuid "b2e5af5c-39d0-53ab-8fc6-a2a70c44d25e" 17592186405038] [17592186405041 1004 17592186406389 17592186406335] [17592186405041 1004 17592186406390 17592186406335] [17592186405041 1004 17592186406391 17592186406335] [17592186405041 1004 17592186406392 17592186406335] [17592186405041 1004 17592186406393 17592186406335] [17592186405041 1004 17592186406394 17592186406335] [17592186405041 1004 17592186406395 17592186406335] [17592186405041 1004 17592186406396 17592186406335] [17592186405041 1004 17592186406397 17592186406335] [17592186405041 1006 "Integral Calculus" 17592186405038] [17592186405041 1007 "Integral Calculus studies accumulation and integration for functions of one variable. Building on differential calculus, the course develops definite and indefinite integrals and their applications. Graphical, numerical, and symbolic approaches support the interpretation of integrals and their use in mathematical modeling and problem solving." 17592186406335] [17592186405041 1008 "The course connects Riemann sums, definite integrals, and antiderivatives through the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus. Students develop integration techniques and use numerical approximation alongside analytical methods. These tools support applications to areas, volumes, curve lengths, motion, force, and work. Logarithmic and exponential functions, polar coordinates, improper integrals, and elementary growth and decay models extend the range of problems addressed." 17592186406335] [17592186405041 1009 "Oregon State University" 17592186405038] [17592186405041 1011 "MTH 252" 17592186405038] [17592186405041 1012 #uuid "72faeb91-1b34-5252-b9a7-456659fc2b8c" 17592186405038] [17592186405042 1004 17592186406355 17592186406335] [17592186405042 1004 17592186406356 17592186406335] [17592186405042 1004 17592186406357 17592186406335] [17592186405042 1004 17592186406358 17592186406335] [17592186405042 1004 17592186406359 17592186406335] [17592186405042 1004 17592186406360 17592186406335] [17592186405042 1004 17592186406361 17592186406335] [17592186405042 1004 17592186406362 17592186406335] [17592186405042 1004 17592186406363 17592186406335] [17592186405042 1004 17592186406364 17592186406335] [17592186405042 1004 17592186406365 17592186406335] [17592186405042 1004 17592186406366 17592186406335] [17592186405042 1004 17592186406367 17592186406335] [17592186405042 1006 "Vector Calculus I" 17592186405038] [17592186405042 1007 "Vector Calculus I extends differentiation and integration to vector-valued functions and functions of several variables. Building on single-variable calculus, the course develops mathematical tools for describing curves, surfaces, motion, and quantities distributed across regions in two or three dimensions. Geometric interpretation and physical applications connect vector operations, multivariable derivatives, and multiple integrals." 17592186406335] [17592186405042 1008 "Vectors and coordinate systems provide a language for describing geometry in the plane and in space. Vector-valued functions represent curves and motion, while derivatives and integrals of these functions describe velocity, acceleration, arc length, and curvature. Equations of surfaces and their level sets extend graphical reasoning to functions of several variables.\n\nMultivariable differentiation includes limits, continuity, partial derivatives, the chain rule, directional derivatives, and gradients. These concepts support tangent-plane approximations and optimization, including constrained problems using Lagrange multipliers. Multiple integration addresses accumulated quantities over planar and spatial regions. Rectangular, polar, cylindrical, and spherical coordinates—and more general changes of variables—allow students to match an integral’s representation to the geometry of its region." 17592186406335] [17592186405042 1009 "Oregon State University" 17592186405038] [17592186405042 1011 "MTH 254" 17592186405038] [17592186405042 1012 #uuid "e9dc19ff-a53b-51f7-bd17-d1b14351322a" 17592186405038] [17592186405043 1004 17592186406345 17592186406335] [17592186405043 1004 17592186406346 17592186406335] [17592186405043 1004 17592186406347 17592186406335] [17592186405043 1004 17592186406348 17592186406335] [17592186405043 1004 17592186406349 17592186406335] [17592186405043 1004 17592186406350 17592186406335] [17592186405043 1004 17592186406351 17592186406335] [17592186405043 1004 17592186406352 17592186406335] [17592186405043 1004 17592186406353 17592186406335] [17592186405043 1004 17592186406354 17592186406335] [17592186405043 1006 "Vector Calculus II" 17592186405038] [17592186405043 1007 "Vector Calculus II develops the calculus of vector fields and integration along curves and over surfaces. Building on Vector Calculus I, the course connects local descriptions of a field with accumulated quantities such as work, circulation, and flux. Its central tools include conservative fields, divergence, curl, and the integral theorems of Gauss and Stokes, with applications involving force, work, mass, and charge." 17592186406335] [17592186405043 1008 "The course revisits vector functions, space curves, gradients, and directional derivatives before extending integration to curves and surfaces. Line integrals connect vector fields with work and circulation. Conservative fields, potential functions, and path independence explain when an integral can be determined from endpoint information rather than the details of a path.\n\nSurface geometry and oriented surface elements provide the foundation for surface integrals and flux. Divergence and curl describe local field behavior, while the Divergence Theorem and Stokes’ Theorem connect these local quantities to integrals over regions and their boundaries. The archived course materials emphasize geometric understanding and the use of vector differentials to connect the various integration methods." 17592186406335] [17592186405043 1009 "Oregon State University" 17592186405038] [17592186405043 1011 "MTH 255" 17592186405038] [17592186405043 1012 #uuid "c37c596e-77c4-5554-8295-443fb5da076b" 17592186405038] [17592186405044 1004 17592186406368 17592186406335] [17592186405044 1004 17592186406369 17592186406335] [17592186405044 1004 17592186406370 17592186406335] [17592186405044 1004 17592186406371 17592186406335] [17592186405044 1004 17592186406372 17592186406335] [17592186405044 1004 17592186406373 17592186406335] [17592186405044 1004 17592186406374 17592186406335] [17592186405044 1004 17592186406375 17592186406335] [17592186405044 1004 17592186406376 17592186406335] [17592186405044 1006 "Applied Differential Equations" 17592186405038] [17592186405044 1007 "Applied Differential Equations introduces methods for describing and solving problems in which an unknown function is related to its derivatives. The course focuses on ordinary differential equations and applications appropriate to science and engineering, particularly electrical circuits and mechanical oscillators. Coverage includes first-order linear and selected nonlinear equations, second-order linear equations, and introductory work with higher-order equations and Laplace transforms." 17592186406335] [17592186405044 1008 "The archived syllabus begins with mathematical modeling and the classification of differential equations, then develops methods for first-order and second-order equations. This organization connects the structure of an equation with an appropriate solution approach. Applications give meaning to the unknown functions and their derivatives rather than treating equations as purely formal expressions.\n\nLaplace transforms provide an additional approach to solving differential equations, while introductory higher-order work extends the range of equations considered. Electrical circuits and mechanical oscillators serve as central application settings. The published description also identifies familiarity with complex numbers and Euler’s identities as relevant background." 17592186406335] [17592186405044 1009 "Oregon State University" 17592186405038] [17592186405044 1011 "MTH 256" 17592186405038] [17592186405044 1012 #uuid "fa274c9b-ca65-5fde-b8ed-49681698e235" 17592186405038] [17592186405045 1004 17592186406377 17592186406335] [17592186405045 1004 17592186406378 17592186406335] [17592186405045 1004 17592186406379 17592186406335] [17592186405045 1004 17592186406380 17592186406335] [17592186405045 1004 17592186406381 17592186406335] [17592186405045 1004 17592186406382 17592186406335] [17592186405045 1004 17592186406383 17592186406335] [17592186405045 1004 17592186406384 17592186406335] [17592186405045 1004 17592186406385 17592186406335] [17592186405045 1004 17592186406386 17592186406335] [17592186405045 1004 17592186406387 17592186406335] [17592186405045 1004 17592186406388 17592186406335] [17592186405045 1006 "Linear Algebra I" 17592186405038] [17592186405045 1007 "Linear Algebra I introduces the computational and conceptual study of linear systems, matrices, and subspaces. The course connects methods for solving equations with the structure of linear transformations and vector spaces. Determinants, linear independence, bases, dimension, and introductory eigenvalue analysis provide complementary ways to understand how a linear system or transformation behaves." 17592186406335] [17592186405045 1008 "Work with linear systems and Gaussian elimination establishes a foundation for matrix operations, invertibility, and determinants. The course then connects these calculations with linear independence, spanning sets, subspaces, bases, and dimension. Row, column, and null spaces describe important features of a matrix, while rank–nullity relates those spaces to the structure of a system’s solutions.\n\nMatrix representations make linear transformations concrete through examples such as rotation, reflection, projection, and scaling. Eigenvalues and eigenvectors introduce directions preserved by a transformation, including examples with complex eigenvalues. The archived section used here also introduces diagonalization and illustrates eigenvalue methods through simple systems of differential equations." 17592186406335] [17592186405045 1009 "Oregon State University" 17592186405038] [17592186405045 1011 "MTH 341" 17592186405038] [17592186405045 1012 #uuid "3ace2150-672f-50e7-9c2c-027b6d4272d7" 17592186405038] [17592186405046 1085 17592186405234 17592186405038] [17592186405046 1085 17592186405602 17592186405038] [17592186405046 1086 "Source question: 6.\n\nLet $0\\le a\\le b$ and let $f(x)\\ge g(x)$ on $[a,b]$.\n\nLet $R$ be the region on the $xz$-plane between $z=f(x)$ and $z=g(x)$ over $[a,b]$, and let $D$ be the solid region obtained by rotating $R$ around the $z$-axis." 17592186405038] [17592186405046 1087 #uuid "0002af53-81f1-5367-a1e1-4ac77ed1db15" 17592186405038] [17592186405046 1088 17592186405234 17592186405038] [17592186405047 1062 #uuid "003748fd-17d4-5b51-bba2-5c11a7d52337" 17592186405038] [17592186405047 1063 17592186405560 17592186405038] [17592186405048 1089 17592186050295 17592186405015] [17592186405048 1089 17592186050866 17592186405015] [17592186405048 1090 17592186405278 17592186405038] [17592186405048 1091 #uuid "003b5958-a3f3-5db4-af36-f5866d42b791" 17592186405038] [17592186405049 1068 17592186406011 17592186405015] [17592186405049 1071 "Source question: 2.\n\n**Question 2**\n\nFind the tangent line for $x(t)=11t$ and $y(t)=t^2-2$ at $t=6$. Give an exact answer.\n\n$y(x)=$ {{field-1}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405049 1072 #uuid "0078d6c2-5079-5550-adfa-d8b2a018000b" 17592186405038] [17592186405049 1074 "The slope is $(2t)/11$, so at $t=6$ it is $12/11$. The point is $(66,34)$, giving $y-34=(12/11)(x-66)$ and $y=12x/11-38$." 17592186405015] [17592186405050 1058 17592186405643 17592186405038] [17592186405050 1062 #uuid "00864a11-4675-58f9-8705-75378908ae67" 17592186405038] [17592186405050 1063 17592186405613 17592186405038] [17592186405051 1058 17592186405629 17592186405038] [17592186405051 1062 #uuid "0176612c-73fb-5a3f-9810-da7e3939f99b" 17592186405038] [17592186405051 1063 17592186405754 17592186405038] [17592186405052 1058 17592186405576 17592186405038] [17592186405052 1062 #uuid "0179cc7b-d73f-5e16-9187-9ee48b3b4b47" 17592186405038] [17592186405052 1063 17592186405368 17592186405038] [17592186405053 1068 17592186406098 17592186405015] [17592186405053 1071 "**Question 11 — Part A**\n\nHow are the field vectors directed?" 17592186405015] [17592186405053 1072 #uuid "01d6d0ba-a3db-5470-bc3f-51935b58fc3a" 17592186405038] [17592186405053 1074 "The field vector is perpendicular to the radius: $\\langle x,y\\rangle\\cdot\\langle-y,x\\rangle=0$. Therefore it is tangent to the circle through that point, except that the origin has the zero vector." 17592186405015] [17592186405054 1071 "**Question 6 — Part A**\n\nGive $D$ as a set of triples in cylindrical coordinates:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(r,\\theta,z)\\mid {?}\\le r\\le {?},\\;{?}\\le\\theta\\le {?},\\;{?}\\le z\\le {?}\\}.\n$$" 17592186405038] [17592186405054 1072 #uuid "02a8dd04-6537-5a0c-b832-3fbc8660a80b" 17592186405038] [17592186405054 1074 "$D=\\{(r,\\theta,z):a\\le r\\le b,\\ 0\\le\\theta\\le2\\pi,\\ g(r)\\le z\\le f(r)\\}$.\n\nRotation about the $z$-axis replaces the nonnegative horizontal coordinate $x$ by radial distance $r$. One full turn gives the angular range; the lower and upper surfaces retain the original functions evaluated at $r$." 17592186405015] [17592186405055 1058 17592186405819 17592186405038] [17592186405055 1062 #uuid "02b8835c-909b-5893-bee0-821a13f8a984" 17592186405038] [17592186405055 1063 17592186405553 17592186405038] [17592186405056 1089 17592186048832 17592186405015] [17592186405056 1089 17592186049150 17592186405015] [17592186405056 1089 17592186049168 17592186405015] [17592186405056 1090 17592186405606 17592186405038] [17592186405056 1091 #uuid "02c291be-ef43-5c72-904f-9019e50e9828" 17592186405038] [17592186405057 1089 17592186049889 17592186405015] [17592186405057 1090 17592186405196 17592186405038] [17592186405057 1091 #uuid "02e2242b-9d8a-5e4e-b6f4-a5d576b72263" 17592186405038] [17592186405058 1058 17592186405500 17592186405038] [17592186405058 1062 #uuid "031e9ed5-107a-523a-8348-3dc2a0d1dac2" 17592186405038] [17592186405058 1063 17592186405169 17592186405038] [17592186405059 1058 17592186405436 17592186405038] [17592186405059 1062 #uuid "034c1800-2acf-59d3-92e4-1a2cb6e56af2" 17592186405038] [17592186405059 1063 17592186405518 17592186405038] [17592186405060 1068 17592186406182 17592186405015] [17592186405060 1071 "Source question: 12.\n\nFind the lateral area above the xy-plane and below $z=6xy$, formed by vertical segments from $C: y=\\sqrt{49-x^2},\\ 0\\le x\\le7$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405060 1072 #uuid "0384d8f4-779d-5541-bc94-33b20624855c" 17592186405038] [17592186405060 1074 "Use x=7cosθ, y=7sinθ, 0≤θ≤π/2; scalar arc length is orientation independent. Then $A=\\int_0^{\\pi/2}294\\cos\\theta\\sin\\theta\\,(7)d\\theta=2058/2=1029$." 17592186405015] [17592186405061 1089 17592186051091 17592186405015] [17592186405061 1090 17592186405559 17592186405038] [17592186405061 1091 #uuid "0387613d-e80d-5c1b-aafe-4ca1ac5c32a2" 17592186405038] [17592186405062 1089 17592186049366 17592186405015] [17592186405062 1089 17592186050033 17592186405015] [17592186405062 1090 17592186405112 17592186405038] [17592186405062 1091 #uuid "043ad055-4d51-5569-b353-b0198d68c711" 17592186405038] [17592186405063 1089 17592186051091 17592186405015] [17592186405063 1090 17592186405567 17592186405038] [17592186405063 1091 #uuid "046fce81-a76e-5427-b9f4-224f078b1f65" 17592186405038] [17592186405064 1058 17592186405386 17592186405038] [17592186405064 1062 #uuid "048abeca-9232-55b4-866a-c7c4eb482243" 17592186405038] [17592186405064 1063 17592186405820 17592186405038] [17592186405065 1058 17592186405789 17592186405038] [17592186405065 1062 #uuid "04bc37bf-66d0-56e4-b88b-abbdc72135db" 17592186405038] [17592186405065 1063 17592186405701 17592186405038] [17592186405066 1089 17592186048684 17592186405015] [17592186405066 1089 17592186050246 17592186405015] [17592186405066 1090 17592186405233 17592186405038] [17592186405066 1091 #uuid "04e594fb-c67a-5d9d-a4ba-54d4721adffb" 17592186405038] [17592186405067 1089 17592186048575 17592186405015] [17592186405067 1089 17592186049487 17592186405015] [17592186405067 1089 17592186050614 17592186405015] [17592186405067 1090 17592186405541 17592186405038] [17592186405067 1091 #uuid "05666c85-c7b9-5149-8738-3a739c3ab71a" 17592186405038] [17592186405068 1085 17592186405212 17592186405038] [17592186405068 1085 17592186405689 17592186405038] [17592186405068 1086 "Source question: 3." 17592186405038] [17592186405068 1087 #uuid "056ef53c-025c-521f-8d9f-19aaa38a2c86" 17592186405038] [17592186405068 1088 17592186405689 17592186405038] [17592186405069 1068 17592186406295 17592186405015] [17592186405069 1071 "Source question: 3.\n\n**Question 3**\n\nFind the tangent line for $x(t)=4/t^2$ and $y(t)=7/t$ at $t=1$. Give an exact answer.\n\n$y(x)=$ {{field-1}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405069 1072 #uuid "05a4b2b6-5604-5780-be4f-67bb5dc2ad84" 17592186405038] [17592186405069 1074 "Differentiate with respect to $t$: $x'=-8/t^3$ and $y'=-7/t^2$. At $t=1$, the slope is $7/8$ and the point is $(4,7)$, so $y-7=(7/8)(x-4)$." 17592186405015] [17592186405070 1068 17592186406115 17592186405015] [17592186405070 1071 "Source question: 8.\n\nUse Stokes’ theorem to find $\\oint_C[3y^2dx+3z^2dy+3x^2dz]$, where C is the triangle with vertices (6,0,0), (0,6,0), (0,0,6), oriented counterclockwise. Interpret counterclockwise as viewed from above (positive z).\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405070 1072 #uuid "060f02f4-487e-5f5b-8a1c-5f000f57d3c8" 17592186405038] [17592186405070 1074 "The spanning plane is z=6−x−y, with upward vector area (1,1,1)dxdy. Curl F=(−6z,−6x,−6y); its dot product with (1,1,1) is −6(x+y+z)=−36. The xy-projection has area 6·6/2=18, so the integral is −648. The reverse traversal gives +648." 17592186405015] [17592186405071 1089 17592186048819 17592186405015] [17592186405071 1089 17592186049487 17592186405015] [17592186405071 1089 17592186050277 17592186405015] [17592186405071 1089 17592186051090 17592186405015] [17592186405071 1090 17592186405759 17592186405038] [17592186405071 1091 #uuid "061705a9-6aa8-5597-8648-b1259a21f17d" 17592186405038] [17592186405072 1068 17592186406279 17592186405015] [17592186405072 1071 "Source question: 2.\n\nFind $\\int_C(22y-x^4)\\,ds$ for $x=t,\\ y=6t,\\ 0\\le t\\le1$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405072 1072 #uuid "0634e164-3396-5330-9fa0-45d465864c53" 17592186405038] [17592186405072 1074 "The speed is $\\sqrt{1+36}=\\sqrt{37}$. Thus $I=\\sqrt{37}\\int_0^1(132t-t^4)dt=\\sqrt{37}(66-1/5)$." 17592186405015] [17592186405073 1068 17592186405875 17592186405015] [17592186405073 1071 "**Question 5 — Part B**\n\nPoints with vertical tangents: {{field-1}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405073 1072 #uuid "06d8e05c-5efd-57f9-bbd4-3dbfed913dff" 17592186405038] [17592186405073 1074 "Vertical tangents occur when $\\sin t=0$ and $\\cos t\\ne0$: $t=0,\\pi,2\\pi$. These give $(0,3)$ and $(20,3)$; the first and last parameter values describe the same point." 17592186405015] [17592186405074 1062 #uuid "06f29e00-2851-58a3-9d9f-cf75ad9d36ef" 17592186405038] [17592186405074 1063 17592186405532 17592186405038] [17592186405075 1068 17592186406108 17592186405015] [17592186405075 1071 "Source question: 3.\n\nFind $\\partial(x,y)/\\partial(u,v)$ for $x=2u/(5v)$, $y=8u-4v$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405075 1072 #uuid "07e25c24-6d0b-5332-b954-74e512dabc9b" 17592186405038] [17592186405075 1074 "For v≠0, the derivative matrix is $\\begin{pmatrix}2/(5v)&-2u/(5v^2)\\\\8&-4\\end{pmatrix}$. Its determinant is $-8/(5v)+16u/(5v^2)$." 17592186405015] [17592186405076 1089 17592186048923 17592186405015] [17592186405076 1089 17592186050142 17592186405015] [17592186405076 1090 17592186405266 17592186405038] [17592186405076 1091 #uuid "07e95435-5f31-518b-9665-76645c61ebd7" 17592186405038] [17592186405077 1089 17592186048960 17592186405015] [17592186405077 1089 17592186049859 17592186405015] [17592186405077 1090 17592186405279 17592186405038] [17592186405077 1091 #uuid "0951d458-8b0c-5450-9c7b-6a77b994cfa4" 17592186405038] [17592186405078 1071 "Textbook problem **1.2.8.a**.\n\n**Question 1**\n\nLet $a$ be a nonzero real number and let $c$ be a real number. Verify that\n\n$$\ny=(x-c)^a\n$$\n\nis a solution of\n\n$$\ny'=ay^{(a-1)/a}\n$$\n\non $(c,\\infty)$." 17592186405038] [17592186405078 1072 #uuid "095f6d16-fa03-5426-8ae5-ecc6d14dd1cf" 17592186405038] [17592186405078 1074 "For $x>c$, the base $x-c$ is positive, so all displayed real powers are defined and the exponent laws apply. Differentiating gives $y\\prime=a(x-c)^{a-1}$. Meanwhile, $ay^{(a-1)/a}=a[(x-c)^a]^{(a-1)/a}=a(x-c)^{a-1}$. The two sides agree for every $x\\in(c,\\infty)$, verifying the solution.\n\nUse the chain rule, including the derivative $1$ of $x-c$. The condition $a\ne0$ makes the exponent $(a-1)/a$ meaningful; $x>c$ is essential when $a$ is an arbitrary real number." 17592186405015] [17592186405079 1089 17592186048960 17592186405015] [17592186405079 1089 17592186049859 17592186405015] [17592186405079 1090 17592186405723 17592186405038] [17592186405079 1091 #uuid "098b4466-078b-535c-bcb0-1638dc514999" 17592186405038] [17592186405080 1068 17592186405840 17592186405015] [17592186405080 1071 "Source question: 1.\n\n**Question 1**\n\nFind the tangent line for $x(t)=4t^2$ and $y(t)=t$ at $t=9$. Give an exact answer.\n\n$y(x)=$ {{field-1}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405080 1072 #uuid "09ab09e1-ceb7-5179-b55b-307a079ef53b" 17592186405038] [17592186405080 1074 "Use $dy/dx=y'(t)/x'(t)$. At $t=9$, the point is $(324,9)$ and the slope is $1/72$. Thus $y-9=(x-324)/72$, or $y=x/72+9/2$." 17592186405015] [17592186405081 1062 #uuid "09c826e3-37e5-588c-8806-3d147992af00" 17592186405038] [17592186405081 1063 17592186405304 17592186405038] [17592186405082 1062 #uuid "0a0161a4-8775-5c8c-8d6d-20a81ee04a00" 17592186405038] [17592186405082 1063 17592186405668 17592186405038] [17592186405083 1071 "**Question 2 — Part B**\n\nFind $d\\mathbf r=\\langle dx,dy,dz\\rangle$ (in terms of $dt$)." 17592186405038] [17592186405083 1072 #uuid "0a9b777c-4f52-5acd-ad72-f0011ed22156" 17592186405038] [17592186405083 1074 "$d\\mathbf r=\\langle3,4,5\\tan t\\rangle\\,dt$, so $dx=3\\,dt$, $dy=4\\,dt$, and $dz=5\\tan t\\,dt$.\n\nThe vector differential is $\\mathbf r\\,\\prime(t)\\,dt$. The same scalar increment $dt$ multiplies every component; it is not a fourth component." 17592186405015] [17592186405084 1071 "**Question 5 — Part C**\n\nDraw the sphere $x^2+y^2+z^2=1$. Fix a value of $y$ and draw a couple of tangent lines to the resulting circle. Compare their slopes with Part B." 17592186405038] [17592186405084 1072 #uuid "0b35d9ec-b384-595e-85ab-21d85a676a98" 17592186405038] [17592186405084 1074 "One valid choice is the slice $y=0$, giving $x^2+z^2=1$ in the $xz$-plane. At $(x,y,z)=(0,0,1)$, draw the tangent $z=1$, with slope $dz/dx=0$. At $(1/\\sqrt2,0,1/\\sqrt2)$, draw $z=-x+\\sqrt2$ within the same plane, with slope $-1$. Both agree with $z_x=-x/z$. Show the slice on the sphere; other nondegenerate slices and correctly checked tangent points are valid.\n\n![Authored reference graph](images/47/47d98e418cee25adf036004c66cffb1de67b2898235229ebd28921b92ea5f2cb.png)\n\nFor a slice $y=y_0$, the circle is $x^2+z^2=1-y_0^2$, so choose $|y_0|<1$. Measure slope in the $xz$-plane while keeping $y$ fixed. At $z=0$, the slice tangent is vertical and $-x/z$ is undefined." 17592186405015] [17592186405085 1089 17592186049386 17592186405015] [17592186405085 1089 17592186049487 17592186405015] [17592186405085 1090 17592186405522 17592186405038] [17592186405085 1091 #uuid "0bce51e8-64a9-50f7-9b97-b4fda3322256" 17592186405038] [17592186405086 1089 17592186048960 17592186405015] [17592186405086 1089 17592186050149 17592186405015] [17592186405086 1089 17592186050561 17592186405015] [17592186405086 1090 17592186405636 17592186405038] [17592186405086 1091 #uuid "0bd0446d-754d-57ba-8f1a-313d3045ef6f" 17592186405038] [17592186405087 1068 17592186406254 17592186405015] [17592186405087 1071 "**Question 4 — Part B**\n\nCompute the dot product, then explain why $\\nabla\\varphi$ is perpendicular to the level curve at $(x,y)$.\n\n$\\nabla\\varphi\\cdot\\langle\\varphi_y,-\\varphi_x\\rangle=$ {{field-1}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405087 1072 #uuid "0d72a085-32f0-50ef-84b8-517e4dcb493e" 17592186405038] [17592186405087 1074 "The gradient is $\\langle\\varphi_x,\\varphi_y\\rangle$, so the dot product is $\\varphi_x\\varphi_y-\\varphi_y\\varphi_x=0$. At a regular point, the nonzero vector from Part A spans the tangent line; therefore the nonzero gradient is normal to the curve. Include this geometric conclusion with the calculation." 17592186405015] [17592186405088 1089 17592186050537 17592186405015] [17592186405088 1089 17592186050866 17592186405015] [17592186405088 1090 17592186405348 17592186405038] [17592186405088 1091 #uuid "0d80bcf3-3ed4-57fb-b172-6b028992696e" 17592186405038] [17592186405089 1068 17592186406260 17592186405015] [17592186405089 1071 "Source question: 8.\n\nLet $\\mathbf F=\\langle x,y,z\\rangle$ and let S bound a solid E satisfying the divergence theorem, with outward normal n. Use $V=\\frac13\\iint_S\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,dS$ to find the volume of a rectangular parallelepiped with side lengths a,b,c.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405089 1072 #uuid "0e2e8dc9-31df-5d18-abb8-1bd279f745b0" 17592186405038] [17592186405089 1074 "Place the box in 0≤x≤a, 0≤y≤b, 0≤z≤c. Flux on the three coordinate-plane faces is 0. On x=a the flux is a·bc=abc; the faces y=b and z=c each contribute abc as well. Total flux is 3abc, so dividing by 3 gives V=abc." 17592186405015] [17592186405090 1071 "**Question 4 — Part B**\n\nAn integral curve that is not a solution." 17592186405038] [17592186405090 1072 #uuid "0e6f5ea5-7fa9-549e-b8f3-ca2305a6870d" 17592186405038] [17592186405090 1074 "Trace a complete small closed trajectory around the center near $(-2,0)$, following the field continuously through the upper arc, right turn, lower arc, and left turn. Label the whole loop “integral curve, not a solution graph.” The loop has two heights for many $x$ values and vertical turns, so the entire loop cannot be represented by a differentiable single-valued $y(x)$.\n\nThe worksheet uses “integral curve” geometrically for a traced trajectory. Match the field’s tangent directions rather than drawing an arbitrary circle; its exact shape need not be circular. The distinction from part (a) is the whole closed trajectory versus a graph segment." 17592186405015] [17592186405091 1062 #uuid "0ea0cc5b-7e67-52d9-97db-0c5da34946fa" 17592186405038] [17592186405091 1063 17592186405752 17592186405038] [17592186405092 1068 17592186406121 17592186405015] [17592186405092 1071 "Source question: 24.\n\nFind the work of $\\mathbf F=e^x\\mathbf i+10y\\mathbf j$ along the oriented curve C shown below.\n\n![Oriented path C](images/3b/3bc389fa33dfa4ae3a4ddbcb9554ef4bc53ba3ce0ddedead9e597759dfacd8e7.png)\n\nThe arrows give (1,0) → (0,1) → (−1,1) → (−1,2).\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405092 1072 #uuid "0ea7bc72-e1da-501e-b602-8872debc87ee" 17592186405038] [17592186405092 1074 "A potential is $\\phi=e^x+5y^2$. Follow the image arrows from (1,0) to (−1,2). Therefore $W=\\phi(-1,2)-\\phi(1,0)=e^{-1}+20-e$. The source image’s automatic alt text misstates an endpoint and reverses traversal; the visible labels and arrows determine this task." 17592186405015] [17592186405093 1089 17592186048152 17592186405015] [17592186405093 1089 17592186049536 17592186405015] [17592186405093 1090 17592186405770 17592186405038] [17592186405093 1091 #uuid "0f1b9599-fc7c-5c7f-9447-5eea83ffc38e" 17592186405038] [17592186405094 1062 #uuid "0f2a4505-cf4c-565d-86c3-85d0ec61ef35" 17592186405038] [17592186405094 1063 17592186405538 17592186405038] [17592186405095 1085 17592186405159 17592186405038] [17592186405095 1085 17592186405767 17592186405038] [17592186405095 1086 "Source question: 4(a)–(b), PDF page 5." 17592186405038] [17592186405095 1087 #uuid "0fde5e2a-8723-5760-981f-9e2e9cb2414c" 17592186405038] [17592186405095 1088 17592186405159 17592186405038] [17592186405096 1071 "**Question 2 — Part C**\n\nFind the differential arc length $ds=|d\\mathbf r|$ (in terms of $dt$)." 17592186405038] [17592186405096 1072 #uuid "101dfad0-8dd2-541a-be72-28b8ba7ed9a6" 17592186405038] [17592186405096 1074 "$ds=5|\\sec t|\\,|dt|$. On the interval $0\\le t\\le\\pi/3$, with increasing $t$, this is $ds=5\\sec t\\,dt$.\n\n$|d\\mathbf r|=\\sqrt{3^2+4^2+25\\tan^2t}\\,|dt|=5\\sqrt{1+\\tan^2t}\\,|dt|=5|\\sec t|\\,|dt|$. The absolute values express the nonnegative length element." 17592186405015] [17592186405097 1043 17592186405268 17592186405038] [17592186405097 1043 17592186405289 17592186405038] [17592186405097 1043 17592186405343 17592186405038] [17592186405097 1043 17592186405457 17592186405038] [17592186405097 1043 17592186405491 17592186405038] [17592186405097 1044 17592186045461 17592186405038] [17592186405097 1045 "MTH 256 — WHW-1" 17592186405038] [17592186405097 1046 17592186405457 17592186405038] [17592186405097 1047 "2026-10-03T06:59:59.000Z" 17592186405038] [17592186405097 1048 17592186405044 17592186405038] [17592186405097 1053 #uuid "105799b2-3037-57ca-951e-b5a3f18df304" 17592186405038] [17592186405098 1058 17592186405638 17592186405038] [17592186405098 1062 #uuid "11c38a6c-b140-543a-be17-08497270d47d" 17592186405038] [17592186405098 1063 17592186405514 17592186405038] [17592186405099 1058 17592186405328 17592186405038] [17592186405099 1062 #uuid "11c80a48-2afe-5975-b84c-517f776a592d" 17592186405038] [17592186405099 1063 17592186405346 17592186405038] [17592186405100 1068 17592186406089 17592186405015] [17592186405100 1071 "**Question 9 — Part A**\n\nFind its exact arc length: {{field-1}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405100 1072 #uuid "127579f2-ed87-5852-a2d8-16b3b9558e0d" 17592186405038] [17592186405100 1074 "The derivatives are $x'=\\cos t-t\\sin t$ and $y'=\\sin t+t\\cos t$, so the squared speed is $1+t^2$. Thus $s=\\int_0^{5\\pi}\\sqrt{1+t^2}\\,dt=\\frac12[5\\pi\\sqrt{1+25\\pi^2}+\\ln(5\\pi+\\sqrt{1+25\\pi^2})]$." 17592186405015] [17592186405101 1068 17592186406097 17592186405015] [17592186405101 1071 "Source question: 12.\n\nUse **Green’s theorem** to find the area bounded by $y=x^2$ and $y=x+42$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405101 1072 #uuid "127a5831-49e6-57dd-9d16-925eb9ca8b6a" 17592186405038] [17592186405101 1074 "The intersections solve x²−x−42=(x−7)(x+6)=0. Use $A=-\\oint_Cy\\,dx$ with positive orientation: lower parabola from −6 to 7, upper line from 7 to −6. Then $A=\\int_{-6}^7(x+42-x^2)dx=2197/6$." 17592186405015] [17592186405102 1068 17592186406140 17592186405015] [17592186405102 1071 "Source question: 11.\n\nFind $\\iint_R16x\\cos(15xy)\\,dA$ over the region bounded by $xy=1$, $xy=5$, $x=1$, $x=16$. Use $u=x$, $v=xy$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405102 1072 #uuid "12cfc085-2336-51ef-9c85-ce58272ebf2b" 17592186405038] [17592186405102 1074 "Invert: x=u,y=v/u. Since u>0, $|J|=1/u$. The rectangle is 1≤u≤16, 1≤v≤5. Thus $I=\\int_1^{16}\\int_1^5 16\\cos(15v)dvdu=16(\\sin75-\\sin15)$." 17592186405015] [17592186405103 1058 17592186405118 17592186405038] [17592186405103 1062 #uuid "138b73a3-42fd-548a-8024-f39dea19e877" 17592186405038] [17592186405103 1063 17592186405191 17592186405038] [17592186405104 1068 17592186405967 17592186405015] [17592186405104 1071 "Source question: 8.\n\n**Question 8**\n\nFind the arc length for $x(t)=13\\sin t$, $y(t)=13\\cos t$, $-\\pi/2\\le t\\le\\pi/2$. Give an exact answer.\n\n$s=$ {{field-1}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405104 1072 #uuid "138cb653-9644-50eb-9825-e0b6e9abad8b" 17592186405038] [17592186405104 1074 "The speed is $\\sqrt{169\\cos^2t+169\\sin^2t}=13$. The parameter interval has length $\\pi$, so $s=13\\pi$, the length of a semicircle of radius $13$." 17592186405015] [17592186405105 1062 #uuid "13d64c2a-3e82-58e2-8312-f805798f9188" 17592186405038] [17592186405105 1063 17592186405573 17592186405038] [17592186405106 1058 17592186405758 17592186405038] [17592186405106 1062 #uuid "143e4469-7624-54fc-934a-076267d6f897" 17592186405038] [17592186405106 1063 17592186405601 17592186405038] [17592186405107 1089 17592186050349 17592186405015] [17592186405107 1090 17592186405046 17592186405038] [17592186405107 1091 #uuid "147eb189-7a83-5424-bc98-c405a82a3bb8" 17592186405038] [17592186405108 1085 17592186405349 17592186405038] [17592186405108 1085 17592186405764 17592186405038] [17592186405108 1085 17592186405800 17592186405038] [17592186405108 1086 "Source question: 2.\n\n**Volume of a parallelepiped**\n\nConsider the slanted box formed by $\\mathbf u$, $\\mathbf v$, and $\\mathbf w$. Let $\\theta$ be the angle between $\\mathbf u\\times\\mathbf v$ and $\\mathbf w$.\n\n![Parallelepiped with base vectors u and v, side w, and normal u cross v](images/f6/f647f7a54f2626f5257f1ed859525799f16e7f825c34e50d200bc4f21151dd3f.png)" 17592186405038] [17592186405108 1087 #uuid "14fd2662-45c2-5d65-b77a-013277de5979" 17592186405038] [17592186405108 1088 17592186405349 17592186405038] [17592186405109 1089 17592186049889 17592186405015] [17592186405109 1090 17592186405198 17592186405038] [17592186405109 1091 #uuid "1676655a-2558-509c-a1ba-303a93c823c9" 17592186405038] [17592186405110 1062 #uuid "17eafedd-08c9-50d1-b9bd-6e33688b0e53" 17592186405038] [17592186405110 1063 17592186405253 17592186405038] [17592186405111 1089 17592186048314 17592186405015] [17592186405111 1089 17592186048960 17592186405015] [17592186405111 1090 17592186405092 17592186405038] [17592186405111 1091 #uuid "1966d70b-3ece-5213-8725-be4fd3e43f4c" 17592186405038] [17592186405112 1068 17592186405904 17592186405015] [17592186405112 1068 17592186405905 17592186405015] [17592186405112 1068 17592186406048 17592186405015] [17592186405112 1071 "Source question: 5.\n\nFind the upward-pointing unit normal $\\mathbf n=n_1\\mathbf i+n_2\\mathbf j+n_3\\mathbf k$ to $z=\\sqrt{49-x^2-y^2}$, $0\\le x^2+y^2\\le49$.\n\n$n_1=$ {{n1}}.\n\n$n_2=$ {{n2}}.\n\n$n_3=$ {{n3}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405112 1072 #uuid "19b61e5c-ef5c-5fb6-bdf2-89030e4ab719" 17592186405038] [17592186405112 1074 "This is the upper radius-7 hemisphere. The radial normal (x,y,z) has length 7, so divide by 7. Its z component is positive in the interior. At the equator it extends continuously as a horizontal unit normal; a strictly upward component there is impossible, and the graph derivatives are singular." 17592186405015] [17592186405113 1062 #uuid "19d9b88e-5569-5b33-8f52-591f091f55da" 17592186405038] [17592186405113 1063 17592186405267 17592186405038] [17592186405114 1062 #uuid "1a1c8b15-ea76-5e15-a1f7-57c1a1631808" 17592186405038] [17592186405114 1063 17592186405736 17592186405038] [17592186405115 1058 17592186405380 17592186405038] [17592186405115 1062 #uuid "1aedeb19-3ac8-5a39-bce8-ce3870fed5da" 17592186405038] [17592186405115 1063 17592186405747 17592186405038] [17592186405116 1058 17592186405119 17592186405038] [17592186405116 1062 #uuid "1b747e56-4e37-56b9-97f4-c94eb6d4c0a0" 17592186405038] [17592186405116 1063 17592186405585 17592186405038] [17592186405117 1058 17592186405081 17592186405038] [17592186405117 1062 #uuid "1b9fe61a-c3fc-56cb-b38c-17072c2c8b11" 17592186405038] [17592186405117 1063 17592186405227 17592186405038] [17592186405118 1058 17592186405365 17592186405038] [17592186405118 1062 #uuid "1bade7f5-3b9e-511d-bb2f-7d79d06a54fe" 17592186405038] [17592186405118 1063 17592186405210 17592186405038] [17592186405119 1058 17592186405103 17592186405038] [17592186405119 1062 #uuid "1bb1f8c9-d608-5dbd-8d45-4fd581d30328" 17592186405038] [17592186405119 1063 17592186405725 17592186405038] [17592186405120 1089 17592186048923 17592186405015] [17592186405120 1089 17592186050418 17592186405015] [17592186405120 1090 17592186405639 17592186405038] [17592186405120 1091 #uuid "1bd17899-d2e1-5157-bd52-fc53ba7450b1" 17592186405038] [17592186405121 1068 17592186405872 17592186405015] [17592186405121 1071 "Source question: 12.\n\nLet $\\mathbf F=\\frac1\\rho\\langle5x,5y,5z\\rangle$, where $\\rho=\\sqrt{x^2+y^2+z^2}$. Calculate $\\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf F$ in terms of ρ.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405121 1072 #uuid "1ca271d3-4582-50a5-83b5-c55a68699ac7" 17592186405038] [17592186405121 1074 "For ρ>0, ∂(5x/ρ)/∂x=5/ρ−5x²/ρ³, and similarly for y and z. Add to get 15/ρ−5(x²+y²+z²)/ρ³=10/ρ. The field and its divergence are undefined at the origin." 17592186405015] [17592186405122 1062 #uuid "1ce36d5a-5df1-5927-8b62-cf6c75d78c09" 17592186405038] [17592186405122 1063 17592186405543 17592186405038] [17592186405123 1068 17592186406079 17592186405015] [17592186405123 1068 17592186406195 17592186405015] [17592186405123 1071 "**(b)** $\\phi=\\tan^{-1}(y/x)$. Find its gradient field and the points excluded from its domain.\n\nGradient: {{gradient}}.\n\nExcluded points: {{excluded}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405123 1072 #uuid "1cea1af1-963f-518b-b83a-160d05ee007e" 17592186405038] [17592186405123 1074 "The chain rule gives $\\phi_x=(-y/x^2)/(1+y^2/x^2)$ and $\\phi_y=(1/x)/(1+y^2/x^2)$. Simplification gives the displayed gradient, but the original potential requires x≠0. Do not enlarge its domain just because the simplified formula exists elsewhere." 17592186405015] [17592186405124 1058 17592186405052 17592186405038] [17592186405124 1062 #uuid "1d0d979d-309a-5028-9975-72bccbd77b6d" 17592186405038] [17592186405124 1063 17592186405780 17592186405038] [17592186405125 1068 17592186406315 17592186405015] [17592186405125 1071 "Source question: 10.\n\nFind the outward electric flux of $\\mathbf E=\\langle x,2y,10z\\rangle$ across the cylindrical side $x^2+y^2=9$, $0\\le z\\le2$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405125 1072 #uuid "1d0ff580-cbfb-5d8f-af8d-e0fd4efc6f0d" 17592186405038] [17592186405125 1074 "Use (3cosθ,3sinθ,z). The outward vector area is (3cosθ,3sinθ,0)dθ dz. Its dot product with E is 9cos²θ+18sin²θ. Integrate θ from 0 to 2π and z from 0 to 2: $2(9\\pi+18\\pi)=54\\pi$. The vertical component contributes nothing on the side." 17592186405015] [17592186405126 1064 "**6.6 Surface Integrals**\n\nDue: 2026-11-19\nSource: [Macmillan Achieve](https://achieve.macmillanlearning.com/ihub/assessment/f188d950-dd73-11e0-9572-0800200c9a66/5a1db2a7-4295-4a4c-9dbb-ceb8b174a282/d49c94ff-8be8-4c47-a468-0e086815d133)\n\nStudy answers and explanations are authored additions, not publisher solutions.\n\n## Instructions\n\nUse exact symbolic notation and fractions as needed. Problems 8–9 omit the numerical constant density; study references use ρ₀ and explain unit density. Outward normals on their open paraboloids are interpreted relative to the naturally capped solids. Source: Sullivan 2e Calculus, W.H. Freeman. Unlimited attempts, no incorrect-attempt penalty; up to 50 days late at a 2% daily penalty." 17592186405015] [17592186405126 1066 #uuid "1d13f037-1b0c-58bb-a6e3-91a137c14d15" 17592186405038] [17592186405126 1067 "Assignment information" 17592186405038] [17592186405127 1062 #uuid "1d95f0eb-29af-5f99-a7a1-aa5baac01532" 17592186405038] [17592186405127 1063 17592186405662 17592186405038] [17592186405128 1089 17592186048225 17592186405015] [17592186405128 1089 17592186049050 17592186405015] [17592186405128 1090 17592186405379 17592186405038] [17592186405128 1091 #uuid "1db67f2b-c9a0-5df8-9043-0bb8521284bd" 17592186405038] [17592186405129 1064 "**6.4 Green’s Theorem**\n\nDue: 2026-10-29\nSource: [Macmillan Achieve](https://achieve.macmillanlearning.com/ihub/assessment/f188d950-dd73-11e0-9572-0800200c9a66/5a1db2a7-4295-4a4c-9dbb-ceb8b174a282/acfcfc0c-cb8c-4110-b65a-55fe3f15159f)\n\nStudy answers and explanations are authored additions, not publisher solutions.\n\n## Instructions\n\nGive exact answers using symbolic notation and fractions. Use Green’s theorem where required. For Problem 11, the reference answer uses the standard positive orientation of a region’s boundary; the platform prompt does not state it explicitly. Source: Sullivan 2e Calculus, W.H. Freeman. Unlimited attempts, no incorrect-attempt penalty; up to 50 days late at a 2% daily penalty." 17592186405015] [17592186405129 1066 #uuid "1e23c156-59e0-56b1-bf9a-9ed09a3e3e67" 17592186405038] [17592186405129 1067 "Assignment information" 17592186405038] [17592186405130 1068 17592186405944 17592186405015] [17592186405130 1071 "**(b)** Find $\\nabla\\eta\\cdot F$ and conclude that at every point on C, F is tangent (parallel) to C.\n\nDot product: {{dot}}. Supply the conclusion and justification below." 17592186405015] [17592186405130 1072 #uuid "1ea08d95-eb9d-5271-8c2b-dd397fb3369d" 17592186405038] [17592186405130 1074 "**conclusion:** $\\nabla\\eta\\cdot F=(2x(-y)+2yx)/\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}=0$. On C, $\\nabla\\eta$ is a nonzero normal. Thus F is perpendicular to this normal and tangent to C.\n\nUse the normal to the level curve, not the position of an arrow tip, to establish tangency. Both F and the normal are nonzero on the circle because a>0." 17592186405015] [17592186405131 1089 17592186048225 17592186405015] [17592186405131 1089 17592186049050 17592186405015] [17592186405131 1090 17592186405419 17592186405038] [17592186405131 1091 #uuid "1f24f0f7-2505-5d0f-97b5-ca73344ef2b1" 17592186405038] [17592186405132 1058 17592186405670 17592186405038] [17592186405132 1062 #uuid "1f97ad93-73d0-589f-a7f3-5e40b21033e3" 17592186405038] [17592186405132 1063 17592186405163 17592186405038] [17592186405133 1064 "**6.5 Divergence and Curl**\n\nDue: 2026-11-05\nSource: [Macmillan Achieve](https://achieve.macmillanlearning.com/ihub/assessment/f188d950-dd73-11e0-9572-0800200c9a66/5a1db2a7-4295-4a4c-9dbb-ceb8b174a282/c2f7ce2a-abae-4f76-b153-1b5653abd0c5)\n\nStudy answers and explanations are authored additions, not publisher solutions.\n\n## Instructions\n\nUse exact symbolic notation and fractions as needed. Source: Sullivan 2e Calculus, W.H. Freeman. Unlimited attempts with no incorrect-attempt penalty; submissions up to 50 days late at a 2% daily penalty." 17592186405015] [17592186405133 1066 #uuid "1fae395d-330b-5b9c-bfd1-4f7f36f65671" 17592186405038] [17592186405133 1067 "Assignment information" 17592186405038] [17592186405134 1068 17592186406003 17592186405015] [17592186405134 1071 "**Question 1 — Part A**\n\nCompute the derivative of $f(x)=e^x\\sin(x)$.\n\n$f\\prime(x)=$ {{field-1}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405134 1072 #uuid "1fbcd441-47d5-54f9-956c-997f296607cb" 17592186405038] [17592186405134 1074 "The product rule gives $(e^x)\\prime\\sin x+e^x(\\sin x)\\prime=e^x\\sin x+e^x\\cos x$. Both factors vary with $x$." 17592186405015] [17592186405135 1089 17592186049114 17592186405015] [17592186405135 1089 17592186049138 17592186405015] [17592186405135 1089 17592186049341 17592186405015] [17592186405135 1089 17592186049659 17592186405015] [17592186405135 1089 17592186050362 17592186405015] [17592186405135 1090 17592186405108 17592186405038] [17592186405135 1091 #uuid "200af8bb-8ee9-5c88-ab54-112581b73bbf" 17592186405038] [17592186405136 1068 17592186406199 17592186405015] [17592186405136 1071 "**Question 3 — Part A**\n\nEnter the rectangular equation in $x$ and $y$: {{field-1}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405136 1072 #uuid "204a2608-fb49-5507-8e9a-efd9c1429e40" 17592186405038] [17592186405136 1074 "From $x=t-6$, obtain $t=x+6$. Then $y=5(x+6)+1=5x+31$." 17592186405015] [17592186405137 1089 17592186050168 17592186405015] [17592186405137 1089 17592186050537 17592186405015] [17592186405137 1090 17592186405069 17592186405038] [17592186405137 1091 #uuid "206a4c48-c01b-5692-9cd2-6fc67860812c" 17592186405038] [17592186405138 1089 17592186050295 17592186405015] [17592186405138 1089 17592186050866 17592186405015] [17592186405138 1090 17592186405549 17592186405038] [17592186405138 1091 #uuid "20a2ba39-f0cd-5d7f-9afa-f6cc4f772544" 17592186405038] [17592186405139 1068 17592186406059 17592186405015] [17592186405139 1068 17592186406184 17592186405015] [17592186405139 1071 "Find both divergences.\n\n$\\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf F=$ {{divf}}.\n\n$\\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf G=$ {{divg}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405139 1072 #uuid "20de299a-3d32-54ca-bfc6-cf1c6e597332" 17592186405038] [17592186405139 1074 "Div F=7+3+20=30 and div G=19+5+6=30. The terms 11x and 5y in the third components vanish when differentiated with respect to z." 17592186405015] [17592186405140 1058 17592186405795 17592186405038] [17592186405140 1062 #uuid "20e9f51f-31e8-503b-8782-0bb2f4296013" 17592186405038] [17592186405140 1063 17592186405816 17592186405038] [17592186405141 1071 "Source exercise: **1.1.2**, [printed page 7 (PDF page 19)](/api/source?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F341%2FKuttler-LinearAlgebra-AFirstCourse-2017A.pdf#page=19).\n\n**Question 2**\n\nGraphically, find the point of intersection of the two lines\n\n$$\n3x+y=3 \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad x+2y=1.\n$$\n\nThat is, graph each line and see where they intersect." 17592186405038] [17592186405141 1072 #uuid "210d7e79-79c0-5def-9165-30e59785183b" 17592186405038] [17592186405141 1074 "Rewrite the lines as $y=3-3x$ and $y=(1-x)/2$. The first passes through $(0,3)$ and $(1,0)$; the second passes through $(0,1/2)$ and $(1,0)$. Plot and label both lines on the same axes. Their common point is $(1,0)$.\n\n![Reference graph showing the two lines intersecting at one point on the x-axis](images/21/212eb96c55d7bb1d6a9700e6334558773624ae385aeeb7698c16ff86b7c2f058.png)\n\nEach line has a different slope, but both reach the x-axis at $x=1$. That shared point is the intersection. Check it in both original equations: $3(1)+0=3$ and $1+2(0)=1$. Include the graph as requested; listing the point without drawing the lines leaves the graphical task unfinished." 17592186405015] [17592186405142 1085 17592186405255 17592186405038] [17592186405142 1085 17592186405322 17592186405038] [17592186405142 1085 17592186405661 17592186405038] [17592186405142 1086 "Source question: 13(a)–(c).\n\nFind $\\int_C e^{2y}\\cos x\\,dx+2e^{2y}\\sin x\\,dy$ for each curve C." 17592186405038] [17592186405142 1087 #uuid "218b3fdd-8b66-51bd-ad5b-cd0f2833da2a" 17592186405038] [17592186405142 1088 17592186405322 17592186405038] [17592186405143 1068 17592186406181 17592186405015] [17592186405143 1071 "**Question 6 — Part A**\n\nEnter the rectangular equation: {{field-1}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405143 1072 #uuid "2231ecda-8c7c-50fd-a1be-159439d28d06" 17592186405038] [17592186405143 1074 "We have $x+3=2\\sin t$ and $y-1=2\\cos t$, so $(x+3)^2+(y-1)^2=4$. The parameter restriction gives $x\\ge-3$, selecting the right semicircle." 17592186405015] [17592186405144 1064 "**Math 255 — Written Homework 2**\n\nDue: 2026-10-08\nSource: [Original worksheet](/api/source?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F255%2FWHW-2%2F255-WHW2.pdf)\n\nStudy answers and explanations are authored additions, not publisher solutions.\n\n## Instructions\n\nShow clear, neat supporting work or a written explanation for every problem. Some problems are graded for correctness; logical, organized progress can earn partial credit, but correct answers without supporting work do not earn full credit. Work on the printed worksheet, digitally on the PDF, or on separate paper with the same layout. Include first name, last name, and OSU student ID on the cover. Upload a single six-page PDF to Gradescope under **Written Homework 2**: cover followed by one page per problem, including blank problems. Scan printed work or photograph separate pages; combine multiple pages for a problem into one page before merging. Digital work already follows the template. Incorrect formatting receives 0. No due date is stated in the PDF." 17592186045416] [17592186405144 1066 #uuid "228f7037-f111-552e-a947-9f78f9f850de" 17592186405038] [17592186405144 1067 "Assignment information" 17592186405038] [17592186405145 1068 17592186406002 17592186405015] [17592186405145 1071 "**Question 3 — Part A**\n\nUse polar coordinates to evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_0^{\\sqrt{3}}\\int_1^{\\sqrt{4-y^2}}\n\\sqrt{x^2+y^2-1}\\,dx\\,dy.\n$$\n\nIntegral: {{field-1}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405145 1072 #uuid "22d488a9-44de-5413-ab9f-6ebef1a36d31" 17592186405038] [17592186405145 1074 "The region is the first-quadrant portion of $r\\le2$ with $x\\ge1$. Thus $0\\le\\theta\\le\\pi/3$ and $\\sec\\theta\\le r\\le2$. Including the Jacobian, the integral is $\\int_0^{\\pi/3}\\int_{\\sec\\theta}^2 r\\sqrt{r^2-1}\\,dr\\,d\\theta=\\frac13\\int_0^{\\pi/3}(3\\sqrt3-\\tan^3\\theta)\\,d\\theta$. Since $\\int\\tan^3\\theta\\,d\\theta=\\tfrac12\\tan^2\\theta+\\ln|\\cos\\theta|$, its value is $\\pi\\sqrt3/3-1/2+\\ln2/3$." 17592186405015] [17592186405146 1058 17592186405574 17592186405038] [17592186405146 1062 #uuid "22d8d109-9a24-54ad-a955-9e86c10189e9" 17592186405038] [17592186405146 1063 17592186405515 17592186405038] [17592186405147 1071 "**Question 3 — Part A**\n\nExplain the geometric significance of: No solution." 17592186405038] [17592186405147 1072 #uuid "2303b6fa-644e-57a3-9106-b23121ca0f8e" 17592186405038] [17592186405147 1074 "There is no point common to all $k$ lines. Their common intersection is empty.\n\nA solution must satisfy every equation at once. Two distinct parallel lines already prevent a solution. With three or more lines, each pair can intersect at a different point while no point lies on all the lines; pairwise intersection alone is insufficient." 17592186405015] [17592186405148 1089 17592186050338 17592186405015] [17592186405148 1090 17592186405765 17592186405038] [17592186405148 1091 #uuid "231ef3d5-93fd-5d85-9133-54ad11c83683" 17592186405038] [17592186405149 1068 17592186406250 17592186405015] [17592186405149 1071 "**(b)** C consists of the line segment(s) joining $(7\\pi,0)\\to(7\\pi+\\pi/2,1/2)\\to(8\\pi,1)$, in that order.\n\nIntegral: {{integral}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405149 1072 #uuid "23437a06-5c21-5aa3-81d4-47ba297dcd1e" 17592186405038] [17592186405149 1074 "The potential is $f=e^{2y}\\sin x$. Both endpoint values vanish, because sin(7π)=sin(8π)=0. Each path therefore has integral 0; intermediate contributions cancel." 17592186405015] [17592186405150 1068 17592186405850 17592186405015] [17592186405150 1071 "Source question: 3.\n\nFind $\\nabla\\times\\mathbf F$ for $\\mathbf F=\\langle7yz,4z^8x,4yz\\rangle$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405150 1072 #uuid "2351fba0-a80b-50c0-a594-02a4735d09ee" 17592186405038] [17592186405150 1074 "The first component is R_y−Q_z=4z−32xz⁷. The second is P_z−R_x=7y−0, and the third is Q_x−P_y=4z⁸−7z." 17592186405015] [17592186405151 1058 17592186405799 17592186405038] [17592186405151 1062 #uuid "239e4797-f8bc-514f-990d-4ea8e9370447" 17592186405038] [17592186405151 1063 17592186405814 17592186405038] [17592186405152 1062 #uuid "23d79f4d-dc9a-5776-b523-7908c2c88d63" 17592186405038] [17592186405152 1063 17592186405230 17592186405038] [17592186405153 1089 17592186049465 17592186405015] [17592186405153 1089 17592186050540 17592186405015] [17592186405153 1090 17592186405473 17592186405038] [17592186405153 1091 #uuid "23f91ac0-09e0-5577-a446-ed12e0144c71" 17592186405038] [17592186405154 1058 17592186405704 17592186405038] [17592186405154 1062 #uuid "24083991-c010-533e-a900-dd9cac40ec43" 17592186405038] [17592186405154 1063 17592186405762 17592186405038] [17592186405155 1068 17592186406008 17592186405015] [17592186405155 1071 "Source question: 2.\n\nFind $\\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf F$ for $\\mathbf F=\\langle5xye^z,6x^2e^z,6x^6ye^z\\rangle$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405155 1072 #uuid "24a0dcf9-24f3-5f0a-90ea-42307bdb8afd" 17592186405038] [17592186405155 1074 "P_x=5yeᶻ, Q_y=0 because the second component contains no y, and R_z=6x⁶yeᶻ. Add and factor; do not differentiate Q with respect to x." 17592186405015] [17592186405156 1071 "**Question 3 — Part B**\n\nExplain the geometric significance of: A unique solution." 17592186405038] [17592186405156 1072 #uuid "252e7f01-4105-5dd3-aafb-4821a8db1687" 17592186405038] [17592186405156 1074 "All $k$ lines have exactly one common point. The coordinates of that point form the unique solution.\n\nFind a point on two distinct intersecting lines, then check that every remaining line also passes through it. Coincident copies of one line do not create additional solutions once another distinct line fixes a single intersection." 17592186405015] [17592186405157 1062 #uuid "2569d25c-6e3a-5336-b6ad-bc18bf2176bc" 17592186405038] [17592186405157 1063 17592186405537 17592186405038] [17592186405158 1068 17592186405881 17592186405015] [17592186405158 1071 "Source question: 1.\n\nUse the **Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals** to evaluate $\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot d\\mathbf r$. The field $\\mathbf F=\\langle6x-3y,20y-3x\\rangle$ is conservative with the supplied potential $f=3x^2-3xy+10y^2$. The piecewise smooth curve joins $(3,-1)$ to $(5,4)$.\n\nIntegral: {{integral}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405158 1072 #uuid "25e8cc7f-7b52-55ed-90f3-afc7af8e5d0c" 17592186405038] [17592186405158 1074 "$f(5,4)=75-60+160=175$ and $f(3,-1)=27+9+10=46$. Subtract final minus initial: 175−46=129." 17592186405015] [17592186405159 1058 17592186405767 17592186405038] [17592186405159 1062 #uuid "26127704-1090-55bf-b512-78ba7c6f5ed7" 17592186405038] [17592186405159 1063 17592186405293 17592186405038] [17592186405160 1058 17592186405331 17592186405038] [17592186405160 1062 #uuid "26382d61-8a02-566e-b9bb-75ce6c6d6453" 17592186405038] [17592186405160 1063 17592186405813 17592186405038] [17592186405161 1068 17592186406187 17592186405015] [17592186405161 1071 "Source question: 3.\n\nFind $\\iint_S(x+y+z)dS$ on $z=x-20y$ above the xy-region bounded by y=0, x=1, and x=20y.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405161 1072 #uuid "265e4772-afae-5156-b83c-de56b8b144f3" 17592186405038] [17592186405161 1074 "The region is 0≤x≤1, 0≤y≤x/20. On S, x+y+z=2x−19y and dS=√402 dxdy. The inner integral is $[2xy-19y^2/2]_0^{x/20}=61x^2/800$. Integrating x gives 61/2400." 17592186405015] [17592186405162 1089 17592186050149 17592186405015] [17592186405162 1089 17592186050418 17592186405015] [17592186405162 1089 17592186050891 17592186405015] [17592186405162 1090 17592186405231 17592186405038] [17592186405162 1091 #uuid "267f9dc5-5178-52db-9112-19f650d22d08" 17592186405038] [17592186405163 1071 "**(a)** Find every point at which the field is orthogonal or tangent to the curve.\n\n$\\mathbf F=\\langle1,0\\rangle$, $C: y-x^2=-1$." 17592186405038] [17592186405163 1072 #uuid "2699c995-b9b2-5d31-ac4d-2299df3907b0" 17592186405038] [17592186405163 1074 "**tangent-and-orthogonal:** Tangent: $(0,-1)$. Orthogonal: no points.\n\nA tangent is $T=(1,2x)$. The determinant of F and T is 2x, so tangency requires x=0. But $F\\cdot T=1$, so orthogonality is impossible." 17592186405015] [17592186405164 1089 17592186049946 17592186405015] [17592186405164 1089 17592186050142 17592186405015] [17592186405164 1090 17592186405505 17592186405038] [17592186405164 1091 #uuid "26bdee27-1cd8-5c63-a407-5021a3ad5be4" 17592186405038] [17592186405165 1068 17592186406274 17592186405015] [17592186405165 1071 "**Question 4 — Part B**\n\nFind a general solution of the differential equation $xy'=9y-6x$.\n\nYour “general solution” should be valid at every point in an open rectangle that includes the initial condition $(x,y)=(1,-4)$.\n\nUse $C$ for the arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=$ {{field-1}}, on $x>0$." 17592186405015] [17592186405165 1072 #uuid "26e6fa45-f47e-5e39-bff0-33cf4a2c6066" 17592186405038] [17592186405165 1074 "The product equation is $(x^{-9}y)\\prime=-6x^{-9}$. Integrating gives $x^{-9}y=(3/4)x^{-8}+C$, hence $y=(3/4)x+Cx^9$. The normalized coefficients are continuous on any open rectangle containing $(1,-4)$ whose $x$-range stays positive." 17592186405015] [17592186405166 1071 "**(b)** Find every point at which the field is orthogonal or tangent to the curve.\n\n$\\mathbf F=\\langle y,-x\\rangle$, $C: y-x^2=1$." 17592186405038] [17592186405166 1072 #uuid "26ee22cb-47df-5d06-a7c5-e7426f0949fb" 17592186405038] [17592186405166 1074 "**tangent-and-orthogonal:** Tangent: $(0,1)$. Orthogonal: $(1,2)$ and $(-1,2)$.\n\nOn the curve, $F=(x^2+1,-x)$ and $T=(1,2x)$. Their determinant is $x(2x^2+3)$, which vanishes only at x=0. Their dot product is $1-x^2$, so orthogonality occurs at x=±1, y=2." 17592186405015] [17592186405167 1062 #uuid "27680012-56a3-5c80-ae3b-080fff69670a" 17592186405038] [17592186405167 1063 17592186405450 17592186405038] [17592186405168 1058 17592186405793 17592186405038] [17592186405168 1062 #uuid "2779bfb0-7365-5939-bacc-4215e2e7bc4a" 17592186405038] [17592186405168 1063 17592186405561 17592186405038] [17592186405169 1089 17592186048225 17592186405015] [17592186405169 1089 17592186050488 17592186405015] [17592186405169 1090 17592186405529 17592186405038] [17592186405169 1091 #uuid "278bbe57-f9f3-5986-bf4f-4ac15ae514a6" 17592186405038] [17592186405170 1068 17592186406157 17592186405015] [17592186405170 1071 "Source question: 7.\n\nFind the mass of the conical lamina $z=\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}$, $2\\le z\\le5$, with surface density $\\rho=30-z$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405170 1072 #uuid "27e51273-0689-5143-82e5-62771ec38f7d" 17592186405038] [17592186405170 1074 "Use (r cosθ,r sinθ,r), 2≤r≤5. The surface element is √2 r drdθ. Therefore $M=2\\pi\\sqrt2\\int_2^5(30-r)rdr=2\\pi\\sqrt2[15r^2-r^3/3]_2^5=552\\pi\\sqrt2$." 17592186405015] [17592186405171 1071 "**Question 3 — Part C**\n\nA curve that is not an integral curve." 17592186405038] [17592186405171 1072 #uuid "28e9d899-99ee-5600-9524-0629a7b3b393" 17592186405038] [17592186405171 1074 "Draw the horizontal line $y=1/2$ and label it “not an integral curve.” Its slope is $0$, whereas the field has slope $2x$ there. For example, at $(1/2,1/2)$ the field slope is $1$, so the line crosses the field directions rather than following them.\n\n![Authored reference graph](images/9d/9d76186dcece593fb5232a41983bd98bbf55dde24b49d6af5430171ceb90c249.png)\n\nAn isolated point of tangency is insufficient. An integral curve must follow the field throughout its regular portions. Check the tangent mismatch at a specific point." 17592186405015] [17592186405172 1089 17592186048923 17592186405015] [17592186405172 1089 17592186048960 17592186405015] [17592186405172 1089 17592186050994 17592186405015] [17592186405172 1090 17592186405202 17592186405038] [17592186405172 1091 #uuid "293d4f90-5b95-5e48-9074-3d9302bfba20" 17592186405038] [17592186405173 1058 17592186405539 17592186405038] [17592186405173 1062 #uuid "298e1259-bd43-5397-a6e3-f71e680ad491" 17592186405038] [17592186405173 1063 17592186405707 17592186405038] [17592186405174 1068 17592186405849 17592186405015] [17592186405174 1071 "**(a)** C consists of the line segment(s) joining $(8,\\pi/3)\\to(9,\\pi/3)\\to(9,\\pi)$, in that order.\n\nIntegral: {{integral}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405174 1072 #uuid "2a22ab5f-8e26-562e-8cf4-a4358e220ad8" 17592186405038] [17592186405174 1074 "The potential is $f=e^x\\cos y$. All three paths have the same endpoints. Thus $f(9,\\pi)-f(8,\\pi/3)=-e^9-e^8/2$. Differentiating f verifies the two coefficients of the differential." 17592186405015] [17592186405175 1064 "**6.8 The Divergence Theorem**\n\nDue: 2026-12-03\nSource: [Macmillan Achieve](https://achieve.macmillanlearning.com/ihub/assessment/f188d950-dd73-11e0-9572-0800200c9a66/5a1db2a7-4295-4a4c-9dbb-ceb8b174a282/3929e7f7-d086-490c-9ebd-e2b4a59f88cf)\n\nStudy answers and explanations are authored additions, not publisher solutions.\n\n## Instructions\n\nUse exact symbolic notation and fractions where needed. For flux problems, n is the outer unit normal and S is the entire boundary unless stated otherwise. Use the requested theorem or formula and show its application. Source: Sullivan 2e Calculus, W.H. Freeman. Unlimited attempts, no incorrect-attempt penalty; up to 50 days late at a 2% daily penalty." 17592186405015] [17592186405175 1066 #uuid "2a3c5e26-e632-560e-9e74-d3646823d19f" 17592186405038] [17592186405175 1067 "Assignment information" 17592186405038] [17592186405176 1068 17592186406114 17592186405015] [17592186405176 1071 "**Question 5 — Part B**\n\nUse Part A to find $\\partial z/\\partial x$ for the sphere $x^2+y^2+z^2=1$.\n\n$\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}=$ {{field-1}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405176 1072 #uuid "2b1ffd25-40f0-5a3a-81ff-3d56a039ac3f" 17592186405038] [17592186405176 1074 "For $f=x^2+y^2+z^2$, the partial derivatives are $f_x=2x$ and $f_z=2z$. Holding $y$ fixed gives $z_x=-2x/(2z)=-x/z$ for $z\\ne0$. The term $y^2$ has zero partial derivative with respect to $x$." 17592186405015] [17592186405177 1064 "**1.1 Parametric Equations**\n\nSource: [Macmillan Achieve](https://achieve.macmillanlearning.com/ihub/assessment/f188d950-dd73-11e0-9572-0800200c9a66/5a1db2a7-4295-4a4c-9dbb-ceb8b174a282/1af7ea5c-56c7-4f8d-aaec-e540bb4b3c9e?actualCourseId=5a1db2a7-4295-4a4c-9dbb-ceb8b174a282)\nDue: 2026-10-01\nImported: 2026-10-01 · 6 questions\n\nStudy answers, explanations, and reference graphics are authored additions to the linked source." 17592186405015] [17592186405177 1066 #uuid "2b4d9eac-0448-59cf-bfc2-ecbbdbe828de" 17592186405038] [17592186405177 1067 "Assignment information" 17592186405038] [17592186405178 1058 17592186405695 17592186405038] [17592186405178 1062 #uuid "2b546f11-e19c-5902-a16a-4dbac8bf9b30" 17592186405038] [17592186405178 1063 17592186405488 17592186405038] [17592186405179 1068 17592186406300 17592186405015] [17592186405179 1071 "Source question: 3.\n\nFind the divergence of $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)=\\langle7x+4\\cos x,8y+7y\\sin x,7z\\rangle$. \n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405179 1072 #uuid "2c051e01-8005-5d78-b740-dd26d934c808" 17592186405038] [17592186405179 1074 "$P_x=7-4\\sin x$, $Q_y=8+7\\sin x$, $R_z=7$. Their sum is 22+3sin x." 17592186405015] [17592186405180 1068 17592186406235 17592186405015] [17592186405180 1071 "Source question: 1.\n\nFind $\\nabla\\times\\mathbf F$ for $\\mathbf F=\\langle4x,2y,0\\rangle$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405180 1072 #uuid "2c8e935b-4fff-5d0b-92dd-00eb9a4ff1f3" 17592186405038] [17592186405180 1074 "For F=(P,Q,R), curl F=(R_y−Q_z,P_z−R_x,Q_x−P_y). All six cross partials here vanish." 17592186405015] [17592186405181 1068 17592186406067 17592186405015] [17592186405181 1071 "**(a)** $\\mathbf F=\\langle x,y\\rangle=x\\mathbf i+y\\mathbf j$.\n\nPolar form: {{polar}}. Explain how the result agrees with the field plot." 17592186405015] [17592186405181 1072 #uuid "2cc5e181-42e1-5d32-8756-f85d19a09fed" 17592186405038] [17592186405181 1074 "**interpretation:** The radial coefficient is $x\\cos\\theta+y\\sin\\theta=r$, and the angular coefficient is $-x\\sin\\theta+y\\cos\\theta=0$. It points outward with length r.\n\nThe radial coefficient is $x\\cos\\theta+y\\sin\\theta=r$, and the angular coefficient is $-x\\sin\\theta+y\\cos\\theta=0$. It points outward with length r. Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$ and the basis identities from 4(b). Polar directions require r>0; in (a) and (c), the Cartesian field extends to the zero vector at the origin." 17592186405015] [17592186405182 1089 17592186048575 17592186405015] [17592186405182 1089 17592186049137 17592186405015] [17592186405182 1089 17592186049412 17592186405015] [17592186405182 1089 17592186049780 17592186405015] [17592186405182 1090 17592186405211 17592186405038] [17592186405182 1091 #uuid "2cfdfcb5-06f8-515e-b5dd-7a6cf3e36d10" 17592186405038] [17592186405183 1068 17592186405855 17592186405015] [17592186405183 1071 "Source question: 18.\n\nFind the work of $\\mathbf F=\\langle8xy,12y^2\\rangle$ along $\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle t,t^2\\rangle$ from t=0 to t=2.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405183 1072 #uuid "2d0e5a7c-d8af-53aa-ada5-834b7994b42d" 17592186405038] [17592186405183 1074 "$F(r)=(8t^3,12t^4)$ and $r\\prime=(1,2t)$. Integrate $8t^3+24t^5$: $[2t^4+4t^6]_0^2=32+256$." 17592186405015] [17592186405184 1058 17592186405597 17592186405038] [17592186405184 1062 #uuid "2d34fa76-e092-5f41-afdd-44873690233e" 17592186405038] [17592186405184 1063 17592186405300 17592186405038] [17592186405185 1068 17592186406302 17592186405015] [17592186405185 1071 "Source question: 8.\n\nUse **Green’s theorem** to find the area bounded by $y=1/\\sqrt{x-7}$, y=0, x=10, and x=18.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405185 1072 #uuid "2d791267-1db8-54f8-a188-c2f7c84f1df1" 17592186405038] [17592186405185 1074 "Choose P=−y,Q=0, giving $Q_x-P_y=1$ and $A=-\\oint_Cy\\,dx$ for positive orientation. Only the upper boundary contributes; it is traversed from x=18 to 10. Hence $A=\\int_{10}^{18}(x-7)^{-1/2}dx=2(\\sqrt{11}-\\sqrt3)$." 17592186405015] [17592186405186 1058 17592186405757 17592186405038] [17592186405186 1062 #uuid "2ddba153-cfaf-587d-b7f6-672f57461a6c" 17592186405038] [17592186405186 1063 17592186405822 17592186405038] [17592186405187 1071 "**Question 3 — Part A**\n\nDifferentiate $c=f(x,y)$ with respect to $x$, using the chain rule on the right. Conclude that $\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=-\\dfrac{f_x}{f_y}$." 17592186405038] [17592186405187 1072 #uuid "2de99047-ea27-55a6-81ee-f6ea1f3cd5f8" 17592186405038] [17592186405187 1074 "Since $c$ is constant and $y=y(x)$, the chain rule gives $0=f_x\\,dx/dx+f_y\\,dy/dx=f_x+f_y\\,dy/dx$. If $f_y\\ne0$, solving gives $dy/dx=-f_x/f_y$.\n\nBoth paths in the dependence tree contribute. The direct $x$ path gives $f_x$ because $dx/dx=1$; the $y$ path gives $f_y\\,dy/dx$. Division by $f_y$ requires $f_y\\ne0$." 17592186405015] [17592186405188 1068 17592186406301 17592186405015] [17592186405188 1071 "**Question 6 — Part B**\n\nChoose the graph of the curve over the stated parameter interval." 17592186405015] [17592186405188 1072 #uuid "2e51ca75-8fa2-5dce-aa80-75d2509c1883" 17592186405038] [17592186405188 1074 "The center is $(-3,1)$ and the radius is $2$. Since $\\sin t\\ge0$ on $[0,\\pi]$, the curve is the right semicircle, from $(-3,3)$ through $(-1,1)$ to $(-3,-1)$." 17592186405015] [17592186405189 1089 17592186050295 17592186405015] [17592186405189 1089 17592186050866 17592186405015] [17592186405189 1090 17592186405627 17592186405038] [17592186405189 1091 #uuid "2e94ae61-155c-5580-aa18-4e5855960283" 17592186405038] [17592186405190 1058 17592186405115 17592186405038] [17592186405190 1062 #uuid "2ef2ab63-09f4-54a4-b836-609f5bf99743" 17592186405038] [17592186405190 1063 17592186405109 17592186405038] [17592186405191 1089 17592186051091 17592186405015] [17592186405191 1090 17592186405406 17592186405038] [17592186405191 1091 #uuid "2efd4f9f-9ec4-5ee8-9489-3a8d7e6429a7" 17592186405038] [17592186405192 1058 17592186405407 17592186405038] [17592186405192 1062 #uuid "2f20bfde-26a4-58ca-a767-b4a4898e3ea3" 17592186405038] [17592186405192 1063 17592186405471 17592186405038] [17592186405193 1089 17592186048299 17592186405015] [17592186405193 1089 17592186048819 17592186405015] [17592186405193 1089 17592186050866 17592186405015] [17592186405193 1090 17592186405546 17592186405038] [17592186405193 1091 #uuid "3010ee9c-3b6f-53bd-8f31-5c3c01065cbb" 17592186405038] [17592186405194 1071 "**Question 2 — Part B**\n\nShow that the volume is $|\\mathbf w\\cdot(\\mathbf u\\times\\mathbf v)|$. The quantity $\\mathbf w\\cdot(\\mathbf u\\times\\mathbf v)$ is the scalar triple product of $\\mathbf u$, $\\mathbf v$, and $\\mathbf w$." 17592186405038] [17592186405194 1072 #uuid "3021b788-f4db-57a9-8db3-90426b903e88" 17592186405038] [17592186405194 1074 "The base area is $\\lVert\\mathbf u\\times\\mathbf v\\rVert$ and the height is $\\lVert\\mathbf w\\rVert|\\cos\\theta|$. Their product is $|\\lVert\\mathbf w\\rVert\\lVert\\mathbf u\\times\\mathbf v\\rVert\\cos\\theta|=|\\mathbf w\\cdot(\\mathbf u\\times\\mathbf v)|$.\n\nThe dot product measures the component of $\\mathbf w$ along the base normal, multiplied by the base area. Its sign records orientation; absolute value gives nonnegative volume. A degenerate base or a side lying in the base plane gives zero volume." 17592186405015] [17592186405195 1043 17592186405103 17592186405038] [17592186405195 1043 17592186405116 17592186405038] [17592186405195 1043 17592186405118 17592186405038] [17592186405195 1043 17592186405119 17592186405038] [17592186405195 1043 17592186405365 17592186405038] [17592186405195 1043 17592186405374 17592186405038] [17592186405195 1043 17592186405520 17592186405038] [17592186405195 1043 17592186405593 17592186405038] [17592186405195 1043 17592186405599 17592186405038] [17592186405195 1043 17592186405741 17592186405038] [17592186405195 1043 17592186405773 17592186405038] [17592186405195 1044 17592186045461 17592186405038] [17592186405195 1045 "MTH 255 — OHW-7" 17592186405038] [17592186405195 1046 17592186405773 17592186405038] [17592186405195 1047 "2026-11-06T07:59:59.000Z" 17592186405038] [17592186405195 1048 17592186405043 17592186405038] [17592186405195 1053 #uuid "30756078-0224-512a-a944-e9e746065a74" 17592186405038] [17592186405196 1068 17592186406057 17592186405015] [17592186405196 1071 "Source question: 1.\n\nLet $S:z=4x+3y+5$, $0\\le x\\le1$, $0\\le y\\le4$. Find $\\iint_S(x^2+y^2)dS$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405196 1072 #uuid "30f7676a-3b0f-5273-80f7-f3100f46de67" 17592186405038] [17592186405196 1074 "$dS=\\sqrt{1+4^2+3^2}dxdy=\\sqrt{26}dxdy$. Over the rectangle, the integral of x² is 4/3 and that of y² is 64/3. Their sum is 68/3." 17592186405015] [17592186405197 1071 "Source exercise: **1.1.1**, [printed page 7 (PDF page 19)](/api/source?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F341%2FKuttler-LinearAlgebra-AFirstCourse-2017A.pdf#page=19).\n\n**Question 1**\n\nGraphically, find the point $(x_1,y_1)$ which lies on both lines,\n\n$$\nx+3y=1 \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad 4x-y=3.\n$$\n\nThat is, graph each line and see where they intersect." 17592186405038] [17592186405197 1072 #uuid "311d12c0-a56a-5aea-bb69-3187ddb6e2d3" 17592186405038] [17592186405197 1074 "Plot $y=(1-x)/3$ using, for example, $(0,1/3)$ and $(1,0)$. Plot $y=4x-3$ using $(0,-3)$ and $(3/4,0)$. Draw both lines on the same axes and mark their common point.\n\nThe graph places the intersection near $(0.77,0.08)$. Its exact coordinates are $\\left(\\frac{10}{13},\\frac{1}{13}\\right)$.\n\n![Reference graph showing both lines and their intersection](images/6b/6b7c1afd7bdc97e66c0477241907e1be5bc065b6e7f456182fc68b509d2012a5.png)\n\nA solution must lie on both lines, so look for their crossing rather than either line's intercept. Your response should include two labeled lines on common axes and an intersection consistent with your plotting scale. The exact point checks both equations: $10/13+3(1/13)=1$ and $4(10/13)-1/13=3$. An algebraic answer alone does not complete the requested graph." 17592186405015] [17592186405198 1068 17592186406284 17592186405015] [17592186405198 1071 "Source question: 2.\n\nLet $S:z=7x^2+6y$, $0\\le x\\le1$, $0\\le y\\le2$. Find $\\iint_S4x\\,dS$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405198 1072 #uuid "31ee107e-5eb3-5a84-a8ab-66a3caab956b" 17592186405038] [17592186405198 1074 "$dS=\\sqrt{37+196x^2}dxdy$. Integrating out y gives $\\int_0^1 8x\\sqrt{37+196x^2}dx$. Substitute u=37+196x²; the result is $2[u^{3/2}]_{37}^{233}/147$." 17592186405015] [17592186405199 1058 17592186405298 17592186405038] [17592186405199 1062 #uuid "3202eba9-b965-58d0-a18b-0e35f595326f" 17592186405038] [17592186405199 1063 17592186405162 17592186405038] [17592186405200 1058 17592186405790 17592186405038] [17592186405200 1062 #uuid "325909e0-a47d-5b9f-8262-dbae6194b18f" 17592186405038] [17592186405200 1063 17592186405336 17592186405038] [17592186405201 1071 "**(a)** The vectors i and j are unit and orthogonal. Confirm that $\\hat{\\mathbf r}$ and $\\hat{\\boldsymbol\\theta}$ are also unit and orthogonal." 17592186405038] [17592186405201 1072 #uuid "33107394-a702-572a-88a7-643e15f33d3a" 17592186405038] [17592186405201 1074 "**proof:** Both squared norms are $\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta=1$. Their dot product is $-\\cos\\theta\\sin\\theta+\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta=0$. Thus each has length 1 and they are perpendicular.\n\nCheck both lengths and the dot product; orthogonality alone does not imply unit length." 17592186405015] [17592186405202 1085 17592186405453 17592186405038] [17592186405202 1085 17592186405692 17592186405038] [17592186405202 1086 "Source question: 11.\n\nConsider $\\mathbf F(x,y)=-y\\mathbf i+x\\mathbf j$." 17592186405038] [17592186405202 1087 #uuid "3329cc20-e925-586c-8436-5d656a240bfe" 17592186405038] [17592186405202 1088 17592186405692 17592186405038] [17592186405203 1058 17592186405229 17592186405038] [17592186405203 1062 #uuid "33729004-b50e-5d2f-834e-d49725efa1e1" 17592186405038] [17592186405203 1063 17592186405751 17592186405038] [17592186405204 1058 17592186405618 17592186405038] [17592186405204 1062 #uuid "33929ea3-656b-50c2-ba37-6249e5ee7e1d" 17592186405038] [17592186405204 1063 17592186405712 17592186405038] [17592186405205 1062 #uuid "345fb758-79b1-504f-bd3a-331aa23e4bd3" 17592186405038] [17592186405205 1063 17592186405530 17592186405038] [17592186405206 1058 17592186405106 17592186405038] [17592186405206 1062 #uuid "34c10257-f839-5851-beec-c7b717ab5a77" 17592186405038] [17592186405206 1063 17592186405375 17592186405038] [17592186405207 1089 17592186050573 17592186405015] [17592186405207 1090 17592186405185 17592186405038] [17592186405207 1091 #uuid "3507a79d-015b-52fa-9724-eae6fd4ab55f" 17592186405038] [17592186405208 1058 17592186405435 17592186405038] [17592186405208 1062 #uuid "35ba5832-b580-5b86-bfcc-81bf0a128a7f" 17592186405038] [17592186405208 1063 17592186405370 17592186405038] [17592186405209 1068 17592186405992 17592186405015] [17592186405209 1071 "Source question: 9.\n\nFind the curl of $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)=\\langle5\\sin(6xy),7\\cos(xy^2),7x\\rangle$. Use component form or standard basis vectors.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405209 1072 #uuid "35e32817-af33-560e-bc5d-e6affe5b9a86" 17592186405038] [17592186405209 1074 "$R_y-Q_z=0$ and $P_z-R_x=-7$. The third component is $Q_x-P_y=-7y^2\\sin(xy^2)-30x\\cos(6xy)$ by the chain rule." 17592186405015] [17592186405210 1089 17592186051091 17592186405015] [17592186405210 1090 17592186405307 17592186405038] [17592186405210 1091 #uuid "368aff29-f906-5b63-b619-270aaff49657" 17592186405038] [17592186405211 1068 17592186406143 17592186405015] [17592186405211 1068 17592186406281 17592186405015] [17592186405211 1071 "Textbook problem **2.1.43**.\n\n**Question 2**\n\nExperiments indicate that glucose is absorbed by the body at a rate proportional to the amount of glucose present in the bloodstream. Let $\\lambda$ denote the (positive) constant of proportionality. Now suppose glucose is injected into a patient's bloodstream at a constant rate of $r$ units per unit of time. Let $G=G(t)$ be the number of units in the patient's bloodstream at time $t>0$. Then\n\n$$\nG'=-\\lambda G+r,\n$$\n\nwhere the first term on the right is due to the absorption of the glucose by the patient's body and the second term is due to the injection. Determine $G$ for $t>0$, given that $G(0)=G_0$. Also, find\n\n$$\n\\lim_{t\\to\\infty}G(t).\n$$\n\n$G(t)=$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\lim_{t\\to\\infty}G(t)=$ {{field-2}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405211 1072 #uuid "36f72ed9-a0cf-588e-bde2-1df93ea2f549" 17592186405038] [17592186405211 1074 "The integrating factor is $e^{\\lambda t}$, giving $(e^{\\lambda t}G)\\prime=re^{\\lambda t}$. Hence $G=r/\\lambda+Ce^{-\\lambda t}$, with $C=G_0-r/\\lambda$. Because $\\lambda>0$, the exponential tends to zero. Differentiation verifies $G\\prime=-\\lambda G+r$, and $G(0)=G_0$. This is a mathematical model, with equilibrium $r/\\lambda$." 17592186405015] [17592186405212 1062 #uuid "3712dea9-ed24-5d95-ae4a-f097f40d967c" 17592186405038] [17592186405212 1063 17592186405675 17592186405038] [17592186405213 1068 17592186405886 17592186405015] [17592186405213 1068 17592186406138 17592186405015] [17592186405213 1071 "**Question 6 — Part B**\n\nCompute both dot products. Conclude that $\\nabla\\varphi$ is perpendicular to both vectors and hence to the level surface at $(x,y,z)$.\n\n$\\nabla\\varphi\\cdot\\langle\\varphi_z,0,-\\varphi_x\\rangle=$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\nabla\\varphi\\cdot\\langle0,\\varphi_z,-\\varphi_y\\rangle=$ {{field-2}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405213 1072 #uuid "378c93ed-8654-5c14-84c7-05d98e1e60b2" 17592186405038] [17592186405213 1074 "For $\\nabla\\varphi=\\langle\\varphi_x,\\varphi_y,\\varphi_z\\rangle$, the products are $\\varphi_x\\varphi_z-\\varphi_z\\varphi_x=0$ and $\\varphi_y\\varphi_z-\\varphi_z\\varphi_y=0$. When $\\varphi_z\\ne0$, Part A supplies two independent tangent directions, so orthogonality to both makes the gradient normal to the tangent plane. Include this conclusion, not just the two zeros." 17592186405015] [17592186405214 1068 17592186405910 17592186405015] [17592186405214 1071 "[Open in WeBWorK](https://webwork2.science.oregonstate.edu/webwork2/MTH_256_020_F2026/WebWork_1/5)\n\n**Question 5**\n\nSolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\ny'+\\frac{1}{x+7}y=x^{-2},\\qquad y(1)=6.\n$$\n\n$y(x)=$ {{field-1}}, on $x>0$." 17592186405015] [17592186405214 1072 #uuid "37d59c3a-3057-5e0d-a4b7-111ae8dd154d" 17592186405038] [17592186405214 1074 "An integrating factor on the interval containing $1$ is $x+7$. Thus $[(x+7)y]\\prime=(x+7)/x^2=1/x+7/x^2$, and $(x+7)y=\\ln x-7/x+C$. At $x=1$, $48=-7+C$, so $C=55$. The maximal coefficient interval containing the initial point is $(0,\\infty)$." 17592186405015] [17592186405215 1043 17592186405250 17592186405038] [17592186405215 1043 17592186405330 17592186405038] [17592186405215 1043 17592186405383 17592186405038] [17592186405215 1043 17592186405630 17592186405038] [17592186405215 1043 17592186405631 17592186405038] [17592186405215 1043 17592186405700 17592186405038] [17592186405215 1043 17592186405733 17592186405038] [17592186405215 1044 17592186045461 17592186405038] [17592186405215 1045 "MTH 255 — R-1" 17592186405038] [17592186405215 1046 17592186405733 17592186405038] [17592186405215 1047 "2026-09-30T06:59:59.000Z" 17592186405038] [17592186405215 1048 17592186405043 17592186405038] [17592186405215 1053 #uuid "381cd82b-c022-5767-b8ee-f081bb6036c6" 17592186405038] [17592186405216 1089 17592186051091 17592186405015] [17592186405216 1090 17592186405150 17592186405038] [17592186405216 1091 #uuid "38596a05-50aa-5553-9465-1f1519b03cf6" 17592186405038] [17592186405217 1062 #uuid "38a3c6d8-8de4-531d-bfbf-b66e46a1ed37" 17592186405038] [17592186405217 1063 17592186405808 17592186405038] [17592186405218 1062 #uuid "39184bf9-ab83-5f6a-910d-a23644208e8a" 17592186405038] [17592186405218 1063 17592186405815 17592186405038] [17592186405219 1068 17592186406310 17592186405015] [17592186405219 1071 "Source question: 4.\n\nFind $\\int_C7y\\,ds$ along $y=x^3/2$ from (0,0) to (2,4).\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405219 1072 #uuid "393c353d-ed34-5314-8a22-8aa25230d955" 17592186405038] [17592186405219 1074 "$ds=\\sqrt{1+9x^4/4}\\,dx$ and 7y=7x³/2. Set u=1+9x⁴/4, du=9x³dx. This gives $\\frac7{27}[u^{3/2}]_1^{37}$." 17592186405015] [17592186405220 1043 17592186405098 17592186405038] [17592186405220 1043 17592186405152 17592186405038] [17592186405220 1043 17592186405360 17592186405038] [17592186405220 1043 17592186405363 17592186405038] [17592186405220 1043 17592186405424 17592186405038] [17592186405220 1043 17592186405455 17592186405038] [17592186405220 1043 17592186405465 17592186405038] [17592186405220 1043 17592186405506 17592186405038] [17592186405220 1043 17592186405600 17592186405038] [17592186405220 1043 17592186405638 17592186405038] [17592186405220 1043 17592186405647 17592186405038] [17592186405220 1043 17592186405729 17592186405038] [17592186405220 1043 17592186405834 17592186405038] [17592186405220 1044 17592186045461 17592186405038] [17592186405220 1045 "MTH 255 — OHW-6" 17592186405038] [17592186405220 1046 17592186405506 17592186405038] [17592186405220 1047 "2026-10-30T06:59:59.000Z" 17592186405038] [17592186405220 1048 17592186405043 17592186405038] [17592186405220 1053 #uuid "3982a924-f205-5275-979b-ad0ff4efbb73" 17592186405038] [17592186405221 1058 17592186405205 17592186405038] [17592186405221 1062 #uuid "39e0b2ed-74d8-5fd1-8603-095cb3a48069" 17592186405038] [17592186405221 1063 17592186405411 17592186405038] [17592186405222 1068 17592186405900 17592186405015] [17592186405222 1071 "**(c)** C consists of the line segment(s) joining $(8,\\pi/3)\\to(8,\\pi/2)\\to(9,\\pi)$, in that order.\n\nIntegral: {{integral}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405222 1072 #uuid "39e772b8-7607-5ac1-82e7-817c3b5815eb" 17592186405038] [17592186405222 1074 "The potential is $f=e^x\\cos y$. All three paths have the same endpoints. Thus $f(9,\\pi)-f(8,\\pi/3)=-e^9-e^8/2$. Differentiating f verifies the two coefficients of the differential." 17592186405015] [17592186405223 1058 17592186405252 17592186405038] [17592186405223 1062 #uuid "3a515895-cb3f-5a02-b043-44de081e3b48" 17592186405038] [17592186405223 1063 17592186405315 17592186405038] [17592186405224 1068 17592186405993 17592186405015] [17592186405224 1071 "**Question 1 — Part B**\n\nWhat is the maximum rate of change of the voltage at $P$?\n\nMaximum rate: {{field-1}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405224 1072 #uuid "3a79ded3-4b07-5789-80aa-ca3c4678dc65" 17592186405038] [17592186405224 1074 "The largest directional derivative is $\\|\\nabla V(P)\\|=\\sqrt{12^2+0^2+16^2}=20$, by Cauchy–Schwarz for unit directions." 17592186405015] [17592186405225 1071 "**Question 4 — Part A**\n\nThere is a helix in the room with parameterization\n\n$$\n\\langle x,y,z\\rangle=\\langle\\cos t,\\sin t,t\\rangle.\n$$\n\nUse chain rule to find $\\dfrac{dw}{dt}$. Reflect on whether your answer makes sense." 17592186405038] [17592186405225 1072 #uuid "3b5a5cc9-b23c-5e50-8e56-9a1731ec22f0" 17592186405038] [17592186405225 1074 "By the chain rule, $dw/dt=2x(-\\sin t)+2y\\cos t+2z=2\\cos t(-\\sin t)+2\\sin t\\cos t+2t=2t$. This makes sense because on the helix $w=1+t^2$: the circular motion leaves $x^2+y^2$ unchanged while height changes.\n\nInclude all three coordinate contributions. Substituting into $w$ first gives an independent check, but the requested derivation should explicitly use the chain rule." 17592186405015] [17592186405226 1089 17592186049768 17592186405015] [17592186405226 1089 17592186051091 17592186405015] [17592186405226 1090 17592186405686 17592186405038] [17592186405226 1091 #uuid "3c0a433d-29b9-540c-a344-f1d9fa83b28f" 17592186405038] [17592186405227 1089 17592186050989 17592186405015] [17592186405227 1090 17592186405259 17592186405038] [17592186405227 1091 #uuid "3c0ac35a-f49d-5436-8f85-53ee103e1ab8" 17592186405038] [17592186405228 1089 17592186048832 17592186405015] [17592186405228 1089 17592186049150 17592186405015] [17592186405228 1089 17592186049168 17592186405015] [17592186405228 1090 17592186405821 17592186405038] [17592186405228 1091 #uuid "3c465680-5f9e-5a65-9642-fc111b2ba28e" 17592186405038] [17592186405229 1058 17592186405572 17592186405038] [17592186405229 1062 #uuid "3c4b870b-8c96-5748-a850-278f9c3ea827" 17592186405038] [17592186405229 1063 17592186405744 17592186405038] [17592186405230 1089 17592186050573 17592186405015] [17592186405230 1090 17592186405101 17592186405038] [17592186405230 1091 #uuid "3c978f8d-d0f0-5398-9e77-0fc642ab3f19" 17592186405038] [17592186405231 1085 17592186405283 17592186405038] [17592186405231 1085 17592186405364 17592186405038] [17592186405231 1085 17592186405607 17592186405038] [17592186405231 1085 17592186405766 17592186405038] [17592186405231 1086 "Source question: 1(a)–(d), PDF page 2.\n\nThe gradient’s domain is the potential’s domain minus any additional points where a partial derivative does not exist." 17592186405038] [17592186405231 1087 #uuid "3cc0e38b-edca-5f6c-857c-76d6bc2e6e13" 17592186405038] [17592186405231 1088 17592186405607 17592186405038] [17592186405232 1089 17592186051076 17592186405015] [17592186405232 1090 17592186405654 17592186405038] [17592186405232 1091 #uuid "3ce2fe39-fb93-5f07-9341-02998105b7a5" 17592186405038] [17592186405233 1085 17592186405323 17592186405038] [17592186405233 1085 17592186405557 17592186405038] [17592186405233 1085 17592186405738 17592186405038] [17592186405233 1086 "Source exercise: **1.1.3**, [printed page 7 (PDF page 19)](/api/source?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F341%2FKuttler-LinearAlgebra-AFirstCourse-2017A.pdf#page=19).\n\nYou have a system of $k$ equations in two variables, $k\\geq 2$. Explain the geometric significance of:" 17592186405038] [17592186405233 1087 #uuid "3d0c16a2-0d5a-55ba-a538-fa1e58cb9b70" 17592186405038] [17592186405233 1088 17592186405323 17592186405038] [17592186405234 1058 17592186405602 17592186405038] [17592186405234 1062 #uuid "3ddca503-d723-542b-9689-19a1ee35b3e5" 17592186405038] [17592186405234 1063 17592186405054 17592186405038] [17592186405235 1089 17592186050514 17592186405015] [17592186405235 1090 17592186405475 17592186405038] [17592186405235 1091 #uuid "3dfbcf3c-112f-5673-827f-ae65affb00af" 17592186405038] [17592186405236 1068 17592186406178 17592186405015] [17592186405236 1071 "Source question: 10.\n\nLet $\\mathbf F=\\langle x,y,z\\rangle$ and use $V=\\frac13\\iint_S\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,dS$, with outward n, to find the volume of a sphere of radius R.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405236 1072 #uuid "3dfe008e-a7e5-5320-9768-ea799426d409" 17592186405038] [17592186405236 1074 "Center the sphere at the origin. On its surface n=(x,y,z)/R, so F·n=R. Multiplying by its surface area 4πR² gives flux 4πR³. Divide by 3 to obtain V=4πR³/3." 17592186405015] [17592186405237 1068 17592186405938 17592186405015] [17592186405237 1071 "Source question: 9.\n\nLet $\\mathbf F=\\langle x,y,z\\rangle$ and use $V=\\frac13\\iint_S\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,dS$, with outward n, to find the volume of a right circular cone of height h and base radius R. Hint: orient the cone with vertex at the origin as shown.\n\n![Cone with vertex at the origin](images/b6/b680c204ce20d30c0451dede0c53b8fbef270bbacb3a2bcc6084796cb91a3018.png)\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405237 1072 #uuid "3e523128-6517-5b34-b017-bec6f0c077df" 17592186405038] [17592186405237 1074 "Place the vertex at the origin and base at z=h. The position vector F is tangent to the conical side along each generating line, so the side contributes zero flux. On the top disk the outward normal is k, so F·n=h and flux=hπR². The stated formula gives V=πR²h/3." 17592186405015] [17592186405238 1071 "**Question 4 — Part A**\n\nConclude that $\\langle\\varphi_y,-\\varphi_x\\rangle$ is tangent to the level curve at $(x,y)$." 17592186405038] [17592186405238 1072 #uuid "3e831647-6617-58b1-8d0e-7be3292e7716" 17592186405038] [17592186405238 1074 "Where $\\varphi_y\\ne0$, a tangent direction is $\\langle1,dy/dx\\rangle=\\langle1,-\\varphi_x/\\varphi_y\\rangle$. Multiplying by $\\varphi_y$ gives $\\langle\\varphi_y,-\\varphi_x\\rangle$, so it is tangent. If $\\varphi_y=0$ but $\\varphi_x\\ne0$, solving locally for $x(y)$ instead gives a vertical tangent, also represented by this vector.\n\nMultiplying a nonzero tangent vector by a nonzero scalar preserves its tangent line, even if the direction reverses. A meaningful tangent direction requires a regular point: $\\nabla\\varphi\\ne\\mathbf0$. At a critical point this formula may give the zero vector and does not determine a tangent." 17592186405015] [17592186405239 1064 "Source: [MTH341-WHW1.pdf](/api/source?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F341%2FWHW-1%2FMTH341-WHW1.pdf)\nDue: 2026-10-07\n\nTextbook: [A First Course in Linear Algebra — Version 2017, Revision A](/api/source?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F341%2FKuttler-LinearAlgebra-AFirstCourse-2017A.pdf), by Lyryx Learning, based on the original text by K. Kuttler. Exercise text reproduced from the CC BY textbook; see its license page.\n\nStudy answers, explanations, and reference graphs are authored additions to the linked textbook exercises.\n\n## Instructions\n\nPut your solutions on separate sheets of paper (8.5 × 11 inches or A4). Use the original assignment sheet as a cover sheet and fill in your name and student ID. Submit your homework on Canvas. The assignment sheet states that late homework is not accepted." 17592186405015] [17592186405239 1066 #uuid "3f266a7a-9f91-53eb-b355-b6c67b3a9be2" 17592186405038] [17592186405239 1067 "Assignment information" 17592186405038] [17592186405240 1089 17592186049512 17592186405015] [17592186405240 1089 17592186050037 17592186405015] [17592186405240 1089 17592186050796 17592186405015] [17592186405240 1089 17592186051090 17592186405015] [17592186405240 1090 17592186405788 17592186405038] [17592186405240 1091 #uuid "3f41e426-021b-5e0f-850b-85a8da8fe58c" 17592186405038] [17592186405241 1058 17592186405498 17592186405038] [17592186405241 1062 #uuid "3f67bab9-a61d-5a2d-975b-d7a157780b0e" 17592186405038] [17592186405241 1063 17592186405189 17592186405038] [17592186405242 1089 17592186048239 17592186405015] [17592186405242 1089 17592186048641 17592186405015] [17592186405242 1089 17592186049261 17592186405015] [17592186405242 1089 17592186050037 17592186405015] [17592186405242 1089 17592186050149 17592186405015] [17592186405242 1090 17592186405806 17592186405038] [17592186405242 1091 #uuid "3fb3d0b7-62c7-5189-95dc-fee0bc320b2a" 17592186405038] [17592186405243 1058 17592186405221 17592186405038] [17592186405243 1062 #uuid "40278735-a000-5feb-b258-2e16563a7bb9" 17592186405038] [17592186405243 1063 17592186405551 17592186405038] [17592186405244 1058 17592186405356 17592186405038] [17592186405244 1062 #uuid "40438b6b-efa9-5dc0-86a3-6a9eba771dc2" 17592186405038] [17592186405244 1063 17592186405596 17592186405038] [17592186405245 1064 "**1.2 Calculus of Parametric Curves**\n\nSource: [Macmillan Achieve](https://achieve.macmillanlearning.com/ihub/assessment/f188d950-dd73-11e0-9572-0800200c9a66/5a1db2a7-4295-4a4c-9dbb-ceb8b174a282/2800fbc5-4e58-452a-89b1-afe688a3b8bf?actualCourseId=5a1db2a7-4295-4a4c-9dbb-ceb8b174a282)\nDue: 2026-10-01\nImported: 2026-10-01 · 10 questions\n\nStudy answers, explanations, and reference graphics are authored additions to the linked source." 17592186405015] [17592186405245 1066 #uuid "40649fcb-7333-54ba-a376-38a8953c6c7d" 17592186405038] [17592186405245 1067 "Assignment information" 17592186405038] [17592186405246 1058 17592186405302 17592186405038] [17592186405246 1062 #uuid "412874e7-71c8-5502-a19e-44a4c5765cf5" 17592186405038] [17592186405246 1063 17592186405193 17592186405038] [17592186405247 1058 17592186405168 17592186405038] [17592186405247 1062 #uuid "4129c234-e9d8-59b9-858e-593f406380ba" 17592186405038] [17592186405247 1063 17592186405783 17592186405038] [17592186405248 1058 17592186405199 17592186405038] [17592186405248 1062 #uuid "413869a6-4e38-5e62-82a1-a5d93522667e" 17592186405038] [17592186405248 1063 17592186405144 17592186405038] [17592186405249 1068 17592186406117 17592186405015] [17592186405249 1071 "Source question: 5.\n\nFor the conservative field $\\mathbf F=\\langle7x^2+y^2,2xy+\\sin y\\rangle$, evaluate $\\int_C(7x^2+y^2)dx+(2xy+\\sin y)dy$ from (0,0) to (4,0) along a piecewise smooth curve.\n\nIntegral: {{integral}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405249 1072 #uuid "413f1c89-e850-582d-a37b-4374f78ad053" 17592186405038] [17592186405249 1074 "A potential is $f=7x^3/3+xy^2-\\cos y$. Its partial derivatives reproduce the field. Then $f(4,0)-f(0,0)=(448/3-1)-(-1)=448/3$." 17592186405015] [17592186405250 1058 17592186405631 17592186405038] [17592186405250 1062 #uuid "414677d8-a81b-50bd-ae0b-c5479c76deab" 17592186405038] [17592186405250 1063 17592186405242 17592186405038] [17592186405251 1089 17592186050566 17592186405015] [17592186405251 1090 17592186405828 17592186405038] [17592186405251 1091 #uuid "4152b34c-8d3f-5593-a073-50b1e7afd378" 17592186405038] [17592186405252 1062 #uuid "415ecc1e-f641-5519-9c75-1245fc0ce7d0" 17592186405038] [17592186405252 1063 17592186405222 17592186405038] [17592186405253 1071 "**Question 4 — Part B**\n\nThere is a cone in the room with parameterization\n\n$$\n\\langle x,y,z\\rangle=\\langle r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta,r\\rangle.\n$$\n\nUse chain rule to find $\\dfrac{\\partial w}{\\partial r}$ and $\\dfrac{\\partial w}{\\partial\\theta}$. Reflect on whether your answers make sense." 17592186405038] [17592186405253 1072 #uuid "4167989d-ba81-5251-9167-13ec920ddaca" 17592186405038] [17592186405253 1074 "$w_r=2x\\cos\\theta+2y\\sin\\theta+2z=4r$. Also $w_\\theta=2x(-r\\sin\\theta)+2y(r\\cos\\theta)+2z(0)=0$. On the cone $w=2r^2$, so it increases with radius and is independent of the angular coordinate.\n\nFor $w_r$, the $z=r$ coordinate contributes another $2r$. For $w_\\theta$, the sine/cosine terms cancel. Hold the other parameter fixed for each partial derivative." 17592186405015] [17592186405254 1085 17592186405105 17592186405038] [17592186405254 1085 17592186405132 17592186405038] [17592186405254 1085 17592186405670 17592186405038] [17592186405254 1086 "Source question: 3(a)–(c), PDF page 4." 17592186405038] [17592186405254 1087 #uuid "41b18ac5-4e50-5775-a25b-c5b24a12bf68" 17592186405038] [17592186405254 1088 17592186405132 17592186405038] [17592186405255 1062 #uuid "41fbe6a2-d901-52bc-a153-73b7d763b33f" 17592186405038] [17592186405255 1063 17592186405710 17592186405038] [17592186405256 1064 "MTH 256 F 2026 Written Homework 1\n\nDate imported: 2026-09-30\nDue: 2026-10-02\n\nSource: [Original worksheet](/api/source?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F256%2FWHW-1%2F256-WHW1.pdf)\n\nStudy answers, explanations, and reference graphics are authored additions to the linked source." 17592186405015] [17592186405256 1066 #uuid "423c452b-5d70-55b4-8032-e4e92ee63d69" 17592186405038] [17592186405256 1067 "Assignment information" 17592186405038] [17592186405257 1058 17592186405658 17592186405038] [17592186405257 1062 #uuid "4260a7a8-0c0f-5b53-b212-2ef38df928b5" 17592186405038] [17592186405257 1063 17592186405083 17592186405038] [17592186405258 1089 17592186048960 17592186405015] [17592186405258 1089 17592186049721 17592186405015] [17592186405258 1090 17592186405254 17592186405038] [17592186405258 1091 #uuid "43336226-8048-5d54-b813-998eb740ce17" 17592186405038] [17592186405259 1068 17592186406111 17592186405015] [17592186405259 1071 "Source question: 11.\n\nFind c so that $\\mathbf F=\\langle9xy,5cx^2,0\\rangle$ is conservative in space.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405259 1072 #uuid "439fb7e3-b5d0-5388-898e-ba5874d315cc" 17592186405038] [17592186405259 1074 "The only nontrivial cross-partial condition is P_y=Q_x: 9x=10cx for every x. Hence c=9/10. A global potential is f=(9/2)x²y, independent of z, which verifies sufficiency." 17592186405015] [17592186405260 1058 17592186405674 17592186405038] [17592186405260 1062 #uuid "444db75a-6f7e-524a-913d-204514d0e797" 17592186405038] [17592186405260 1063 17592186405432 17592186405038] [17592186405261 1068 17592186406103 17592186405015] [17592186405261 1071 "**Question 2 — Part B**\n\nGive the domain in interval notation: $x\\in$ {{field-1}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405261 1072 #uuid "449cb27b-f44b-5945-b6fe-56020bda4ddb" 17592186405038] [17592186405261 1074 "As $t$ ranges over all real numbers, the nonconstant linear function $x=8t+1$ also takes every real value. The domain is $(-\\infty,\\infty)$." 17592186405015] [17592186405262 1058 17592186405682 17592186405038] [17592186405262 1062 #uuid "4505d764-700d-5d20-af13-5e321650fa23" 17592186405038] [17592186405262 1063 17592186405240 17592186405038] [17592186405263 1068 17592186406159 17592186405015] [17592186405263 1071 "**Question 11 — Part B**\n\nHow does the magnitude of a field vector depend on its position?" 17592186405015] [17592186405263 1072 #uuid "4521702d-06b2-51ae-b0a7-3094c46ca97a" 17592186405038] [17592186405263 1074 "The magnitude is $\\sqrt{(-y)^2+x^2}=\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}$, which is the radius of the circle centered at the origin." 17592186405015] [17592186405264 1068 17592186406207 17592186405015] [17592186405264 1071 "**Question 4 — Part B**\n\nPoints with vertical tangents: {{field-1}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405264 1072 #uuid "4576d74b-e149-587c-9e92-e199d7405580" 17592186405038] [17592186405264 1074 "A vertical tangent requires $x'=0$ and $y'\\ne0$. Solving $3t^2-144=0$ gives $t=\\pm4\\sqrt3$. Since $x=t(48-144)=-96t$ and $y=96$, the points are $(-384\\sqrt3,96)$ and $(384\\sqrt3,96)$." 17592186405015] [17592186405265 1089 17592186051091 17592186405015] [17592186405265 1090 17592186405590 17592186405038] [17592186405265 1091 #uuid "457de754-e7fa-575e-b6fa-066b295b9bd9" 17592186405038] [17592186405266 1068 17592186406123 17592186405015] [17592186405266 1068 17592186406173 17592186405015] [17592186405266 1068 17592186406221 17592186405015] [17592186405266 1068 17592186406246 17592186405015] [17592186405266 1068 17592186406270 17592186405015] [17592186405266 1068 17592186406304 17592186405015] [17592186405266 1071 "Source question: 2, PDF page 3.\n\nLet $\\mathbf F=\\langle x,y\\rangle$, $\\mathbf G=\\langle-y,x\\rangle$, and $\\mathbf H=\\langle x,-y\\rangle$. Match plots A–F to (a)–(f). Some plots are scaled for clarity.\n\n![Vector field plots A–F](images/ba/bab4c1e1ab7751751726e766e5fadef2c28e3dce5665f44345eb3315c65355cd.png)\n\n(a) $-\\mathbf G$; (b) $\\mathbf G-\\mathbf F$; (c) $\\mathbf F+\\mathbf H$; (d) $\\mathbf G+\\mathbf H$; (e) $\\mathbf H$; (f) $\\mathbf G+\\mathbf F$.\n\nPlot A: {{plot-a}}.\n\nPlot B: {{plot-b}}.\n\nPlot C: {{plot-c}}.\n\nPlot D: {{plot-d}}.\n\nPlot E: {{plot-e}}.\n\nPlot F: {{plot-f}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405266 1072 #uuid "45d8f0f6-281d-501a-8a32-10aa83f6f4de" 17592186405038] [17592186405266 1074 "A–e, B–f, C–c, D–d, E–b, F–a. Compute components: $-G=(y,-x)$ is clockwise; $G-F=(-x-y,x-y)$ rotates inward; $F+H=(2x,0)$ is horizontal; $G+H=(x-y,x-y)$ has equal components; $H=(x,-y)$ is a saddle; $G+F=(x-y,x+y)$ rotates outward. Check signs on each axis to distinguish B from E." 17592186405015] [17592186405267 1068 17592186406242 17592186405015] [17592186405267 1071 "**(b)** Evaluate $\\int_C3x\\,dx+8y\\,dy$ along a piecewise smooth curve from (1,3) to (2,5). Integral: {{integral}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405267 1072 #uuid "46150f7a-2a95-5436-bf30-8755f3888245" 17592186405038] [17592186405267 1074 "Use the potential from part (a). The final value (without K) is 6+100=106; the initial value is 3/2+36=75/2. Their difference is 137/2." 17592186405015] [17592186405268 1058 17592186405343 17592186405038] [17592186405268 1062 #uuid "46151900-dd0e-5341-a33c-31f08e2e81fb" 17592186405038] [17592186405268 1063 17592186405272 17592186405038] [17592186405269 1089 17592186048314 17592186405015] [17592186405269 1089 17592186050875 17592186405015] [17592186405269 1090 17592186405623 17592186405038] [17592186405269 1091 #uuid "465bf1fc-94b3-5db7-987a-e65e026f34f5" 17592186405038] [17592186405270 1068 17592186406249 17592186405015] [17592186405270 1071 "Source question: 5.\n\n**Question 5**\n\nChoose the graph of $\\mathbf F(x,y)=4\\mathbf i$." 17592186405015] [17592186405270 1072 #uuid "46c33d4e-0487-5f1a-b473-1aca40b3d31c" 17592186405038] [17592186405270 1074 "The vector $4\\mathbf i=\\langle4,0\\rangle$ is independent of position. Every arrow has the same length and points right." 17592186405015] [17592186405271 1043 17592186405055 17592186405038] [17592186405271 1043 17592186405154 17592186405038] [17592186405271 1043 17592186405178 17592186405038] [17592186405271 1043 17592186405192 17592186405038] [17592186405271 1043 17592186405200 17592186405038] [17592186405271 1043 17592186405241 17592186405038] [17592186405271 1043 17592186405296 17592186405038] [17592186405271 1043 17592186405310 17592186405038] [17592186405271 1043 17592186405337 17592186405038] [17592186405271 1043 17592186405357 17592186405038] [17592186405271 1043 17592186405359 17592186405038] [17592186405271 1043 17592186405407 17592186405038] [17592186405271 1043 17592186405434 17592186405038] [17592186405271 1043 17592186405451 17592186405038] [17592186405271 1043 17592186405466 17592186405038] [17592186405271 1043 17592186405498 17592186405038] [17592186405271 1043 17592186405513 17592186405038] [17592186405271 1043 17592186405584 17592186405038] [17592186405271 1043 17592186405695 17592186405038] [17592186405271 1043 17592186405704 17592186405038] [17592186405271 1043 17592186405715 17592186405038] [17592186405271 1043 17592186405784 17592186405038] [17592186405271 1043 17592186405790 17592186405038] [17592186405271 1043 17592186405819 17592186405038] [17592186405271 1043 17592186405836 17592186405038] [17592186405271 1044 17592186045461 17592186405038] [17592186405271 1045 "MTH 255 — OHW-4" 17592186405038] [17592186405271 1046 17592186405192 17592186405038] [17592186405271 1047 "2026-10-16T06:59:59.000Z" 17592186405038] [17592186405271 1048 17592186405043 17592186405038] [17592186405271 1053 #uuid "47b13091-547c-5f42-be3a-5a03d0ded7d2" 17592186405038] [17592186405272 1089 17592186049492 17592186405015] [17592186405272 1089 17592186051090 17592186405015] [17592186405272 1090 17592186405078 17592186405038] [17592186405272 1091 #uuid "47bd3318-94e3-558f-8129-3f9f98974de4" 17592186405038] [17592186405273 1089 17592186048960 17592186405015] [17592186405273 1089 17592186049859 17592186405015] [17592186405273 1090 17592186405648 17592186405038] [17592186405273 1091 #uuid "47c908f7-3f33-59b0-bb25-8c8778d91b76" 17592186405038] [17592186405274 1058 17592186405503 17592186405038] [17592186405274 1062 #uuid "47f845ae-462c-5d63-929d-d246cffaf3f1" 17592186405038] [17592186405274 1063 17592186405646 17592186405038] [17592186405275 1058 17592186405146 17592186405038] [17592186405275 1062 #uuid "47fc78a6-53cb-5dc4-a296-8373dbc8c6c1" 17592186405038] [17592186405275 1063 17592186405510 17592186405038] [17592186405276 1058 17592186405280 17592186405038] [17592186405276 1062 #uuid "48077779-7829-5fbe-92f8-442d8151aa88" 17592186405038] [17592186405276 1063 17592186405666 17592186405038] [17592186405277 1058 17592186405367 17592186405038] [17592186405277 1062 #uuid "486db1f7-1b40-52b7-88d7-53ae04f0c008" 17592186405038] [17592186405277 1063 17592186405444 17592186405038] [17592186405278 1068 17592186405970 17592186405015] [17592186405278 1071 "Source question: 3.\n\nFind $\\int_C3x\\,ds$ for $x=3t,\\ y=7t^2,\\ 0\\le t\\le1$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405278 1072 #uuid "488b7fed-19aa-5c2b-b3a5-3a41cf3c7eb3" 17592186405038] [17592186405278 1074 "$ds=\\sqrt{9+196t^2}dt$. Integrate $9t\\sqrt{9+196t^2}$ using u=9+196t², du=392t dt; the antiderivative is $3(9+196t^2)^{3/2}/196$." 17592186405015] [17592186405279 1068 17592186405880 17592186405015] [17592186405279 1071 "Source question: 21.\n\nFind the work of $\\mathbf F=\\langle y^3,x^3\\rangle$ along $\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle6\\cos t,8\\sin t\\rangle$ from t=0 to t=2π.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405279 1072 #uuid "49201a35-4468-5c00-89e7-4a9d1d683cae" 17592186405038] [17592186405279 1074 "The dot product is $-3072\\sin^4t+1728\\cos^4t$. Both fourth-power integrals over 0 to 2π equal 3π/4, giving $(1728-3072)3\\pi/4=-1008\\pi$." 17592186405015] [17592186405280 1058 17592186405621 17592186405038] [17592186405280 1062 #uuid "49942951-fa67-5fd2-8423-82b6deacbda0" 17592186405038] [17592186405280 1063 17592186405548 17592186405038] [17592186405281 1068 17592186405945 17592186405015] [17592186405281 1071 "**Question 5 — Part A**\n\nPoints with horizontal tangents: {{field-1}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405281 1072 #uuid "4a2a25d3-ef81-553c-aa1d-87f5b06922fb" 17592186405038] [17592186405281 1074 "Here $x'=10\\sin t$ and $y'=-3\\cos t$. Horizontal tangents occur at $t=\\pi/2,3\\pi/2$, where $y'=0$ and $x'\\ne0$, giving $(10,0)$ and $(10,6)$." 17592186405015] [17592186405282 1068 17592186405856 17592186405015] [17592186405282 1071 "**Question 1 — Part C**\n\nCompute the following derivative ($\\exp(x)=e^x$):\n\n$$\n\\frac{d}{dx}\\exp(x^2).\n$$\n\nDerivative: {{field-1}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405282 1072 #uuid "4a520d04-73da-5ea2-9040-ef0ef4ce8ef6" 17592186405038] [17592186405282 1074 "For $e^{u(x)}$, the derivative is $e^{u(x)}u\\prime(x)$. Here $u=x^2$ and $u\\prime=2x$." 17592186405015] [17592186405283 1058 17592186405766 17592186405038] [17592186405283 1062 #uuid "4a58d696-61d2-5b7a-9601-2438620bbaa3" 17592186405038] [17592186405283 1063 17592186405123 17592186405038] [17592186405284 1068 17592186405857 17592186405015] [17592186405284 1071 "Source question: 6.\n\n**Question 6**\n\nChoose the graph of $\\mathbf F(x,y)=-2\\mathbf i+3\\mathbf j$." 17592186405015] [17592186405284 1072 #uuid "4a95b981-b16a-58af-bca1-201ef10e1632" 17592186405038] [17592186405284 1074 "The constant vector $\\langle-2,3\\rangle$ points left and up, with vertical magnitude larger than horizontal magnitude. All arrows agree in direction and length." 17592186405015] [17592186405285 1089 17592186048575 17592186405015] [17592186405285 1089 17592186049487 17592186405015] [17592186405285 1089 17592186050614 17592186405015] [17592186405285 1090 17592186405214 17592186405038] [17592186405285 1091 #uuid "4b0fa278-e621-5acd-855b-bea93a9113cf" 17592186405038] [17592186405286 1089 17592186048314 17592186405015] [17592186405286 1089 17592186050149 17592186405015] [17592186405286 1089 17592186050875 17592186405015] [17592186405286 1090 17592186405490 17592186405038] [17592186405286 1091 #uuid "4bd5ee04-799e-555f-b62d-0a7f7918f84a" 17592186405038] [17592186405287 1058 17592186405190 17592186405038] [17592186405287 1062 #uuid "4bf4ac62-5d84-5346-8986-8737ae4017d9" 17592186405038] [17592186405287 1063 17592186405057 17592186405038] [17592186405288 1085 17592186405074 17592186405038] [17592186405288 1085 17592186405437 17592186405038] [17592186405288 1086 "Source question: 11.\n\nLet $\\mathbf F=\\langle7x,3y,20z+11x\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf G=\\langle19x,5y,6z+5y\\rangle$." 17592186405038] [17592186405288 1087 #uuid "4cfa5b10-9ece-55db-b3b1-f2cc189a863c" 17592186405038] [17592186405288 1088 17592186405437 17592186405038] [17592186405289 1058 17592186405491 17592186405038] [17592186405289 1062 #uuid "4d0d5766-90e9-59f4-b56d-1d3c73f3ed6d" 17592186405038] [17592186405289 1063 17592186405735 17592186405038] [17592186405290 1089 17592186050573 17592186405015] [17592186405290 1090 17592186405459 17592186405038] [17592186405290 1091 #uuid "4d51339a-cc1f-53f3-9331-eb933dc2a018" 17592186405038] [17592186405291 1068 17592186406240 17592186405015] [17592186405291 1071 "Source question: 23.\n\nFind the work of $\\mathbf F=\\langle44x,6y,3\\rangle$ along $\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle\\sin t,\\cos t,t\\rangle$, $0\\le t\\le\\pi$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405291 1072 #uuid "4d8e6cfe-a28a-5ec6-9c58-e1021e8c8c3d" 17592186405038] [17592186405291 1074 "The dot product is $38\\sin t\\cos t+3$. The trigonometric term integrates to zero over 0 to π. Alternatively use the potential $22x^2+3y^2+3z$." 17592186405015] [17592186405292 1068 17592186405917 17592186405015] [17592186405292 1071 "Source question: 3.\n\nFind $\\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf F$ for $\\mathbf F=\\langle\\sqrt{4x^2+5y^2},\\sqrt{4x^2+5y^2},2z\\rangle$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405292 1072 #uuid "4de38b69-fc49-5414-ba38-3e44e52ed23d" 17592186405038] [17592186405292 1074 "The chain rule gives P_x=4x/√(4x²+5y²) and Q_y=5y/√(4x²+5y²); R_z=2. The formula applies when (x,y)≠(0,0). The required partial derivatives fail to exist on the z-axis, so the divergence is undefined there." 17592186405015] [17592186405293 1068 17592186405842 17592186405015] [17592186405293 1071 "**(a)** Find the unique two-dimensional field that is undefined at (3,2), unit everywhere else, and points toward (3,2).\n\n$\\mathbf F(x,y)=$ {{field}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405293 1072 #uuid "4e6407c6-2c27-58f3-aad5-e22d8eba63d8" 17592186405038] [17592186405293 1074 "The displacement toward (3,2) is $(3-x,2-y)$. Divide by its positive length. The denominator vanishes exactly at (3,2), and the resulting magnitude is 1." 17592186405015] [17592186405294 1089 17592186048239 17592186405015] [17592186405294 1089 17592186048923 17592186405015] [17592186405294 1089 17592186049721 17592186405015] [17592186405294 1090 17592186405749 17592186405038] [17592186405294 1091 #uuid "4ed75a27-f552-5b95-a2a6-0be6b8193c2c" 17592186405038] [17592186405295 1068 17592186406131 17592186405015] [17592186405295 1071 "Source question: 2.\n\nFind the divergence of $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)=\\langle8x,3xy,6xyz^5\\rangle$. \n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405295 1072 #uuid "4eff6f77-e744-536b-9f24-4fc46d8a0aa5" 17592186405038] [17592186405295 1074 "$P_x=8$, $Q_y=3x$, $R_z=30xyz^4$. Hold the other coordinates fixed in each partial derivative." 17592186405015] [17592186405296 1058 17592186405359 17592186405038] [17592186405296 1062 #uuid "4f04d52c-ffa9-5588-a3c5-bfb61a877ed7" 17592186405038] [17592186405296 1063 17592186405418 17592186405038] [17592186405297 1058 17592186405474 17592186405038] [17592186405297 1062 #uuid "4f0dbf9f-588f-5d07-8e0a-dee2f95b7682" 17592186405038] [17592186405297 1063 17592186405624 17592186405038] [17592186405298 1058 17592186405351 17592186405038] [17592186405298 1062 #uuid "4f2a3008-2156-556f-9ba1-d0ce4b2b1e7c" 17592186405038] [17592186405298 1063 17592186405641 17592186405038] [17592186405299 1089 17592186048641 17592186405015] [17592186405299 1089 17592186050037 17592186405015] [17592186405299 1090 17592186405318 17592186405038] [17592186405299 1091 #uuid "4f5bdf7b-34f1-5e2c-80ae-bdeaf78ef45f" 17592186405038] [17592186405300 1064 "Source: [MTH255-WHW-Week1_v1.pdf](/api/source?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F255%2FWHW-1%2FMTH255-WHW-Week1_v1.pdf)\nDue: 2026-10-01\n\n## Instructions\n\nShow clear, organized work or a written explanation for every problem where required. Some problems are graded for correctness; an answer without supporting work does not earn full credit.\n\nYou may write on a printed copy, annotate the PDF digitally, or use your own paper with the same problem layout. Submit one seven-page PDF to **Gradescope → Written Homework 1**: the cover page followed by one page per problem, including blank problems. If a solution uses several sheets, combine them into one page for that problem. The source warns that submissions in the wrong format receive zero credit. See the linked PDF for the complete instructions.\n\nStudy answers, explanations, and reference graphics are authored additions to the linked source." 17592186405015] [17592186405300 1066 #uuid "4f8c08b2-5695-5a91-88a9-a89a3a427871" 17592186405038] [17592186405300 1067 "Assignment information" 17592186405038] [17592186405301 1089 17592186048960 17592186405015] [17592186405301 1089 17592186049512 17592186405015] [17592186405301 1089 17592186049517 17592186405015] [17592186405301 1089 17592186050033 17592186405015] [17592186405301 1090 17592186405438 17592186405038] [17592186405301 1091 #uuid "4fc001b4-4f6d-5efc-82e8-917c8b65fa94" 17592186405038] [17592186405302 1062 #uuid "500a960b-d094-5e4d-837f-39eddffd5599" 17592186405038] [17592186405302 1063 17592186405088 17592186405038] [17592186405303 1089 17592186048299 17592186405015] [17592186405303 1089 17592186049494 17592186405015] [17592186405303 1089 17592186049576 17592186405015] [17592186405303 1089 17592186049878 17592186405015] [17592186405303 1089 17592186050728 17592186405015] [17592186405303 1090 17592186405564 17592186405038] [17592186405303 1091 #uuid "50e8174c-9e06-599d-8418-295525af1404" 17592186405038] [17592186405304 1089 17592186048191 17592186405015] [17592186405304 1090 17592186405694 17592186405038] [17592186405304 1091 #uuid "5146e3e9-3ab5-5080-aee4-539ec2512013" 17592186405038] [17592186405305 1058 17592186405262 17592186405038] [17592186405305 1062 #uuid "51c9fa1e-215a-5565-8e55-93367e686758" 17592186405038] [17592186405305 1063 17592186405135 17592186405038] [17592186405306 1058 17592186405569 17592186405038] [17592186405306 1062 #uuid "51d79b89-5ff3-5106-aaa4-183df5ec38b5" 17592186405038] [17592186405306 1063 17592186405391 17592186405038] [17592186405307 1068 17592186406099 17592186405015] [17592186405307 1071 "Source question: 6.\n\nFind the curl of $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)=\\langle6x,-2y,-7z\\rangle$. Enter the vector in angle-bracket form.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405307 1072 #uuid "5247068c-f916-57ea-9e1f-cfa2ca5d4597" 17592186405038] [17592186405307 1074 "Each component depends only on its own coordinate. Every cross derivative in the curl formula is zero. Return the zero vector, not a scalar." 17592186405015] [17592186405308 1068 17592186406001 17592186405015] [17592186405308 1071 "**Question 9 — Part B**\n\nChoose the graph of the spiral over the stated interval." 17592186405015] [17592186405308 1072 #uuid "5298eb2a-c650-56b2-8ea2-196e65587270" 17592186405038] [17592186405308 1074 "In polar form, $r=t$ and $\\theta=t$. The spiral starts at the origin, moves counterclockwise, and ends at $(-5\\pi,0)$ after $2.5$ turns. At $t=9\\pi/2$ it crosses the positive $y$-axis near $14.14$, matching this graph." 17592186405015] [17592186405309 1064 "**6.1 Vector Fields**\n\nSource: [Macmillan Achieve](https://achieve.macmillanlearning.com/ihub/assessment/f188d950-dd73-11e0-9572-0800200c9a66/5a1db2a7-4295-4a4c-9dbb-ceb8b174a282/652f6185-14b0-408e-92fc-5cf7a73767b4?actualCourseId=5a1db2a7-4295-4a4c-9dbb-ceb8b174a282)\nDue: 2026-10-08\nImported: 2026-10-01 · 12 questions\n\nStudy answers, explanations, and reference graphics are authored additions to the linked source." 17592186405015] [17592186405309 1066 #uuid "53343a60-4e64-5900-95b7-4e118122dfc6" 17592186405038] [17592186405309 1067 "Assignment information" 17592186405038] [17592186405310 1058 17592186405784 17592186405038] [17592186405310 1062 #uuid "53dcad96-a249-584d-acca-2fadcae990ba" 17592186405038] [17592186405310 1063 17592186405398 17592186405038] [17592186405311 1068 17592186405869 17592186405015] [17592186405311 1071 "**Question 6 — Part C**\n\nAlternatively, compute the cross product below. What does the result tell you about $\\nabla\\varphi$ and the tangent plane?\n\n$\\langle\\varphi_z,0,-\\varphi_x\\rangle\\times\\langle0,\\varphi_z,-\\varphi_y\\rangle=$ {{field-1}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405311 1072 #uuid "54030489-7b70-5752-923e-1d1ccfff7638" 17592186405038] [17592186405311 1074 "The cross product is $\\langle\\varphi_x\\varphi_z,\\varphi_y\\varphi_z,\\varphi_z^2\\rangle=\\varphi_z\\nabla\\varphi$. A cross product of two independent tangent vectors is normal to their plane. For $\\varphi_z\\ne0$, it is a nonzero scalar multiple of the gradient, so the gradient is also normal. If $\\varphi_z=0$, these particular vectors do not establish the normal; a different local coordinate choice is needed at a regular point." 17592186405015] [17592186405312 1068 17592186406287 17592186405015] [17592186405312 1071 "Source question: 6.\n\nUse Stokes’ theorem to find $\\iint_S(\\nabla\\times\\mathbf F)\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,dS$, where $\\mathbf F=\\langle3z,8x,9y\\rangle$ and S is the paraboloid cap $z=6-x^2-y^2$, z≥3. **Source ambiguity:** No normal direction is given; use the upward normal for this study answer.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405312 1072 #uuid "550d2871-2ff9-50f1-976f-5226181d2091" 17592186405038] [17592186405312 1074 "The boundary is the circle x²+y²=3 at z=3. Replace the cap by the upward-oriented disk with the same boundary. The curl is (9,3,8), so its flux through the disk is 8 times its area, 8·3π=24π. A downward normal gives −24π. If “surface enclosed” were intended to include a closing base as well, the total curl flux of that closed surface would be 0." 17592186405015] [17592186405313 1089 17592186048314 17592186405015] [17592186405313 1089 17592186050149 17592186405015] [17592186405313 1090 17592186405462 17592186405038] [17592186405313 1091 #uuid "556e1fb8-43a0-571a-a865-1040ea79a200" 17592186405038] [17592186405314 1068 17592186406175 17592186405015] [17592186405314 1071 "Source question: 5.\n\nUse the divergence theorem to find the outward flux of $\\mathbf F=\\langle2x-6y,9y-6z,7x-y\\rangle$ through the cube with faces x=±2, y=±2, z=±2.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405314 1072 #uuid "56055d00-5395-54ac-a58d-32197cc65912" 17592186405038] [17592186405314 1074 "Divergence is 2+9+0=11. The cube has side length 4, not 2, and volume 64. Thus the flux is 11·64=704." 17592186405015] [17592186405315 1068 17592186406190 17592186405015] [17592186405315 1071 "**(b)** C consists of the line segment(s) joining $(8,\\pi/3)\\to(9,\\pi)$, in that order.\n\nIntegral: {{integral}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405315 1072 #uuid "569f1f93-9d97-5c63-a310-de3dff6860ee" 17592186405038] [17592186405315 1074 "The potential is $f=e^x\\cos y$. All three paths have the same endpoints. Thus $f(9,\\pi)-f(8,\\pi/3)=-e^9-e^8/2$. Differentiating f verifies the two coefficients of the differential." 17592186405015] [17592186405316 1085 17592186405257 17592186405038] [17592186405316 1085 17592186405489 17592186405038] [17592186405316 1085 17592186405516 17592186405038] [17592186405316 1085 17592186405658 17592186405038] [17592186405316 1086 "Source question: 2.\n\nConsider the space curve\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle x(t),y(t),z(t)\\rangle\n=\\langle 3t,4t,5\\ln\\sec t\\rangle.\n$$" 17592186405038] [17592186405316 1087 #uuid "5729a5c0-155c-5d48-8a99-c052c7c857f4" 17592186405038] [17592186405316 1088 17592186405516 17592186405038] [17592186405317 1068 17592186406107 17592186405015] [17592186405317 1071 "Source question: 5.\n\n**Question 5**\n\nEliminate $t$ from $x(t)=8\\sin t$, $y(t)=5\\cos t$, $0\\le t\\le2\\pi$. Enter an exact rectangular equation: {{field-1}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405317 1072 #uuid "574c4856-8c8e-5211-9580-de409f2cb2a5" 17592186405038] [17592186405317 1074 "Divide the coordinates by their amplitudes: $x/8=\\sin t$ and $y/5=\\cos t$. Squaring and adding gives $x^2/64+y^2/25=1$." 17592186405015] [17592186405318 1071 "Source question: 5.\n\n**Question 5**\n\nThe temperature in a room is given by the function\n\n$$\nw=f(x,y,z)=x^2+y^2+z^2.\n$$\n\nThere is a sphere in the room with parameterization\n\n$$\n\\langle x,y,z\\rangle\n=\\langle\\rho\\sin\\phi\\cos\\theta,\\rho\\sin\\phi\\sin\\theta,\\rho\\cos\\phi\\rangle.\n$$\n\nUse chain rule to find $\\dfrac{\\partial w}{\\partial\\rho}$, $\\dfrac{\\partial w}{\\partial\\phi}$, and $\\dfrac{\\partial w}{\\partial\\theta}$. Reflect on whether your answers make sense." 17592186405038] [17592186405318 1072 #uuid "58dee5b5-8a4b-592d-bde6-a58086d626eb" 17592186405038] [17592186405318 1074 "With $\\mathbf r=\\langle\\rho\\sin\\phi\\cos\\theta,\\rho\\sin\\phi\\sin\\theta,\\rho\\cos\\phi\\rangle$, the chain rule gives $w_q=2\\mathbf r\\cdot\\mathbf r_q$. Thus $w_\\rho=2\\rho(\\sin^2\\phi\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\phi\\sin^2\\theta+\\cos^2\\phi)=2\\rho$. The terms in $w_\\phi=2\\rho^2\\sin\\phi\\cos\\phi(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta-1)$ cancel, and the terms in $w_\\theta=2\\rho^2\\sin^2\\phi(-\\cos\\theta\\sin\\theta+\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta)$ cancel. Therefore $w_\\rho=2\\rho$, $w_\\phi=0$, and $w_\\theta=0$. This agrees with $w=\\rho^2$: temperature depends only on distance from the origin.\n\nCompute the coordinate derivatives before taking each dot product. At fixed radius, moving in either angular direction stays on the same sphere and cannot change $x^2+y^2+z^2$." 17592186405015] [17592186405319 1089 17592186049465 17592186405015] [17592186405319 1089 17592186050540 17592186405015] [17592186405319 1090 17592186405236 17592186405038] [17592186405319 1091 #uuid "58fb2e0a-c06b-59e0-9660-f2fef42941d5" 17592186405038] [17592186405320 1058 17592186405487 17592186405038] [17592186405320 1062 #uuid "59466ced-3069-5773-8ffc-657db1de63f5" 17592186405038] [17592186405320 1063 17592186405612 17592186405038] [17592186405321 1068 17592186405991 17592186405015] [17592186405321 1071 "[Open in WeBWorK](https://webwork2.science.oregonstate.edu/webwork2/MTH_256_020_F2026/WebWork_1/2)\n\n**Question 2**\n\nSolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}-y=3e^t+36e^{5t}\n$$\n\nwith $y(0)=4$.\n\n$y(t)=$ {{field-1}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405321 1072 #uuid "599b693d-6e02-5377-bef6-014fa6bb3f8c" 17592186405038] [17592186405321 1074 "Multiply by $e^{-t}$: $(e^{-t}y)\\prime=3+36e^{4t}$. Thus $e^{-t}y=3t+9e^{4t}+C$. At $0$, $4=9+C$, so $C=-5$. The resonant $3e^t$ forcing produces the $3te^t$ term." 17592186405015] [17592186405322 1058 17592186405661 17592186405038] [17592186405322 1062 #uuid "59a316f0-0216-5d07-9d60-b5c6d493c061" 17592186405038] [17592186405322 1063 17592186405771 17592186405038] [17592186405323 1058 17592186405738 17592186405038] [17592186405323 1062 #uuid "59ec431f-6761-599b-ab37-cdbc022279fe" 17592186405038] [17592186405323 1063 17592186405147 17592186405038] [17592186405324 1089 17592186051091 17592186405015] [17592186405324 1090 17592186405652 17592186405038] [17592186405324 1091 #uuid "5a1d8c22-269a-5902-bc22-58d19093d606" 17592186405038] [17592186405325 1068 17592186405977 17592186405015] [17592186405325 1071 "Source question: 1.\n\n**Question 1**\n\nWhich type of curve is described by $x(t)=9\\sin t$ and $y(t)=11\\cos t$?" 17592186405015] [17592186405325 1072 #uuid "5a8ce0ff-66bf-5c93-9779-a5ae2f4be25e" 17592186405038] [17592186405325 1074 "Divide by the amplitudes and use $\\sin^2t+\\cos^2t=1$: $x^2/81+y^2/121=1$. Unequal positive semiaxes give an ellipse." 17592186405015] [17592186405326 1058 17592186405502 17592186405038] [17592186405326 1062 #uuid "5abfc990-eafe-5224-9b65-e278e6119057" 17592186405038] [17592186405326 1063 17592186405785 17592186405038] [17592186405327 1089 17592186048239 17592186405015] [17592186405327 1089 17592186048424 17592186405015] [17592186405327 1089 17592186051090 17592186405015] [17592186405327 1090 17592186405316 17592186405038] [17592186405327 1091 #uuid "5b19ab97-b57a-5a0a-a0d6-25b4a8bea932" 17592186405038] [17592186405328 1058 17592186405334 17592186405038] [17592186405328 1062 #uuid "5b7936e7-c49a-5870-b96c-740872fa0933" 17592186405038] [17592186405328 1063 17592186405176 17592186405038] [17592186405329 1068 17592186406238 17592186405015] [17592186405329 1071 "Source question: 7.\n\nThe field $\\mathbf F=\\langle6x-5y+2,9-5x\\rangle$ is conservative. Find a potential f. Use K for the arbitrary constant and absorb into K as much as possible.\n\n$f(x,y)=$ {{potential}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405329 1072 #uuid "5baa9f7a-2863-5acb-9cd7-97395d050c8c" 17592186405038] [17592186405329 1074 "Integrate $f_x=6x-5y+2$ to get $3x^2-5xy+2x+g(y)$. Matching $f_y=9-5x$ gives g′=9, hence g=9y+K. Differentiating both components verifies the result." 17592186405015] [17592186405330 1058 17592186405700 17592186405038] [17592186405330 1062 #uuid "5bdd3bcc-030e-54b3-a3f5-1ff76279f0c5" 17592186405038] [17592186405330 1063 17592186405086 17592186405038] [17592186405331 1058 17592186405552 17592186405038] [17592186405331 1062 #uuid "5cc7a0e4-d4c0-5cd8-840c-41c7c6214eb6" 17592186405038] [17592186405331 1063 17592186405313 17592186405038] [17592186405332 1062 #uuid "5d2833ea-bdc5-5197-aaa7-5dd6c12a331c" 17592186405038] [17592186405332 1063 17592186405665 17592186405038] [17592186405333 1068 17592186406016 17592186405015] [17592186405333 1071 "Source question: 11.\n\nLet $\\mathbf F=\\langle x+y,3x-z,y\\rangle$ and let S be $z+2x+2y=4$ in the first octant. Find its outward flux $\\iint_S\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,dS$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405333 1072 #uuid "5d6a40a2-5a20-57b3-b9db-6cdb825d7b5f" 17592186405038] [17592186405333 1074 "Outward from the first-octant tetrahedron gives vector area (2,2,1)dxdy on the plane. Substituting z=4−2x−2y yields F·(2,2,1)=12x+7y−8. On x≥0,y≥0,x+y≤2, area=2 and both first moments equal 4/3. Thus flux =12·4/3+7·4/3−16=28/3." 17592186405015] [17592186405334 1062 #uuid "5dd3c3ed-0489-56be-a773-8eb8a944c187" 17592186405038] [17592186405334 1063 17592186405084 17592186405038] [17592186405335 1089 17592186048299 17592186405015] [17592186405335 1089 17592186049494 17592186405015] [17592186405335 1089 17592186049576 17592186405015] [17592186405335 1089 17592186049878 17592186405015] [17592186405335 1089 17592186050728 17592186405015] [17592186405335 1090 17592186405467 17592186405038] [17592186405335 1091 #uuid "5e134656-0c79-5475-9378-0981d3b69322" 17592186405038] [17592186405336 1089 17592186048546 17592186405015] [17592186405336 1090 17592186405588 17592186405038] [17592186405336 1091 #uuid "5e53c922-4405-550e-b492-5530f1f6f7c1" 17592186405038] [17592186405337 1058 17592186405466 17592186405038] [17592186405337 1062 #uuid "5f39c2c0-b106-539a-b206-71775d02cea8" 17592186405038] [17592186405337 1063 17592186405763 17592186405038] [17592186405338 1085 17592186405381 17592186405038] [17592186405338 1085 17592186405718 17592186405038] [17592186405338 1086 "Source question: 5.\n\nFor $x(t)=10-10\\cos t$ and $y(t)=3-3\\sin t$, with $0\\le t\\le2\\pi$, find all points with horizontal or vertical tangents. Give exact coordinate pairs as a comma-separated list; enter `DNE` if there are none." 17592186405038] [17592186405338 1087 #uuid "5f6cb1d9-eb04-51e3-b9ee-bb77c57f7859" 17592186405038] [17592186405338 1088 17592186405381 17592186405038] [17592186405339 1058 17592186405448 17592186405038] [17592186405339 1062 #uuid "5f798a24-13ed-59a4-9c49-a60a44419cef" 17592186405038] [17592186405339 1063 17592186405737 17592186405038] [17592186405340 1085 17592186405047 17592186405038] [17592186405340 1085 17592186405186 17592186405038] [17592186405340 1085 17592186405757 17592186405038] [17592186405340 1086 "Source question: 4.\n\nOn the provided direction field sketch and label the following:\n\n![Direction field provided for Problem 4](images/05/05f804c32b466c20fed261a3932b5748cde3835c787f3a96bfb9aa9e4b9456fc.png)" 17592186405038] [17592186405340 1087 #uuid "5f910c0c-f925-5be8-83e1-32b240647491" 17592186405038] [17592186405340 1088 17592186405186 17592186405038] [17592186405341 1062 #uuid "6007e7ac-8aed-5242-acb2-28d44badf486" 17592186405038] [17592186405341 1063 17592186405067 17592186405038] [17592186405342 1089 17592186050295 17592186405015] [17592186405342 1089 17592186050866 17592186405015] [17592186405342 1090 17592186405405 17592186405038] [17592186405342 1091 #uuid "60617532-73d6-5e5a-831c-45d4495270e4" 17592186405038] [17592186405343 1058 17592186405289 17592186405038] [17592186405343 1062 #uuid "60e34426-45e0-525b-9681-9a7023e94329" 17592186405038] [17592186405343 1063 17592186405182 17592186405038] [17592186405344 1058 17592186405372 17592186405038] [17592186405344 1062 #uuid "612a167a-8271-5b89-a939-59276995958c" 17592186405038] [17592186405344 1063 17592186405720 17592186405038] [17592186405345 1071 "**Question 3 — Part C**\n\nExplain the geometric significance of: An infinite number of solutions." 17592186405038] [17592186405345 1072 #uuid "61b02812-4719-5d6e-aaba-a96faba07692" 17592186405038] [17592186405345 1074 "All $k$ lines coincide: they describe the same geometric line. Every point on that line satisfies every equation, so there are infinitely many solutions.\n\nTwo distinct lines can share at most one point. Therefore infinitely many common points require every equation to describe the same line. Distinct parallel lines have no common points, so being parallel is not enough." 17592186405015] [17592186405346 1071 "**Question 5 — Part A**\n\nDifferentiate $c=f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $x$, using the chain rule on the right. Conclude that $\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}=-\\dfrac{f_x}{f_z}$ and, similarly, $\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}=-\\dfrac{f_y}{f_z}$." 17592186405038] [17592186405346 1072 #uuid "62269627-33f0-548a-bd98-964ed5e629d0" 17592186405038] [17592186405346 1074 "Holding $y$ fixed gives $0=f_x(1)+f_y(0)+f_z z_x=f_x+f_z z_x$. Thus $z_x=-f_x/f_z$ if $f_z\\ne0$. Holding $x$ fixed similarly gives $0=f_x(0)+f_y(1)+f_z z_y$, hence $z_y=-f_y/f_z$.\n\nWhen differentiating with respect to $x$, the independent coordinate $y$ stays fixed but the dependent coordinate $z$ changes. Keep the $f_z z_x$ chain-rule term. The formulas require $f_z\\ne0$, the condition for solving locally for $z(x,y)$." 17592186405015] [17592186405347 1089 17592186049465 17592186405015] [17592186405347 1090 17592186405155 17592186405038] [17592186405347 1091 #uuid "62c7cb1a-bf92-5f6d-8441-46b657c9ad66" 17592186405038] [17592186405348 1068 17592186406074 17592186405015] [17592186405348 1071 "Source question: 10.\n\n**Question 10**\n\nFind the distance traveled along $x(t)=3\\cos t$, $y(t)=3\\sin t$ during $0\\le t\\le2\\pi$. Give an exact answer.\n\n$s=$ {{field-1}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405348 1072 #uuid "62ea1902-3f7a-5886-8f6a-f7c0d2a853a7" 17592186405038] [17592186405348 1074 "The particle has speed $\\sqrt{(-3\\sin t)^2+(3\\cos t)^2}=3$. Integrating for $2\\pi$ units of parameter gives $s=6\\pi$. The displacement is zero, but the distance traveled is one circumference." 17592186405015] [17592186405349 1058 17592186405800 17592186405038] [17592186405349 1062 #uuid "6325a316-192f-54f2-8602-53bc8c6281f5" 17592186405038] [17592186405349 1063 17592186405614 17592186405038] [17592186405350 1068 17592186406124 17592186405015] [17592186405350 1071 "**Question 2 — Part B**\n\n$y'(x)=\\sqrt{x},\\quad y(0)=4$.\n\n$y(x)=$ {{field-1}}, for $x\\ge0$." 17592186405015] [17592186405350 1072 #uuid "6356ed6a-ade3-5dff-93b8-239095fa922a" 17592186405038] [17592186405350 1074 "Integrate $x^{1/2}$ to get $(2/3)x^{3/2}+C$. The initial condition sets $C=4$. This real solution is defined for $x\\ge0$; its derivative at the endpoint is understood from the right." 17592186405015] [17592186405351 1058 17592186405274 17592186405038] [17592186405351 1062 #uuid "63ce76ac-4de7-580c-a2f5-398f2420a1e7" 17592186405038] [17592186405351 1063 17592186405523 17592186405038] [17592186405352 1058 17592186405461 17592186405038] [17592186405352 1062 #uuid "63d730f7-9bf6-5703-9edd-93ef0b6cae4a" 17592186405038] [17592186405352 1063 17592186405782 17592186405038] [17592186405353 1058 17592186405094 17592186405038] [17592186405353 1062 #uuid "63e33a13-7151-584b-9780-5cc216b61db6" 17592186405038] [17592186405353 1063 17592186405085 17592186405038] [17592186405354 1058 17592186405653 17592186405038] [17592186405354 1062 #uuid "6433d198-5ddf-5bc6-9a85-2eda89bba82b" 17592186405038] [17592186405354 1063 17592186405818 17592186405038] [17592186405355 1058 17592186405113 17592186405038] [17592186405355 1062 #uuid "644241c0-34f6-53ff-be4e-efed6f2f55f9" 17592186405038] [17592186405355 1063 17592186405526 17592186405038] [17592186405356 1058 17592186405677 17592186405038] [17592186405356 1062 #uuid "64c556e1-1dd2-5339-862f-ee4f0e7dc162" 17592186405038] [17592186405356 1063 17592186405684 17592186405038] [17592186405357 1058 17592186405296 17592186405038] [17592186405357 1062 #uuid "64ce8281-e715-58f3-b88d-65348b80e6b3" 17592186405038] [17592186405357 1063 17592186405823 17592186405038] [17592186405358 1089 17592186049465 17592186405015] [17592186405358 1090 17592186405810 17592186405038] [17592186405358 1091 #uuid "64e8fc06-fda9-527f-a6d6-8b8089ea0ae9" 17592186405038] [17592186405359 1062 #uuid "660e5620-f427-5e3b-81b9-600531711b6f" 17592186405038] [17592186405359 1063 17592186405111 17592186405038] [17592186405360 1058 17592186405363 17592186405038] [17592186405360 1062 #uuid "662e29a0-760d-51cc-9d4b-8ccba2cfa02c" 17592186405038] [17592186405360 1063 17592186405148 17592186405038] [17592186405361 1068 17592186405928 17592186405015] [17592186405361 1071 "**(c)** $\\mathbf F=\\langle-y,x\\rangle=-y\\mathbf i+x\\mathbf j$.\n\nPolar form: {{polar}}. Explain how the result agrees with the field plot." 17592186405015] [17592186405361 1072 #uuid "665e3941-44ad-5e1a-9a1b-b2b2a52b1ef8" 17592186405038] [17592186405361 1074 "**interpretation:** The radial coefficient $-y\\cos\\theta+x\\sin\\theta$ is 0; the angular coefficient $y\\sin\\theta+x\\cos\\theta$ is r. It circulates counterclockwise with length r.\n\nThe radial coefficient $-y\\cos\\theta+x\\sin\\theta$ is 0; the angular coefficient $y\\sin\\theta+x\\cos\\theta$ is r. It circulates counterclockwise with length r. Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$ and the basis identities from 4(b). Polar directions require r>0; in (a) and (c), the Cartesian field extends to the zero vector at the origin." 17592186405015] [17592186405362 1064 "**6.7 Stokes’ Theorem**\n\nDue: 2026-11-26\nSource: [Macmillan Achieve](https://achieve.macmillanlearning.com/ihub/assessment/f188d950-dd73-11e0-9572-0800200c9a66/5a1db2a7-4295-4a4c-9dbb-ceb8b174a282/d50b694c-c2bb-4457-ac38-dfafa8283ee5)\n\nStudy answers and explanations are authored additions, not publisher solutions.\n\n## Instructions\n\nUse exact symbolic notation, fractions, and component form or standard basis vectors as appropriate. Orientation assumptions are explicit where the source is incomplete. Source: Sullivan 2e Calculus, W.H. Freeman. Unlimited attempts, no incorrect-attempt penalty; up to 50 days late at a 2% daily penalty." 17592186405015] [17592186405362 1066 #uuid "665fee2b-46d6-532a-b0c2-7f31ecaa6fe5" 17592186405038] [17592186405362 1067 "Assignment information" 17592186405038] [17592186405363 1058 17592186405647 17592186405038] [17592186405363 1062 #uuid "66618ad8-d2c2-56b1-8b68-fcae77da031f" 17592186405038] [17592186405363 1063 17592186405232 17592186405038] [17592186405364 1062 #uuid "666ab9de-2eb0-59e8-ba62-b808e1a751c2" 17592186405038] [17592186405364 1063 17592186405805 17592186405038] [17592186405365 1058 17592186405741 17592186405038] [17592186405365 1062 #uuid "666e7924-3f82-55c7-a518-616ba4ea4bac" 17592186405038] [17592186405365 1063 17592186405063 17592186405038] [17592186405366 1068 17592186406113 17592186405015] [17592186405366 1071 "Source question: 7.\n\nFind $\\iint_R5x\\,dA$, where R is enclosed by $5x-y=0$, $5x-y=8$, $x+y=0$, $x+y=3$. Use $u=5x-y$, $v=x+y$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405366 1072 #uuid "66b4c714-c89d-531f-a0b4-9a94ac90d295" 17592186405038] [17592186405366 1074 "Invert: $x=(u+v)/6$, $y=(5v-u)/6$. The new rectangle is 0≤u≤8, 0≤v≤3, and $|J|=1/6$. Thus $I=\\frac5{36}\\int_0^8\\int_0^3(u+v)dvdu=\\frac5{36}(132)=55/3$." 17592186405015] [17592186405367 1058 17592186405468 17592186405038] [17592186405367 1062 #uuid "67080205-77f9-57c4-92b3-4dd6faded73f" 17592186405038] [17592186405367 1063 17592186405562 17592186405038] [17592186405368 1089 17592186048575 17592186405015] [17592186405368 1089 17592186049152 17592186405015] [17592186405368 1090 17592186405321 17592186405038] [17592186405368 1091 #uuid "67cf92ba-f020-52d8-8b9d-91ba3fffa472" 17592186405038] [17592186405369 1068 17592186406024 17592186405015] [17592186405369 1071 "**Question 3 — Part B**\n\nGive the domain in interval notation: {{field-1}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405369 1072 #uuid "67db9163-a31a-513e-b2a5-ee0b12e7a217" 17592186405038] [17592186405369 1074 "The increasing function $x=t-6$ maps the included endpoints $t=0,2$ to $x=-6,-4$. Thus $-6\\le x\\le-4$." 17592186405015] [17592186405370 1089 17592186048923 17592186405015] [17592186405370 1089 17592186050418 17592186405015] [17592186405370 1090 17592186405284 17592186405038] [17592186405370 1091 #uuid "685c1aca-2b98-53c2-90c9-48a9f57e87b1" 17592186405038] [17592186405371 1058 17592186405140 17592186405038] [17592186405371 1062 #uuid "689d6767-6ff7-5ce0-8c1f-dc595e2970bf" 17592186405038] [17592186405371 1063 17592186405120 17592186405038] [17592186405372 1062 #uuid "68b8b44a-9349-5d69-b47c-f0c524e51f67" 17592186405038] [17592186405372 1063 17592186405464 17592186405038] [17592186405373 1071 "**Question 3 — Part B**\n\nAn integral curve that is not a solution." 17592186405038] [17592186405373 1072 #uuid "69e45544-bf18-5e99-9dc5-8b465e7cc190" 17592186405038] [17592186405373 1074 "Using the worksheet’s geometric meaning of integral curve, draw the entire right branch $x^2-y^2=1/4$, $x>0$, including its turn at $(1/2,0)$. One parametrization is $(x,y)=(\\tfrac12\\cosh s,\\tfrac12\\sinh s)$. Draw its visible portion within the supplied axes. Away from $y=0$, its slope is $dy/dx=\\cosh s/\\sinh s=x/y$. The whole curve is not a solution graph $y(x)$: for $x>1/2$ it has two $y$ values, and the explicit ODE is undefined at its vertical tangent $(1/2,0)$.\n\n![Authored reference graph](images/1a/1a426ad2bc023da1fc079d9ca7017c8735d1495114389a0d020cf1874195071d.png)\n\nHere “integral curve” means a geometric trajectory following the line directions, including a vertical turn; it is broader than a classical solution graph of $y\\prime=x/y$. The parametric tangent $(\\tfrac12\\sinh s,\\tfrac12\\cosh s)=(y,x)$ remains nonzero at the turn. Each upper/lower portion can separately define a solution for $x>1/2$, but their union cannot." 17592186405015] [17592186405374 1058 17592186405520 17592186405038] [17592186405374 1062 #uuid "69ebae71-59dd-5ef4-80c3-8543331b9a5e" 17592186405038] [17592186405374 1063 17592186405358 17592186405038] [17592186405375 1089 17592186049768 17592186405015] [17592186405375 1089 17592186051091 17592186405015] [17592186405375 1090 17592186405070 17592186405038] [17592186405375 1091 #uuid "6a660494-6591-503b-a265-1ffd682ec62f" 17592186405038] [17592186405376 1068 17592186406086 17592186405015] [17592186405376 1071 "Source question: 5.\n\nFind $\\partial(x,y,z)/\\partial(u,v,w)$ for $x=5u-14v$, $y=-6v+11w$, $z=15u-4w$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405376 1072 #uuid "6a74899c-c307-5274-bad1-8b0e83322a84" 17592186405038] [17592186405376 1074 "The matrix is $\\begin{pmatrix}5&-14&0\\\\0&-6&11\\\\15&0&-4\\end{pmatrix}$. Expansion along the first row gives $5(24)+14(0-165)=120-2310=-2190$." 17592186405015] [17592186405377 1062 #uuid "6aa0e69b-1208-510b-bbc6-b77959d8e927" 17592186405038] [17592186405377 1063 17592186405777 17592186405038] [17592186405378 1089 17592186048960 17592186405015] [17592186405378 1089 17592186049859 17592186405015] [17592186405378 1090 17592186405779 17592186405038] [17592186405378 1091 #uuid "6b212159-b2ab-54af-b3bb-14e702e8b990" 17592186405038] [17592186405379 1068 17592186405954 17592186405015] [17592186405379 1071 "Source question: 1.\n\nFind $\\partial(x,y)/\\partial(u,v)$ for $x=2u+15$, $y=-12+5v$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405379 1072 #uuid "6b367126-1343-53ae-9c34-8bc4267f0529" 17592186405038] [17592186405379 1074 "The derivative matrix is $\\begin{pmatrix}2&0\\\\0&5\\end{pmatrix}$, with determinant 10. Translation constants disappear under differentiation." 17592186405015] [17592186405380 1058 17592186405428 17592186405038] [17592186405380 1062 #uuid "6badec0e-125d-5613-8f09-cc118c0be38b" 17592186405038] [17592186405380 1063 17592186405235 17592186405038] [17592186405381 1058 17592186405718 17592186405038] [17592186405381 1062 #uuid "6cb70292-7d59-5411-9453-c3136f66b466" 17592186405038] [17592186405381 1063 17592186405281 17592186405038] [17592186405382 1068 17592186406188 17592186405015] [17592186405382 1071 "Source question: 4.\n\nFind $\\nabla\\times\\mathbf F$ for $\\mathbf F=\\langle4\\sin(8xy),5\\cos(xy^8),8x\\rangle$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405382 1072 #uuid "6cb915d9-32da-5882-8a15-52de048ec570" 17592186405038] [17592186405382 1074 "R_y−Q_z=0. P_z−R_x=−8. By the chain rule, Q_x=−5y⁸sin(xy⁸) and P_y=32xcos(8xy); subtract these to obtain the third component." 17592186405015] [17592186405383 1062 #uuid "6cd573fb-f7fa-5d84-9042-4988b110ce49" 17592186405038] [17592186405383 1063 17592186405107 17592186405038] [17592186405384 1068 17592186406134 17592186405015] [17592186405384 1071 "Source question: 7.\n\n**Question 7**\n\nChoose the graph of $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)=z\\mathbf k$." 17592186405015] [17592186405384 1072 #uuid "6cfd612c-72d9-547c-a04e-5b67330f4718" 17592186405038] [17592186405384 1074 "For $z\\mathbf k$, arrows point upward at $z>0$ and downward at $z<0$, with magnitude $|z|$. The field vanishes on the plane $z=0$." 17592186405015] [17592186405385 1089 17592186051076 17592186405015] [17592186405385 1090 17592186405583 17592186405038] [17592186405385 1091 #uuid "6d7e7f26-f65a-52c5-835c-16e641946749" 17592186405038] [17592186405386 1058 17592186405208 17592186405038] [17592186405386 1062 #uuid "6df5070d-c5ff-5ffe-af19-3cddc1bb53b6" 17592186405038] [17592186405386 1063 17592186405441 17592186405038] [17592186405387 1058 17592186405344 17592186405038] [17592186405387 1062 #uuid "6dffcdff-0bf2-524b-a618-c79af90b25f4" 17592186405038] [17592186405387 1063 17592186405286 17592186405038] [17592186405388 1071 "**Question 6 — Part B**\n\nLet $V$ be the volume of $D$. Prove the shell formula:\n\n$$\nV=\\iiint_D dV=2\\pi\\int_a^b\\bigl[xf(x)-xg(x)\\bigr]\\,dx.\n$$" 17592186405038] [17592186405388 1072 #uuid "6e013021-17a2-5e93-a708-796917c64419" 17592186405038] [17592186405388 1074 "Using the cylindrical volume element $dV=r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta$,\n\n$$V=\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\int_a^b\\int_{g(r)}^{f(r)}r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta=2\\pi\\int_a^b r[f(r)-g(r)]\\,dr=2\\pi\\int_a^b[xf(x)-xg(x)]\\,dx.$$\n\nThe last step only renames the dummy variable. As usual, the functions must define an integrable solid of finite volume for this finite-volume formula.\n\nThe factor $r$ is the Jacobian. Integrating over height gives $r(f-g)$, and integrating over the full angle gives $2\\pi$. The assumptions $r\\ge0$ and $f\\ge g$ make the shell contribution nonnegative." 17592186405015] [17592186405389 1068 17592186405913 17592186405015] [17592186405389 1071 "Source question: 2.\n\nUse the **Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals** to evaluate $\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot d\\mathbf r$. The field $\\mathbf F=\\langle13y^2+26x,26xy\\rangle$ is conservative with the supplied potential $f=13xy^2+13x^2$. The piecewise smooth curve joins $(2,2)$ to $(-3,8)$.\n\nIntegral: {{integral}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405389 1072 #uuid "6e71df8a-2048-5f70-9531-c01214bf86b0" 17592186405038] [17592186405389 1074 "$f(-3,8)=-2496+117=-2379$ and $f(2,2)=104+52=156$. The difference is −2535." 17592186405015] [17592186405390 1058 17592186405688 17592186405038] [17592186405390 1062 #uuid "6e7d4b58-fcd1-5a46-b281-11a251d0a17f" 17592186405038] [17592186405390 1063 17592186405238 17592186405038] [17592186405391 1089 17592186049465 17592186405015] [17592186405391 1090 17592186405570 17592186405038] [17592186405391 1091 #uuid "6e977281-ebc8-53e5-9390-35dbbcb36186" 17592186405038] [17592186405392 1058 17592186405260 17592186405038] [17592186405392 1062 #uuid "6ec35a50-baa3-5667-8239-91e6701e8c2b" 17592186405038] [17592186405392 1063 17592186405362 17592186405038] [17592186405393 1058 17592186405050 17592186405038] [17592186405393 1062 #uuid "6ed9d1e2-3d21-5e7d-9c44-7795c0743974" 17592186405038] [17592186405393 1063 17592186405181 17592186405038] [17592186405394 1058 17592186405354 17592186405038] [17592186405394 1062 #uuid "6f22bf3d-00b0-5d4d-9c6d-5c35da0625c5" 17592186405038] [17592186405394 1063 17592186405637 17592186405038] [17592186405395 1058 17592186405591 17592186405038] [17592186405395 1062 #uuid "6f53c13d-e2e3-5d2e-91a0-025140c8a4c9" 17592186405038] [17592186405395 1063 17592186405476 17592186405038] [17592186405396 1068 17592186406311 17592186405015] [17592186405396 1071 "Source question: 1.\n\n**Question 1**\n\nLet $P(x,y,z)$, $Q(x,y,z)$, and $R(x,y,z)$ be scalar functions on a region $E$. True or false: the formula $F(x,y,z)=P(x,y,z)+Q(x,y,z)+R(x,y,z)$ defines a vector field on $E$." 17592186405015] [17592186405396 1072 #uuid "6fc5de92-5e3b-5f47-bc37-7ab9debf2a81" 17592186405038] [17592186405396 1074 "A vector field assigns a vector to each point. The displayed sum is scalar; a three-dimensional vector field has the form $\\mathbf F=P\\mathbf i+Q\\mathbf j+R\\mathbf k$." 17592186405015] [17592186405397 1089 17592186048832 17592186405015] [17592186405397 1089 17592186050196 17592186405015] [17592186405397 1090 17592186405568 17592186405038] [17592186405397 1091 #uuid "6fceb2a5-80b2-51fa-8637-69c79216f727" 17592186405038] [17592186405398 1089 17592186048960 17592186405015] [17592186405398 1089 17592186049859 17592186405015] [17592186405398 1090 17592186405440 17592186405038] [17592186405398 1091 #uuid "7000c2a3-bef7-547e-a2d9-7e23a8b9c2a7" 17592186405038] [17592186405399 1068 17592186405864 17592186405015] [17592186405399 1071 "**Question 2 — Part A**\n\nFind\n\n$$\n\\frac{d\\mathbf r}{dt}=\\mathbf r'(t)=\\langle x'(t),y'(t),z'(t)\\rangle.\n$$\n\n$\\mathbf r\\,\\prime(t)=$ {{field-1}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405399 1072 #uuid "700ccd4c-2830-5ef7-a43a-6a74ddcf99c3" 17592186405038] [17592186405399 1074 "Differentiate each component. In particular, $(\\ln\\sec t)\\prime=\\sec t\\tan t/\\sec t=\\tan t$ wherever the real-valued logarithm is defined." 17592186405015] [17592186405400 1068 17592186406185 17592186405015] [17592186405400 1071 "Source question: 12.\n\n**Question 12**\n\nA small mass $m$ (kg) produces gravitational potential $u=-Gm/r$, where $r$ is the distance from the mass in meters, $G=6.67\\times10^{-11}\\ \\mathrm{N\\,m^2/kg^2}$, and $\\mathbf r=x\\mathbf i+y\\mathbf j+z\\mathbf k$. If $\\mathbf g=-\\nabla u$, choose the expression for $\\mathbf g$." 17592186405015] [17592186405400 1072 #uuid "701c6ca9-db81-5c06-87d0-ae18e2e96b57" 17592186405038] [17592186405400 1074 "Using $r=\\sqrt{x^2+y^2+z^2}$ gives $\\nabla(1/r)=-\\mathbf r/r^3$. Hence $\\nabla u=Gm\\mathbf r/r^3$ and $\\mathbf g=-Gm\\mathbf r/r^3$, directed toward the mass." 17592186405015] [17592186405401 1058 17592186405481 17592186405038] [17592186405401 1062 #uuid "703e4d70-5ac9-582a-b992-782ae37bf704" 17592186405038] [17592186405401 1063 17592186405620 17592186405038] [17592186405402 1068 17592186406294 17592186405015] [17592186405402 1071 "**(b)** Find the unique two-dimensional field that is undefined on y=x, unit everywhere else, and points away from and orthogonal to y=x.\n\n$\\mathbf F(x,y)=$ {{field}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405402 1072 #uuid "706cd50f-7f72-519c-9239-88dfe94c53eb" 17592186405038] [17592186405402 1074 "The closest point on the line is $((x+y)/2,(x+y)/2)$. The displacement away from that point is proportional to $(x-y,y-x)$, perpendicular to (1,1). Its length is $\\sqrt2|x-y|$. Normalize; the sign automatically reverses across the line." 17592186405015] [17592186405403 1058 17592186405524 17592186405038] [17592186405403 1062 #uuid "70cec3ec-ccdd-50b9-8459-b78b529383af" 17592186405038] [17592186405403 1063 17592186405422 17592186405038] [17592186405404 1068 17592186406193 17592186405015] [17592186405404 1071 "Source exercise: **1.2.1**, [printed page 42 (PDF page 54)](/api/source?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F341%2FKuttler-LinearAlgebra-AFirstCourse-2017A.pdf#page=54).\n\n**Question 4**\n\nFind the point $(x_1,y_1)$ which lies on both lines,\n\n$$\nx+3y=1 \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad 4x-y=3.\n$$\n\nEnter the exact ordered pair $(x_1,y_1)$, with fractions simplified: {{field-1}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405404 1072 #uuid "7108b45a-bee1-5b0d-a9fa-58c7694ce7cd" 17592186405038] [17592186405404 1074 "A common point satisfies both equations. Multiply $x+3y=1$ by $4$ to get $4x+12y=4$, then subtract $4x-y=3$. This gives $13y=1$, so $y=1/13$ and $x=1-3/13=10/13$. Thus the point is $\\left(\\frac{10}{13},\\frac{1}{13}\\right)$. Substitution gives $1$ and $3$ in the two original equations. Enter $x$ first and $y$ second." 17592186405015] [17592186405405 1068 17592186406216 17592186405015] [17592186405405 1071 "Source question: 1.\n\nFind $\\int_C21x^2y\\,ds$ for $x=\\cos(13t),\\ y=\\cos(13t),\\ 0\\le t\\le\\pi/26$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405405 1072 #uuid "710bf196-32e2-56c2-825f-5f467096d5d8" 17592186405038] [17592186405405 1074 "$ds=13\\sqrt2\\sin(13t)dt$ on this interval. With u=cos(13t), the integral is $21\\sqrt2\\int_0^1u^3du=21\\sqrt2/4$. The straight segment from (1,1) to (0,0) gives the same result." 17592186405015] [17592186405406 1068 17592186406141 17592186405015] [17592186405406 1071 "Source question: 5.\n\nFind the curl of $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)=\\langle9xyz,2xz,5z\\rangle$. \n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405406 1072 #uuid "712bff71-e563-5c10-ac8d-80ae2cd3b59e" 17592186405038] [17592186405406 1074 "$R_y-Q_z=0-2x$, $P_z-R_x=9xy-0$, and $Q_x-P_y=2z-9xz$." 17592186405015] [17592186405407 1058 17592186405451 17592186405038] [17592186405407 1062 #uuid "716080cb-abb6-58af-bf99-6b3e4f0cb63e" 17592186405038] [17592186405407 1063 17592186405342 17592186405038] [17592186405408 1068 17592186405843 17592186405015] [17592186405408 1071 "Source question: 5.\n\nFind $\\int_C10x\\,ds$ along $x=3y^3$ from (0,0) to (3,1).\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405408 1072 #uuid "719bde2f-accb-5617-8b3e-5c9ccf1a2350" 17592186405038] [17592186405408 1074 "$ds=\\sqrt{1+81y^4}dy$. Integrate $30y^3\\sqrt{1+81y^4}$ from 0 to 1; u=1+81y⁴ yields $\\frac5{81}[u^{3/2}]_1^{82}$." 17592186405015] [17592186405409 1058 17592186405483 17592186405038] [17592186405409 1062 #uuid "71eca351-2638-547f-b636-b4aa591a37ca" 17592186405038] [17592186405409 1063 17592186405446 17592186405038] [17592186405410 1068 17592186406257 17592186405015] [17592186405410 1068 17592186406314 17592186405015] [17592186405410 1071 "Source question: 7.\n\nFor $f(x,y)=2xe^y$, find its line integrals with respect to x and y along $5x-y=8$ from (0,−8) to (1,−3).\n\n$\\int_C f\\,dx=$ {{dx}}.\n\n$\\int_C f\\,dy=$ {{dy}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405410 1072 #uuid "724da0d3-e6c1-56a7-84cd-b38b5511b26d" 17592186405038] [17592186405410 1074 "Use y=5x−8 and dy=5dx, x from 0 to 1. Integration by parts gives $\\int_0^1 2xe^{5x-8}dx=[(2/25)e^{5x-8}(5x-1)]_0^1$. The dy integral is five times the dx integral." 17592186405015] [17592186405411 1071 "**Question 1 — Part B**\n\nShow that the area is $\\lVert\\mathbf u\\times\\mathbf v\\rVert$." 17592186405038] [17592186405411 1072 #uuid "72764034-8319-5088-8a08-690c2046e292" 17592186405038] [17592186405411 1074 "Area is base times perpendicular height: $A=\\lVert\\mathbf u\\rVert\\,\\lVert\\mathbf v\\rVert\\sin\\theta$. By the magnitude formula for a cross product, this equals $\\lVert\\mathbf u\\times\\mathbf v\\rVert$.\n\nUse $\\lVert\\mathbf u\\rVert$ as the base length and the height from Part A. The cross product is a vector; its magnitude is the nonnegative scalar area. Parallel vectors give zero area." 17592186405015] [17592186405412 1043 17592186405199 17592186405038] [17592186405412 1043 17592186405248 17592186405038] [17592186405412 1043 17592186405274 17592186405038] [17592186405412 1043 17592186405298 17592186405038] [17592186405412 1043 17592186405351 17592186405038] [17592186405412 1043 17592186405503 17592186405038] [17592186405412 1044 17592186045461 17592186405038] [17592186405412 1045 "MTH 255 — WHW-2" 17592186405038] [17592186405412 1046 17592186405248 17592186405038] [17592186405412 1047 "2026-10-09T06:59:59.000Z" 17592186045416] [17592186405412 1048 17592186405043 17592186405038] [17592186405412 1053 #uuid "72b79f03-044d-5395-9b3e-84395f2b0012" 17592186405038] [17592186405413 1062 #uuid "7328ff42-e8b3-52fa-a540-e630bd5aa047" 17592186405038] [17592186405413 1063 17592186405264 17592186405038] [17592186405414 1068 17592186406022 17592186405015] [17592186405414 1071 "Source question: 5.\n\nFind $\\nabla\\times\\mathbf F$ for $\\mathbf F=\\langle3x+y,3y+9z,5z+6x\\rangle$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405414 1072 #uuid "735c0ea9-3a81-5b5f-a1e0-8fa3c4265e9c" 17592186405038] [17592186405414 1074 "The three components are R_y−Q_z=0−9, P_z−R_x=0−6, and Q_x−P_y=0−1." 17592186405015] [17592186405415 1058 17592186405394 17592186405038] [17592186405415 1062 #uuid "73d94423-2860-50a9-b897-85a158061430" 17592186405038] [17592186405415 1063 17592186405309 17592186405038] [17592186405416 1089 17592186049512 17592186405015] [17592186405416 1089 17592186050037 17592186405015] [17592186405416 1089 17592186050164 17592186405015] [17592186405416 1090 17592186405470 17592186405038] [17592186405416 1091 #uuid "7402b070-a8d3-54db-b8fd-5e5160f9774c" 17592186405038] [17592186405417 1068 17592186406092 17592186405015] [17592186405417 1071 "Source question: 4.\n\nUse the **Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals** to evaluate $\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot d\\mathbf r$. The field $\\mathbf F=\\left\\langle\\frac{5x}{5x^2+3y^2},\\frac{3y}{5x^2+3y^2}\\right\\rangle$ is conservative with the supplied potential $f=\\ln\\sqrt{5x^2+3y^2}$. The piecewise smooth curve joins $(3,4)$ to $(5,12)$.\n\nIntegral: {{integral}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405417 1072 #uuid "740fb150-6096-5941-a88d-25c2d5508769" 17592186405038] [17592186405417 1074 "$5(5)^2+3(12)^2=557$ and $5(3)^2+3(4)^2=93$. Thus $f(B)-f(A)=\\ln\\sqrt{557}-\\ln\\sqrt{93}$. The curve is understood to lie in the field domain, excluding the origin." 17592186405015] [17592186405418 1089 17592186048960 17592186405015] [17592186405418 1089 17592186049859 17592186405015] [17592186405418 1090 17592186405291 17592186405038] [17592186405418 1091 #uuid "7511fb3a-b93f-576c-bb8a-8396139ecb02" 17592186405038] [17592186405419 1068 17592186406112 17592186405015] [17592186405419 1071 "Source question: 2.\n\nFind $\\partial(x,y)/\\partial(u,v)$ for $x=5u+4v$, $y=6u-v/7$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405419 1072 #uuid "7549a26f-4b6b-5acb-ac4c-dcfc90235f10" 17592186405038] [17592186405419 1074 "The determinant is $5(-1/7)-4(6)=-5/7-24=-173/7$. A Jacobian determinant retains its sign." 17592186405015] [17592186405420 1068 17592186406312 17592186405015] [17592186405420 1071 "**Question 4 — Part C**\n\nSolve the initial value problem.\n\n\n\nNote: You can earn partial credit on this problem.\n\n$y(x)=$ {{field-1}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405420 1072 #uuid "7614da17-ac59-5fb4-bc36-123b0e673350" 17592186405038] [17592186405420 1074 "Insert $x=1$, $y=-4$ into $(3/4)x+Cx^9$: $C=-4-3/4=-19/4$. Substitution in $xy\\prime=9y-6x$ checks both sides as $(3/4)x-(171/4)x^9$." 17592186405015] [17592186405421 1089 17592186048314 17592186405015] [17592186405421 1089 17592186050149 17592186405015] [17592186405421 1090 17592186405417 17592186405038] [17592186405421 1091 #uuid "76c352a6-fc05-5d9f-a133-b4533abf0591" 17592186405038] [17592186405422 1068 17592186406037 17592186405015] [17592186405422 1071 "**Question 4 — Part A**\n\nIdentify an integrating factor, $\\alpha(x)$.\n\n$\\alpha(x)=$ {{field-1}}, on $x>0$." 17592186405015] [17592186405422 1072 #uuid "774ef1c9-2be1-52e3-81f1-9b8fcb25c3b7" 17592186405038] [17592186405422 1074 "Normalize first: $y\\prime-(9/x)y=-6$. Then $\\alpha=e^{\\int-9/x\\,dx}=x^{-9}$ on the positive interval containing $x=1$. Any nonzero constant multiple is also an integrating factor." 17592186405015] [17592186405423 1068 17592186406313 17592186405015] [17592186405423 1071 "Source question: 2.\n\n**Question 2**\n\nTrue or false: the gradient of a differentiable scalar function $f$ is a vector field." 17592186405015] [17592186405423 1072 #uuid "780ba61c-d062-542b-ade5-ed2455551f56" 17592186405038] [17592186405423 1074 "The gradient assigns the vector of partial derivatives to each point: $\\nabla f=\\langle f_x,f_y,f_z\\rangle$ in three dimensions. This is a vector field wherever those derivatives exist." 17592186405015] [17592186405424 1058 17592186405600 17592186405038] [17592186405424 1062 #uuid "7842d431-9dab-59c5-bb44-90e2533fdff3" 17592186405038] [17592186405424 1063 17592186405691 17592186405038] [17592186405425 1089 17592186048495 17592186405015] [17592186405425 1090 17592186405340 17592186405038] [17592186405425 1091 #uuid "78bcf688-168c-5155-b59e-537387414c48" 17592186405038] [17592186405426 1062 #uuid "79136b16-3c28-5c70-8b52-16d8f5df9ad7" 17592186405038] [17592186405426 1063 17592186405756 17592186405038] [17592186405427 1089 17592186051006 17592186405015] [17592186405427 1090 17592186405672 17592186405038] [17592186405427 1091 #uuid "79314918-f2f2-5be3-b254-ed9f28f8725d" 17592186405038] [17592186405428 1058 17592186405173 17592186405038] [17592186405428 1062 #uuid "79622859-208d-5c92-bed4-e41ba0af259b" 17592186405038] [17592186405428 1063 17592186405062 17592186405038] [17592186405429 1089 17592186050149 17592186405015] [17592186405429 1089 17592186050875 17592186405015] [17592186405429 1090 17592186405329 17592186405038] [17592186405429 1091 #uuid "79dcb780-769d-5936-9a71-d9d802d443c5" 17592186405038] [17592186405430 1058 17592186405275 17592186405038] [17592186405430 1062 #uuid "79eec771-0417-53b5-a386-6e08f10f94c5" 17592186405038] [17592186405430 1063 17592186405239 17592186405038] [17592186405431 1064 "Date: 2026-09-29\nDue: 2026-09-29\n\nSource: [Original worksheet](/api/source?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F256%2FR1%2F256-R1.pdf)\n\nThe purpose of this recitation is to review material from previous calculus classes that will be important during the quarter.\n\nStudy answers, explanations, and reference graphics are authored additions to the linked source." 17592186405015] [17592186405431 1066 #uuid "79f244bc-585f-55c6-b7af-ebf165d173ff" 17592186405038] [17592186405431 1067 "Assignment information" 17592186405038] [17592186405432 1089 17592186051091 17592186405015] [17592186405432 1090 17592186405180 17592186405038] [17592186405432 1091 #uuid "7a4dd39c-1d2c-5300-b344-442151bffcae" 17592186405038] [17592186405433 1043 17592186405157 17592186405038] [17592186405433 1043 17592186405409 17592186405038] [17592186405433 1043 17592186405483 17592186405038] [17592186405433 1043 17592186405507 17592186405038] [17592186405433 1043 17592186405687 17592186405038] [17592186405433 1043 17592186405817 17592186405038] [17592186405433 1044 17592186045461 17592186405038] [17592186405433 1045 "MTH 255 — R-2" 17592186405038] [17592186405433 1046 17592186405409 17592186405038] [17592186405433 1047 "2026-10-07T06:59:59.000Z" 17592186045416] [17592186405433 1048 17592186405043 17592186405038] [17592186405433 1053 #uuid "7a8c0838-9d2b-5f03-b06d-477868fc5040" 17592186405038] [17592186405434 1058 17592186405055 17592186405038] [17592186405434 1062 #uuid "7ac273ab-f292-5fa7-b804-a0db326cbdac" 17592186405038] [17592186405434 1063 17592186405273 17592186405038] [17592186405435 1058 17592186405371 17592186405038] [17592186405435 1062 #uuid "7ade2004-9d50-579c-b1c8-67b44ade79b9" 17592186405038] [17592186405435 1063 17592186405746 17592186405038] [17592186405436 1058 17592186405051 17592186405038] [17592186405436 1062 #uuid "7b4e4fac-50e2-5400-8062-b6085aa57a72" 17592186405038] [17592186405436 1063 17592186405153 17592186405038] [17592186405437 1058 17592186405074 17592186405038] [17592186405437 1062 #uuid "7b7558bd-f13c-515c-963d-a00141ef595a" 17592186405038] [17592186405437 1063 17592186405139 17592186405038] [17592186405438 1085 17592186405390 17592186405038] [17592186405438 1085 17592186405688 17592186405038] [17592186405438 1086 "Source question: 4.\n\n**The 2D gradient is orthogonal to a level curve**\n\nLet $\\varphi(x,y)$ be a scalar function and consider $\\varphi(x,y)=c$. From Problem 3, $\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=-\\dfrac{\\varphi_x}{\\varphi_y}$." 17592186405038] [17592186405438 1087 #uuid "7b8d7f31-40e1-5d67-9f68-fdca314f6dbe" 17592186405038] [17592186405438 1088 17592186405390 17592186405038] [17592186405439 1068 17592186406083 17592186405015] [17592186405439 1071 "Source question: 22.\n\nAn object follows $\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle16t,14t-3t^2\\rangle$ from t=0 to t=7. A force has magnitude proportional to speed and direction opposite velocity. Find its work W, using k for the proportionality constant.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405439 1072 #uuid "7ba0c993-1461-54bf-ab68-81e93a577d6d" 17592186405038] [17592186405439 1074 "The force is $F=-k r\\prime$, so $W=-k\\int_0^7\\|r\\prime\\|^2dt=-k\\int_0^7[256+(14-6t)^2]dt=-k(1792+1372)$. The negative sign agrees with resistance to motion." 17592186405015] [17592186405440 1068 17592186405997 17592186405015] [17592186405440 1071 "Source question: 14.\n\nFind $\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot d\\mathbf r$ for $\\mathbf F=\\langle x^2y,xy^2\\rangle$, $\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle2t,t^2\\rangle$, $1\\le t\\le2$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405440 1072 #uuid "7c6c8261-e7ba-50b0-a96f-e25174046ca6" 17592186405038] [17592186405440 1074 "$F(r)=(4t^4,2t^5)$ and $r\\prime=(2,2t)$, so integrate $8t^4+4t^6$: $[8t^5/5+4t^7/7]_1^2=248/5+508/7$." 17592186405015] [17592186405441 1089 17592186048923 17592186405015] [17592186405441 1089 17592186050418 17592186405015] [17592186405441 1090 17592186405270 17592186405038] [17592186405441 1091 #uuid "7d4b6094-42eb-5fca-b020-01d3f2d4cd51" 17592186405038] [17592186405442 1058 17592186405493 17592186405038] [17592186405442 1062 #uuid "7da1cd95-a447-52ba-98ee-93ed57cb49e9" 17592186405038] [17592186405442 1063 17592186405644 17592186405038] [17592186405443 1089 17592186048314 17592186405015] [17592186405443 1089 17592186050149 17592186405015] [17592186405443 1090 17592186405158 17592186405038] [17592186405443 1091 #uuid "7db79555-306d-5e13-83a5-81ee7e48d3e2" 17592186405038] [17592186405444 1071 "**Question 3 — Part A**\n\nUse the product rule to express the following in terms of $e^x$, $y$, and $y'$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{d}{dx}(e^xy).\n$$\n\nIf $e^xy'+e^xy=0$, what must be true about $\\dfrac{d}{dx}(e^xy)$?" 17592186405038] [17592186405444 1072 #uuid "7e087087-e9d1-5aab-955b-b1c9a4f59158" 17592186405038] [17592186405444 1074 "$\\frac{d}{dx}(e^xy)=e^xy+e^xy\\prime$. By the given differential equation this derivative equals $0$.\n\nThe product rule combines the two terms of the ODE into one derivative. The zero is the derivative of the product, not a claim that $y$ or $e^xy$ itself must be zero." 17592186405015] [17592186405445 1089 17592186049366 17592186405015] [17592186405445 1090 17592186405095 17592186405038] [17592186405445 1091 #uuid "7e5ec52f-a8e3-522d-b0a7-9a4a87670d0a" 17592186405038] [17592186405446 1064 "**Math 255 — Recitation Worksheet 2**\n\nDue: 2026-10-06\nSource: [Original worksheet](/api/source?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F255%2FR-2%2F255-R2.pdf)\n\nStudy answers and explanations are authored additions, not publisher solutions.\n\n## Instructions\n\nGraded for completeness. Work on the printed worksheet, write/type on the PDF, or use your own paper with the same layout. Provide first name, last name, and OSU student ID on the cover. Submit one PDF to Gradescope under **Recitation Worksheet 2**, preserving the six-page template: cover followed by one page per problem, including blank problems. Scan printed work; digital work already has the required format. For separate paper, photograph all pages and merge them; combine multiple pages for one problem into a single page (a collage). Incorrectly formatted submissions receive 0. No due date is stated in the PDF." 17592186045416] [17592186405446 1066 #uuid "7e73da9e-d433-5af5-a7c6-2cf078020f7f" 17592186405038] [17592186405446 1067 "Assignment information" 17592186405038] [17592186405447 1058 17592186405401 17592186405038] [17592186405447 1062 #uuid "7ebfe464-e7d9-562e-af3d-253bed3e218d" 17592186405038] [17592186405447 1063 17592186405093 17592186405038] [17592186405448 1062 #uuid "8016b096-ee52-5467-938c-f0d50a99e5b5" 17592186405038] [17592186405448 1063 17592186405303 17592186405038] [17592186405449 1062 #uuid "808c12e7-4aaf-5484-b1e5-96fad9e2715e" 17592186405038] [17592186405449 1063 17592186405528 17592186405038] [17592186405450 1068 17592186406018 17592186405015] [17592186405450 1071 "**Question 3 — Part D**\n\nUse the above to determine what $y(x)$ must be equal to.\n\n$y(x)=$ {{field-1}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405450 1072 #uuid "817abe0c-0d61-5710-8723-65691e6065d4" 17592186405038] [17592186405450 1074 "From $e^xy=y_0$, divide by $e^x>0$ to get $y=y_0e^{-x}$. Its derivative is $-y_0e^{-x}=-y$, and at $x=0$ its value is $y_0$." 17592186405015] [17592186405451 1058 17592186405715 17592186405038] [17592186405451 1062 #uuid "819ba26f-46cb-5b77-83a8-b4f684630c85" 17592186405038] [17592186405451 1063 17592186405558 17592186405038] [17592186405452 1068 17592186406327 17592186405015] [17592186405452 1071 "Source question: 6.\n\nUse the divergence theorem to find the outward flux of $\\mathbf F=\\langle5x,3y^2,9z^2\\rangle$ through the entire boundary of the solid cylinder $x^2+y^2\\le9$, 0≤z≤5.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405452 1072 #uuid "81e342ef-0ce2-5e29-8051-7809751b201e" 17592186405038] [17592186405452 1074 "Divergence is 5+6y+18z. The y term integrates to zero over each centered disk. The area is 9π, so the integral is $9\\pi\\int_0^5(5+18z)dz=9\\pi(25+225)=2250\\pi$. The boundary includes both end disks." 17592186405015] [17592186405453 1062 #uuid "81e3a94a-19ee-5c49-82aa-aa850913fdfa" 17592186405038] [17592186405453 1063 17592186405263 17592186405038] [17592186405454 1089 17592186051091 17592186405015] [17592186405454 1090 17592186405456 17592186405038] [17592186405454 1091 #uuid "820b191f-ad2d-578f-9376-b82873b3fe03" 17592186405038] [17592186405455 1058 17592186405360 17592186405038] [17592186405455 1062 #uuid "825b7b1c-4d8f-5bc8-8dfd-2e7cdabe772e" 17592186405038] [17592186405455 1063 17592186405760 17592186405038] [17592186405456 1068 17592186406171 17592186405015] [17592186405456 1071 "Source question: 10.\n\nDetermine whether the following force is a conservative vector field.\n\n$\\mathbf F=\\langle10xy,yz,8xz\\rangle$." 17592186405015] [17592186405456 1072 #uuid "82617c5c-e6ff-5ca5-859d-739ccead9946" 17592186405038] [17592186405456 1074 "Curl F=(−y,−8z,−10x), which is not identically zero. Therefore F cannot be a gradient field on all of space. For instance, P_y=10x differs from Q_x=0." 17592186405015] [17592186405457 1058 17592186405268 17592186405038] [17592186405457 1062 #uuid "829de6c7-587d-59b1-9551-2b26fcd06aa4" 17592186405038] [17592186405457 1063 17592186405256 17592186405038] [17592186405458 1062 #uuid "82ff387f-5526-5e8a-859e-4dd849e663e1" 17592186405038] [17592186405458 1063 17592186405188 17592186405038] [17592186405459 1068 17592186406049 17592186405015] [17592186405459 1071 "Source question: 9.\n\nThe folium of Descartes is parameterized by $x(t)=8t/(1+t^3)$, $y(t)=8t^2/(1+t^3)$. Use **Green’s theorem** to find the area enclosed by its loop.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405459 1072 #uuid "833b6edc-bdc6-5193-a541-4ce8a5225340" 17592186405038] [17592186405459 1074 "The counterclockwise loop runs from t=0 to t→∞. Green’s area formula gives $A=\\frac12\\int_0^\\infty(xy′-yx′)dt=32\\int_0^\\infty t^2/(1+t^3)^2dt$. With u=1+t³ this is $\\frac{32}{3}\\int_1^\\infty u^{-2}du=32/3$." 17592186405015] [17592186405460 1058 17592186405724 17592186405038] [17592186405460 1062 #uuid "83451e94-b7cc-591b-bf09-797da1982e85" 17592186405038] [17592186405460 1063 17592186405781 17592186405038] [17592186405461 1062 #uuid "8485d523-7107-507d-8e13-d151278c31e3" 17592186405038] [17592186405461 1063 17592186405130 17592186405038] [17592186405462 1068 17592186405877 17592186405015] [17592186405462 1071 "Source question: 3.\n\nUse the **Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals** to evaluate $\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot d\\mathbf r$. The field $\\mathbf F=\\langle14xe^{7x^2+2y^2},4ye^{7x^2+2y^2}\\rangle$ is conservative with the supplied potential $f=e^{7x^2+2y^2}$. The piecewise smooth curve joins $(0,0)$ to $(1,1)$.\n\nIntegral: {{integral}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405462 1072 #uuid "849d1ca7-6e6d-571a-9244-5dd9c265d746" 17592186405038] [17592186405462 1074 "The final exponent is 7+2=9 and the initial exponent is 0. Thus f(B)−f(A)=e⁹−1." 17592186405015] [17592186405463 1043 17592186405094 17592186405038] [17592186405463 1043 17592186405353 17592186405038] [17592186405463 1043 17592186405478 17592186405038] [17592186405463 1043 17592186405485 17592186405038] [17592186405463 1044 17592186045461 17592186405038] [17592186405463 1045 "MTH 256 — R-1" 17592186405038] [17592186405463 1046 17592186405485 17592186405038] [17592186405463 1047 "2026-09-30T06:59:59.000Z" 17592186405038] [17592186405463 1048 17592186405044 17592186405038] [17592186405463 1053 #uuid "8509d51d-f29b-518a-ac91-1287b63e5d4e" 17592186405038] [17592186405464 1089 17592186048314 17592186405015] [17592186405464 1089 17592186050875 17592186405015] [17592186405464 1090 17592186405142 17592186405038] [17592186405464 1091 #uuid "85182afa-b690-5574-9b3c-c6f00ad809f4" 17592186405038] [17592186405465 1058 17592186405834 17592186405038] [17592186405465 1062 #uuid "8597a425-2b7e-5a6b-be36-3b4246d23046" 17592186405038] [17592186405465 1063 17592186405207 17592186405038] [17592186405466 1058 17592186405178 17592186405038] [17592186405466 1062 #uuid "85a6e120-67d6-5e1f-911d-2e917cc2f801" 17592186405038] [17592186405466 1063 17592186405743 17592186405038] [17592186405467 1085 17592186405218 17592186405038] [17592186405467 1085 17592186405480 17592186405038] [17592186405467 1086 "Source question: 4.\n\nConsider $x(t)=5\\sin t$ and $y(t)=5\\cos t$, where $0\\le t\\le2\\pi$. Use exact values." 17592186405038] [17592186405467 1087 #uuid "85dfec84-a988-56e8-b658-a46ba3bdd809" 17592186405038] [17592186405467 1088 17592186405480 17592186405038] [17592186405468 1058 17592186405167 17592186405038] [17592186405468 1062 #uuid "85e946e9-046b-5c63-94e0-0364425689c5" 17592186405038] [17592186405468 1063 17592186405671 17592186405038] [17592186405469 1089 17592186050935 17592186405015] [17592186405469 1089 17592186051006 17592186405015] [17592186405469 1090 17592186405410 17592186405038] [17592186405469 1091 #uuid "8628c127-644b-5eff-a949-785f7eca069b" 17592186405038] [17592186405470 1085 17592186405099 17592186405038] [17592186405470 1085 17592186405328 17592186405038] [17592186405470 1085 17592186405334 17592186405038] [17592186405470 1086 "Source question: 5.\n\n**Implicit differentiation — multivariable**\n\nLet $f(x,y,z)$ be a scalar function and consider the level surface $f(x,y,z)=c$. Regard $z$ as a function of independent variables $x$ and $y$, so $\\partial y/\\partial x=\\partial x/\\partial y=0$. The dependence tree is:\n\n![f depends on x, y, and z; each branch is differentiated with respect to x and y](images/e6/e62ca88ac96b7eff736b8f6e72079a49562861ebcec69b3be4150e5efe087c90.png)" 17592186405038] [17592186405470 1087 #uuid "862cc95f-2d16-59de-83e5-3c4048895e63" 17592186405038] [17592186405470 1088 17592186405099 17592186405038] [17592186405471 1064 "**6.2 Line Integrals**\n\nDue: 2026-10-15\nSource: [Macmillan Achieve](https://achieve.macmillanlearning.com/ihub/assessment/f188d950-dd73-11e0-9572-0800200c9a66/5a1db2a7-4295-4a4c-9dbb-ceb8b174a282/cad9d5c1-5041-449d-b720-8f2e67d796cc)\n\nStudy answers and explanations are authored additions, not publisher solutions.\n\n## Instructions\n\nGive exact answers with symbolic notation and fractions as needed. Source: Sullivan 2e Calculus, W.H. Freeman. All 24 assigned questions are retained; questions 7 and 8 each have separate dx and dy answer slots. The live platform allows unlimited attempts without an incorrect-attempt penalty and submissions up to 50 days late at a 2% daily penalty." 17592186405015] [17592186405471 1066 #uuid "86c1dae1-90fa-5016-bc6d-3fc87e25d60a" 17592186405038] [17592186405471 1067 "Assignment information" 17592186405038] [17592186405472 1058 17592186405058 17592186405038] [17592186405472 1062 #uuid "86e5be5f-994e-5f25-b0ab-109c9a7601ca" 17592186405038] [17592186405472 1063 17592186405547 17592186405038] [17592186405473 1068 17592186406069 17592186405015] [17592186405473 1071 "Source question: 4.\n\nUse the divergence theorem to find the outward flux of $\\mathbf F=\\langle5xy+2z,9y^2+8,-8x-9y\\rangle$ through the surface of the solid bounded by x+y+z=2 and the three coordinate planes.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405473 1072 #uuid "86edb82e-3c3e-550d-8bce-8b606a2ef8ab" 17592186405038] [17592186405473 1074 "Divergence is 5y+18y=23y. The first-octant tetrahedron has volume 2³/6=4/3 and centroid y-coordinate 2/4=1/2. Hence ∭23y dV=23·(1/2)·(4/3)=46/3. Equivalently integrate 23y with 0≤x≤2, 0≤y≤2−x, 0≤z≤2−x−y." 17592186405015] [17592186405474 1058 17592186405727 17592186405038] [17592186405474 1062 #uuid "8715e248-ad20-5616-9aa6-02efb1da94bb" 17592186405038] [17592186405474 1063 17592186405373 17592186405038] [17592186405475 1068 17592186406081 17592186405015] [17592186405475 1071 "Source question: 4.\n\nFind $\\iint_Sx^2z\\,dS$ on the cylindrical surface $x^2+y^2=64$, $0\\le z\\le5$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405475 1072 #uuid "87aee391-df14-5d8e-81d7-0df427ac5219" 17592186405038] [17592186405475 1074 "Parameterize (8cosθ,8sinθ,z); the surface element is 8dθ dz. Thus $I=512\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\cos^2\\theta d\\theta\\int_0^5z\\,dz=512\\pi(25/2)=6400\\pi$. S contains only the side, not end disks." 17592186405015] [17592186405476 1089 17592186048314 17592186405015] [17592186405476 1089 17592186050875 17592186405015] [17592186405476 1090 17592186405249 17592186405038] [17592186405476 1091 #uuid "87b44d53-8c5c-5df6-bac3-1905b40bbe0d" 17592186405038] [17592186405477 1085 17592186405449 17592186405038] [17592186405477 1085 17592186405545 17592186405038] [17592186405477 1086 "Source question: 4(a)–(b), PDF page 5.\n\n**Unit vectors in polar coordinates.** Consider $P(x,y)$, $\\mathbf r=\\overrightarrow{OP}=\\langle x,y\\rangle=\\langle r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta\\rangle$, the unit circle centered at P, and\n\n$$\\mathbf i=\\langle1,0\\rangle,\\quad\\mathbf j=\\langle0,1\\rangle,\\quad\\hat{\\mathbf r}=\\langle\\cos\\theta,\\sin\\theta\\rangle,\\quad\\hat{\\boldsymbol\\theta}=\\langle-\\sin\\theta,\\cos\\theta\\rangle.$$\n\n![Source polar unit vectors at P](images/35/35a19c56fb5bee3177ff22b713fb8f5b30ea312559b5a1024abcc8360deb2b9e.png)" 17592186405038] [17592186405477 1087 #uuid "880e33b4-548c-5f00-a2dc-f3f54b6cc045" 17592186405038] [17592186405477 1088 17592186405545 17592186405038] [17592186405478 1058 17592186405353 17592186405038] [17592186405478 1062 #uuid "88836068-2deb-5c0c-aa17-5c344e1ddb71" 17592186405038] [17592186405478 1063 17592186405071 17592186405038] [17592186405479 1089 17592186050168 17592186405015] [17592186405479 1089 17592186050537 17592186405015] [17592186405479 1090 17592186405338 17592186405038] [17592186405479 1091 #uuid "889e9b15-d05d-5609-96f2-8cb7cc248a76" 17592186405038] [17592186405480 1058 17592186405218 17592186405038] [17592186405480 1062 #uuid "88ea6e1c-94b0-5dee-8632-a5ec8e48ec79" 17592186405038] [17592186405480 1063 17592186405494 17592186405038] [17592186405481 1058 17592186405472 17592186405038] [17592186405481 1062 #uuid "89a414a6-ba90-5661-8392-c74c4b88074c" 17592186405038] [17592186405481 1063 17592186405778 17592186405038] [17592186405482 1058 17592186405595 17592186405038] [17592186405482 1062 #uuid "89fe4cce-28a2-5c30-add0-597f38857876" 17592186405038] [17592186405482 1063 17592186405796 17592186405038] [17592186405483 1058 17592186405507 17592186405038] [17592186405483 1062 #uuid "8a2449b9-c2a6-5ae7-9099-4ab778ec69a1" 17592186405038] [17592186405483 1063 17592186405294 17592186405038] [17592186405484 1068 17592186406305 17592186405015] [17592186405484 1071 "**Question 3 — Part B**\n\nUse Part A to find $dy/dx$ for the circle $x^2+y^2=1$.\n\n$\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=$ {{field-1}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405484 1072 #uuid "8a4c59a9-98c0-5117-98f1-aa1ac38cd7f0" 17592186405038] [17592186405484 1074 "With $f=x^2+y^2$, $f_x=2x$ and $f_y=2y$. Thus $dy/dx=-2x/(2y)=-x/y$ where $y\\ne0$. Direct differentiation gives the same equation, $2x+2y\\,dy/dx=0$. At $(\\pm1,0)$ the tangent is vertical." 17592186405015] [17592186405485 1058 17592186405478 17592186405038] [17592186405485 1062 #uuid "8a567045-11f2-5c4d-aac5-a47bdeded03a" 17592186405038] [17592186405485 1063 17592186405431 17592186405038] [17592186405486 1089 17592186050149 17592186405015] [17592186405486 1089 17592186050875 17592186405015] [17592186405486 1090 17592186405824 17592186405038] [17592186405486 1091 #uuid "8a74cfa8-3873-5060-ae2d-ff2ccd7dc43c" 17592186405038] [17592186405487 1058 17592186405582 17592186405038] [17592186405487 1062 #uuid "8a9a669a-47ee-5c94-b177-4e6ac8cabc6a" 17592186405038] [17592186405487 1063 17592186405734 17592186405038] [17592186405488 1089 17592186050295 17592186405015] [17592186405488 1089 17592186050866 17592186405015] [17592186405488 1090 17592186405533 17592186405038] [17592186405488 1091 #uuid "8b9ae13e-c429-59f7-ba8b-07928cf21834" 17592186405038] [17592186405489 1062 #uuid "8bce52f4-560c-5f2a-a011-142eea67431a" 17592186405038] [17592186405489 1063 17592186405603 17592186405038] [17592186405490 1085 17592186405113 17592186405038] [17592186405490 1085 17592186405355 17592186405038] [17592186405490 1086 "Source question: 11(a)–(b)." 17592186405038] [17592186405490 1087 #uuid "8be4e529-b068-50b1-9fb5-96f48c8389d2" 17592186405038] [17592186405490 1088 17592186405355 17592186405038] [17592186405491 1062 #uuid "8c1eb0a5-02d2-5a63-a245-a5ba289649f7" 17592186405038] [17592186405491 1063 17592186405425 17592186405038] [17592186405492 1058 17592186405114 17592186405038] [17592186405492 1062 #uuid "8c40b84d-c4fc-51f4-9acc-56510960bc57" 17592186405038] [17592186405492 1063 17592186405397 17592186405038] [17592186405493 1058 17592186405127 17592186405038] [17592186405493 1062 #uuid "8c51de34-8e91-5cdd-8ba0-e50b0c0da2ad" 17592186405038] [17592186405493 1063 17592186405664 17592186405038] [17592186405494 1068 17592186406161 17592186405015] [17592186405494 1071 "**Question 4 — Part A**\n\nEnter the rectangular equation: {{field-1}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405494 1072 #uuid "8d094c68-435b-5c9d-8f3e-bca9fd89d7cf" 17592186405038] [17592186405494 1074 "Squaring and adding gives $x^2+y^2=25(\\sin^2t+\\cos^2t)=25$. The parameter interval traces the whole circle." 17592186405015] [17592186405495 1062 #uuid "8d8f61ec-4fb2-5e1f-843d-42b3b3e84cbb" 17592186405038] [17592186405495 1063 17592186405308 17592186405038] [17592186405496 1058 17592186405287 17592186405038] [17592186405496 1062 #uuid "8e174821-d7a3-543c-98e4-7b7dee412e81" 17592186405038] [17592186405496 1063 17592186405126 17592186405038] [17592186405497 1089 17592186049465 17592186405015] [17592186405497 1089 17592186050540 17592186405015] [17592186405497 1090 17592186405288 17592186405038] [17592186405497 1091 #uuid "8e190f48-fc64-5493-80e4-c984ed8e0404" 17592186405038] [17592186405498 1058 17592186405584 17592186405038] [17592186405498 1062 #uuid "8e1f95e1-ad50-5afa-add5-2b04b1e47392" 17592186405038] [17592186405498 1063 17592186405616 17592186405038] [17592186405499 1058 17592186405426 17592186405038] [17592186405499 1062 #uuid "8e2a8e32-f1c0-576d-85b8-630cda12606a" 17592186405038] [17592186405499 1063 17592186405540 17592186405038] [17592186405500 1058 17592186405091 17592186405038] [17592186405500 1062 #uuid "8f2ed886-cf0a-5202-b660-8448841e72b1" 17592186405038] [17592186405500 1063 17592186405619 17592186405038] [17592186405501 1071 "**Question 6 — Part A**\n\nShow that $\\langle\\varphi_z,0,-\\varphi_x\\rangle$ and $\\langle0,\\varphi_z,-\\varphi_y\\rangle$ are tangent to the level surface at $(x,y,z)$." 17592186405038] [17592186405501 1072 #uuid "8f3b824d-a891-57ed-abdc-50d8dec94ac5" 17592186405038] [17592186405501 1074 "Assume $\\varphi_z\\ne0$ and parametrize the surface locally by $\\mathbf r(x,y)=\\langle x,y,z(x,y)\\rangle$. Its coordinate tangent vectors are $\\mathbf r_x=\\langle1,0,-\\varphi_x/\\varphi_z\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf r_y=\\langle0,1,-\\varphi_y/\\varphi_z\\rangle$. Multiplying each by $\\varphi_z$ gives the two stated tangent vectors.\n\nThe first tangent follows a curve with $y$ fixed, and the second follows one with $x$ fixed. When $\\varphi_z\\ne0$, they are independent and span the tangent plane. If that partial derivative vanishes at a regular point, choose another coordinate with nonzero partial derivative for the local parametrization." 17592186405015] [17592186405502 1058 17592186405341 17592186405038] [17592186405502 1062 #uuid "8f76091d-f246-5df7-8bbb-913f84db6e5d" 17592186405038] [17592186405502 1063 17592186405285 17592186405038] [17592186405503 1062 #uuid "8ffc03a7-3cdf-53c0-a513-2906efa6f95e" 17592186405038] [17592186405503 1063 17592186405164 17592186405038] [17592186405504 1058 17592186405306 17592186405038] [17592186405504 1062 #uuid "901b49af-c35f-5c52-8f93-0a3d38d35191" 17592186405038] [17592186405504 1063 17592186405175 17592186405038] [17592186405505 1085 17592186405050 17592186405038] [17592186405505 1085 17592186405082 17592186405038] [17592186405505 1085 17592186405393 17592186405038] [17592186405505 1085 17592186405643 17592186405038] [17592186405505 1086 "Source question: 5(a)–(d), PDF page 6.\n\nUse Problem 4(b) to express each field entirely in $r,\\theta,\\hat{\\mathbf r},\\hat{\\boldsymbol\\theta}$. Treat the unit vectors as directions of increasing r and θ and reflect on whether the results make sense in the field plots." 17592186405038] [17592186405505 1087 #uuid "90720a6d-ddb7-5303-9b25-3a4c14c8c708" 17592186405038] [17592186405505 1088 17592186405393 17592186405038] [17592186405506 1058 17592186405098 17592186405038] [17592186405506 1062 #uuid "90e45460-8965-502c-a428-0c6e1c796da2" 17592186405038] [17592186405506 1063 17592186405129 17592186405038] [17592186405507 1058 17592186405817 17592186405038] [17592186405507 1062 #uuid "911156e2-edae-51e9-affa-fc1bf558f66c" 17592186405038] [17592186405507 1063 17592186405076 17592186405038] [17592186405508 1085 17592186405413 17592186405038] [17592186405508 1085 17592186405706 17592186405038] [17592186405508 1086 "Source question: 4.\n\nFor $x(t)=t^3-144t$ and $y(t)=2t^2$, find all points with horizontal or vertical tangent lines. Give exact coordinate pairs as a comma-separated list; enter `DNE` if there are none." 17592186405038] [17592186405508 1087 #uuid "91143f11-89c8-563a-afa3-9a3d2e8b071c" 17592186405038] [17592186405508 1088 17592186405706 17592186405038] [17592186405509 1068 17592186406277 17592186405015] [17592186405509 1071 "**Question 2 — Part A**\n\n$y'(x)=x^4,\\quad y(0)=5$.\n\n$y(x)=$ {{field-1}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405509 1072 #uuid "9188c534-eecb-580b-8ca2-b0d089a1834b" 17592186405038] [17592186405509 1074 "Integrating $x^4$ gives $x^5/5+C$. The value at $0$ fixes $C=5$. Differentiating the result recovers $x^4$." 17592186405015] [17592186405510 1089 17592186048684 17592186405015] [17592186405510 1089 17592186050796 17592186405015] [17592186405510 1090 17592186405197 17592186405038] [17592186405510 1091 #uuid "91b0e312-e352-505f-b5cd-b2d66830b9f6" 17592186405038] [17592186405511 1068 17592186406231 17592186405015] [17592186405511 1071 "Source question: 12.\n\nFind $\\iint_S(x+4y)dS$ for $\\mathbf r(u,v)=\\langle6u\\cos v,u^2+1,4u\\cos v\\rangle$, $0\\le u\\le1$, $0\\le v\\le\\pi/2$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405511 1072 #uuid "922ce6de-c171-533d-adb5-9b6fea505dad" 17592186405038] [17592186405511 1074 "$r_u=(6\\cos v,2u,4\\cos v)$ and $r_v=(-6u\\sin v,0,-4u\\sin v)$. Their cross product is $(-8u^2\\sin v,0,12u^2\\sin v)$, with norm $4\\sqrt{13}u^2\\sin v$. The integrand is $6u\\cos v+4u^2+4$. Integrate in v, then u: $4\\sqrt{13}\\int_0^1u^2(3u+4u^2+4)du=4\\sqrt{13}(3/4+4/5+4/3)$." 17592186405015] [17592186405512 1062 #uuid "930ae6f2-4bf3-519b-ae21-b3c9e2fd0053" 17592186405038] [17592186405512 1063 17592186405311 17592186405038] [17592186405513 1058 17592186405200 17592186405038] [17592186405513 1062 #uuid "939ea94e-30fe-5d0c-ae7f-470d9ce06020" 17592186405038] [17592186405513 1063 17592186405427 17592186405038] [17592186405514 1089 17592186051076 17592186405015] [17592186405514 1090 17592186405719 17592186405038] [17592186405514 1091 #uuid "93bd2316-ff02-5292-9fc0-a0d01f43d04b" 17592186405038] [17592186405515 1089 17592186048684 17592186405015] [17592186405515 1089 17592186050796 17592186405015] [17592186405515 1090 17592186405141 17592186405038] [17592186405515 1091 #uuid "94273f38-45f8-5317-8c4a-f76b39a4c502" 17592186405038] [17592186405516 1058 17592186405257 17592186405038] [17592186405516 1062 #uuid "94556729-3a5a-5d93-9aad-a34d17641f08" 17592186405038] [17592186405516 1063 17592186405399 17592186405038] [17592186405517 1068 17592186406034 17592186405015] [17592186405517 1071 "Source question: 7.\n\nUse the divergence theorem to find the outward flux of $\\mathbf F=\\langle5yz,3xz,-9xy\\rangle$ through the tetrahedron with vertices (0,0,0), (5,0,0), (0,5,0), and (0,0,5).\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405517 1072 #uuid "94b74c00-d8ab-51c7-b89c-2d184ff5338a" 17592186405038] [17592186405517 1074 "P is independent of x, Q of y, and R of z, so divergence is 0 everywhere. Its integral over the tetrahedron is therefore 0. The field is smooth on the entire solid." 17592186405015] [17592186405518 1089 17592186049465 17592186405015] [17592186405518 1090 17592186405292 17592186405038] [17592186405518 1091 #uuid "94c02ce9-9caa-555b-8d4f-cebbdebe4335" 17592186405038] [17592186405519 1058 17592186405065 17592186405038] [17592186405519 1062 #uuid "94cc26c8-59ff-519f-9c03-2046cdc07976" 17592186405038] [17592186405519 1063 17592186405592 17592186405038] [17592186405520 1058 17592186405116 17592186405038] [17592186405520 1062 #uuid "9598117e-5af4-5ed5-b6c5-13bb07d6c4e1" 17592186405038] [17592186405520 1063 17592186405804 17592186405038] [17592186405521 1068 17592186406028 17592186405015] [17592186405521 1071 "Source question: 10.\n\nUse **Green’s theorem** to find the work of $\\mathbf F=5x\\mathbf i+2y^2\\mathbf j$ along $x=t,y=t^2$, $0\\le t\\le3$, then back to (0,0) along $x=t,y=3t$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405521 1072 #uuid "96e3d9de-8d87-5c0f-8f11-82863c4ad4c0" 17592186405038] [17592186405521 1074 "The return line is traversed with t decreasing from 3 to 0. Green’s theorem gives $\\iint_R(Q_x-P_y)dA=0$, since both cross partials vanish. A check is the global potential $5x^2/2+2y^3/3$: its net change around the closed path is zero." 17592186405015] [17592186405522 1085 17592186405554 17592186405038] [17592186405522 1085 17592186405685 17592186405038] [17592186405522 1085 17592186405775 17592186405038] [17592186405522 1085 17592186405792 17592186405038] [17592186405522 1086 "Source question: 2.\n\nSolving initial value problems was an important application of integration from MTH 252. Recall that the function $y(x)$ satisfying\n\n$$\ny'(x)=f(x),\\qquad y(x_0)=a\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\ny(x)=a+\\int_{x_0}^x f(s)\\,ds.\n$$\n\nThe function $y(x)$ is known as the solution to the initial value problem. Solve the following initial value problems:" 17592186405038] [17592186405522 1087 #uuid "9768ff19-fbdb-57cb-98b2-c90310055446" 17592186405038] [17592186405522 1088 17592186405685 17592186405038] [17592186405523 1089 17592186048923 17592186405015] [17592186405523 1089 17592186048960 17592186405015] [17592186405523 1089 17592186049366 17592186405015] [17592186405523 1089 17592186050033 17592186405015] [17592186405523 1090 17592186405610 17592186405038] [17592186405523 1091 #uuid "987120e0-4212-5cdb-bc05-9d156e59f9de" 17592186405038] [17592186405524 1058 17592186405769 17592186405038] [17592186405524 1062 #uuid "99076efb-018d-59e1-aa5e-87e3037beab1" 17592186405038] [17592186405524 1063 17592186405165 17592186405038] [17592186405525 1068 17592186406026 17592186405015] [17592186405525 1068 17592186406316 17592186405015] [17592186405525 1071 "**Question 1 — Part C**\n\nIn which direction does the voltage increase/decrease most rapidly at $P$?\n\nUnit direction of fastest increase: {{field-1}}.\n\nUnit direction of fastest decrease: {{field-2}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405525 1072 #uuid "993ff6df-13ee-5888-9f6a-38f0ce68a202" 17592186405038] [17592186405525 1074 "Normalize $\\langle12,0,16\\rangle$ by its magnitude $20$ for increase. Negate it for decrease. Their directional derivatives are respectively $20$ and $-20$." 17592186405015] [17592186405526 1068 17592186406096 17592186405015] [17592186405526 1071 "**(a)** Given $\\nabla f=3x\\mathbf i+8y\\mathbf j$, find f. Use K for the arbitrary constant and absorb into K as much as possible.\n\n$f(x,y)=$ {{potential}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405526 1072 #uuid "995cbe4d-53fa-547b-aaf6-3f7270f7c624" 17592186405038] [17592186405526 1074 "Integrate 3x in x, then match the y derivative to 8y. The two partial derivatives of $3x^2/2+4y^2+K$ are exactly 3x and 8y." 17592186405015] [17592186405527 1085 17592186405205 17592186405038] [17592186405527 1085 17592186405221 17592186405038] [17592186405527 1085 17592186405243 17592186405038] [17592186405527 1086 "Source question: 1.\n\n**Area of a parallelogram**\n\nConsider the parallelogram formed by $\\mathbf u$ and $\\mathbf v$. Let $\\theta$ be the angle between them.\n\n![Parallelogram formed by u and v with included angle theta](images/ca/ca9c54ad7c41bea1e72278f7d24742fbde8a4f6a4abf46b7ddadf557dad7bae4.png)" 17592186405038] [17592186405527 1087 #uuid "99c3ae3a-448c-526b-96b7-0c561f1a8187" 17592186405038] [17592186405527 1088 17592186405243 17592186405038] [17592186405528 1071 "**(b)** Using $\\hat{\\mathbf r}=\\cos\\theta\\,\\mathbf i+\\sin\\theta\\,\\mathbf j$ and $\\hat{\\boldsymbol\\theta}=-\\sin\\theta\\,\\mathbf i+\\cos\\theta\\,\\mathbf j$, prove\n\n$$\\mathbf i=\\cos\\theta\\,\\hat{\\mathbf r}-\\sin\\theta\\,\\hat{\\boldsymbol\\theta},\\qquad\\mathbf j=\\sin\\theta\\,\\hat{\\mathbf r}+\\cos\\theta\\,\\hat{\\boldsymbol\\theta}.$$" 17592186405038] [17592186405528 1072 #uuid "99fc74a6-7d3f-54e2-a15c-0c3e107e6a51" 17592186405038] [17592186405528 1074 "**proof:** Expand the first right-hand side: $(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)\\mathbf i+(\\cos\\theta\\sin\\theta-\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta)\\mathbf j=\\mathbf i$. Expand the second: $(\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta-\\cos\\theta\\sin\\theta)\\mathbf i+(\\sin^2\\theta+\\cos^2\\theta)\\mathbf j=\\mathbf j$.\n\nSubstitute the given basis vectors and collect Cartesian components. Mixed terms cancel and squared sine/cosine terms sum to 1." 17592186405015] [17592186405529 1068 17592186405863 17592186405015] [17592186405529 1071 "Source question: 10.\n\nFind $\\iint_R10x\\,dA$ over the ellipse $x^2/16+y^2/49\\le1$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405529 1072 #uuid "9a124eab-9d1a-503a-b9a6-310f8f6a3adb" 17592186405038] [17592186405529 1074 "Use x=4u,y=7v, so the region is the unit disk and dA=28du dv. The transformed integrand is 1120u, odd across u=0; symmetric halves cancel." 17592186405015] [17592186405530 1071 "**Question 1 — Part C**\n\nLet $\\mathbf u=\\langle u_1,u_2,u_3\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf v=\\langle v_1,v_2,v_3\\rangle$. Show that the area is\n\n$$A=\\left\\lVert\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf i&\\mathbf j&\\mathbf k\\\\u_1&u_2&u_3\\\\v_1&v_2&v_3\\end{vmatrix}\\right\\rVert.$$" 17592186405038] [17592186405530 1072 #uuid "9a2e296b-a844-5cc3-9353-12489716d182" 17592186405038] [17592186405530 1074 "Expanding along the first row gives the vector $\\langle u_2v_3-u_3v_2,\\ u_3v_1-u_1v_3,\\ u_1v_2-u_2v_1\\rangle=\\mathbf u\\times\\mathbf v$. Its magnitude is the area by Part B. Equivalently, $A=\\sqrt{(u_2v_3-u_3v_2)^2+(u_3v_1-u_1v_3)^2+(u_1v_2-u_2v_1)^2}$.\n\nThe determinant with basis vectors in its first row is cross-product notation, so its expansion is a vector. Keep the minus sign in the middle cofactor and take the vector magnitude to obtain area." 17592186405015] [17592186405531 1071 "Source exercise: **1.2.3**, [printed page 42 (PDF page 54)](/api/source?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F341%2FKuttler-LinearAlgebra-AFirstCourse-2017A.pdf#page=54).\n\n**Question 6**\n\nDo the three lines\n\n$$\nx+2y=1, \\qquad 2x-y=1, \\qquad 4x+3y=3\n$$\n\nhave a common point of intersection? If so, find the point; if not, tell why they do not have such a common point of intersection." 17592186405038] [17592186405531 1072 #uuid "9a3ab431-ea46-5c56-b51b-2479efd1cb3f" 17592186405038] [17592186405531 1074 "Yes. All three lines meet at $\\left(\\frac35,\\frac15\\right)$.\n\nFrom $x+2y=1$, write $x=1-2y$. Substitute into $2x-y=1$:\n\n$$2(1-2y)-y=1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad 2-5y=1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad y=\\frac15.$$\n\nThen $x=1-2/5=3/5$. Check the remaining equation:\n\n$$4\\left(\\frac35\\right)+3\\left(\\frac15\\right)=\\frac{12}{5}+\\frac35=3.$$\n\nThe point obtained from the first two lines lies on the third, so it is common to all three.\n\nSolving two equations gives a candidate, not yet a solution to a three-equation system. Always substitute the candidate into the remaining equation. Here it satisfies the third equation as well. The first two lines are distinct and have only one intersection, so the verified common point is unique." 17592186405015] [17592186405532 1068 17592186406139 17592186405015] [17592186405532 1071 "Let S be the surface of a solid E satisfying the assumptions of the divergence theorem. With outward normal n, choose the correct statement." 17592186405015] [17592186405532 1072 #uuid "9a68a410-ec32-5adf-8fc2-00eddc758300" 17592186405038] [17592186405532 1074 "The divergence theorem gives each outward flux as 30·Volume(E), so they are equal for every eligible solid. Different component functions need not produce different total fluxes when their divergences agree." 17592186405015] [17592186405533 1068 17592186406056 17592186405015] [17592186405533 1071 "Source question: 6.\n\nFind $\\int_C(2x+7y)\\,ds$ for $x=\\sin(5t),\\ y=3\\sin(5t),\\ 0\\le t\\le\\pi/5$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405533 1072 #uuid "9a9e3065-d6b9-5dba-9912-3e722a0b62c5" 17592186405038] [17592186405533 1074 "The speed is $5\\sqrt{10}|\\cos(5t)|$ and the integrand is 23sin(5t). With u=5t, $I=23\\sqrt{10}\\int_0^\\pi\\sin u|\\cos u|du=23\\sqrt{10}$. Split at π/2: each half contributes 1/2. The path retraces the segment, so both passes count." 17592186405015] [17592186405534 1058 17592186405608 17592186405038] [17592186405534 1062 #uuid "9b33b557-7783-5bc2-917b-0a8f37b681f7" 17592186405038] [17592186405534 1063 17592186405803 17592186405038] [17592186405535 1089 17592186050514 17592186405015] [17592186405535 1090 17592186405511 17592186405038] [17592186405535 1091 #uuid "9b51bdeb-0f77-5a56-9bd5-478e0198a655" 17592186405038] [17592186405536 1058 17592186405680 17592186405038] [17592186405536 1062 #uuid "9c27f6c1-416a-5ffd-ab9a-81ca3d03aef3" 17592186405038] [17592186405536 1063 17592186405571 17592186405038] [17592186405537 1089 17592186048239 17592186405015] [17592186405537 1090 17592186405642 17592186405038] [17592186405537 1091 #uuid "9c527199-c32b-542b-943b-e54d5d894c30" 17592186405038] [17592186405538 1089 17592186048575 17592186405015] [17592186405538 1089 17592186048819 17592186405015] [17592186405538 1089 17592186049386 17592186405015] [17592186405538 1090 17592186405678 17592186405038] [17592186405538 1091 #uuid "9cbeb7a6-4f1e-5970-bfb6-6da8c0933dee" 17592186405038] [17592186405539 1058 17592186405745 17592186405038] [17592186405539 1062 #uuid "9d185b86-e154-5afc-868b-25f1b429b40e" 17592186405038] [17592186405539 1063 17592186405640 17592186405038] [17592186405540 1071 "**(a)** Confirm that the given curve is a flow curve for the field.\n\n$\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle\\cos t,\\sin t,e^t\\rangle$, $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)=\\langle-y,x,z\\rangle$." 17592186405038] [17592186405540 1072 #uuid "9d24bd49-2c8c-554c-82b9-08aca2bb217e" 17592186405038] [17592186405540 1074 "**verification:** $\\mathbf r\\prime(t)=\\langle-\\sin t,\\cos t,e^t\\rangle=\\mathbf F(\\cos t,\\sin t,e^t)$ for every real t.\n\nDifferentiate each coordinate of r and separately substitute r into F. Equality of all three components proves $\\mathbf r\\prime=\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r)$ on the stated domain." 17592186405015] [17592186405541 1068 17592186406116 17592186405015] [17592186405541 1071 "[Open in WeBWorK](https://webwork2.science.oregonstate.edu/webwork2/MTH_256_020_F2026/WebWork_1/6)\n\n**Question 6**\n\nFind the function $y(t)$ that satisfies the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}-2ty=15t^2e^{t^2}\n$$\n\nand the condition $y(0)=2$.\n\n$y(t)=$ {{field-1}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405541 1072 #uuid "9d298f94-7673-5f4f-9204-6d79fb43db38" 17592186405038] [17592186405541 1074 "Multiply by $e^{-t^2}$ to get $(e^{-t^2}y)\\prime=15t^2$. Integration gives $e^{-t^2}y=5t^3+C$, and $y(0)=2$ gives $C=2$. Differentiating the final expression cancels the $2ty$ term and leaves $15t^2e^{t^2}$." 17592186405015] [17592186405542 1068 17592186406102 17592186405015] [17592186405542 1071 "Source question: 9.\n\nFind $\\iiint_E(x+3)\\,dV$ inside $x^2/16+y^2/25+z^2/36=1$. Use $u=x/4$, $v=y/5$, $w=z/6$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405542 1072 #uuid "9dc2d24e-6f5e-5f85-ac98-621c35f81b07" 17592186405038] [17592186405542 1074 "The ellipsoid becomes the unit ball. The inverse map (4u,5v,6w) has determinant 120, so integrate $120(4u+3)$. The odd u term vanishes; the constant contributes $360\\cdot4\\pi/3=480\\pi$." 17592186405015] [17592186405543 1089 17592186050149 17592186405015] [17592186405543 1089 17592186050891 17592186405015] [17592186405543 1089 17592186051090 17592186405015] [17592186405543 1090 17592186405400 17592186405038] [17592186405543 1091 #uuid "9e8e7c89-c457-505e-8c6f-873fe67255ac" 17592186405038] [17592186405544 1071 "**Question 2 — Part C**\n\nLet $\\mathbf u=\\langle u_1,u_2,u_3\\rangle$, $\\mathbf v=\\langle v_1,v_2,v_3\\rangle$, and $\\mathbf w=\\langle w_1,w_2,w_3\\rangle$. Show that the volume is\n\n$$V=\\left|\\det\\begin{pmatrix}w_1&w_2&w_3\\\\u_1&u_2&u_3\\\\v_1&v_2&v_3\\end{pmatrix}\\right|.$$" 17592186405038] [17592186405544 1072 #uuid "9ebd1eb7-31ef-51b3-b552-fe69f2171cfa" 17592186405038] [17592186405544 1074 "The scalar triple product expands to $w_1(u_2v_3-u_3v_2)+w_2(u_3v_1-u_1v_3)+w_3(u_1v_2-u_2v_1)$. This is the first-row cofactor expansion of the determinant with rows $\\mathbf w,\\mathbf u,\\mathbf v$. Taking its absolute value gives the volume from Part B.\n\nUnlike the determinant notation in Problem 1, all entries here are scalars, so the determinant is a scalar. Check the alternating cofactor signs and retain the outer absolute value." 17592186405015] [17592186405545 1058 17592186405449 17592186405038] [17592186405545 1062 #uuid "9efe47ab-9494-5878-a7df-71ccedd3dca6" 17592186405038] [17592186405545 1063 17592186405201 17592186405038] [17592186405546 1085 17592186405495 17592186405038] [17592186405546 1085 17592186405633 17592186405038] [17592186405546 1086 "Source question: 9.\n\nConsider the spiral $x(t)=t\\cos t$, $y(t)=t\\sin t$, for $0\\le t\\le5\\pi$." 17592186405038] [17592186405546 1087 #uuid "9f56feff-ea76-5578-9e9e-96bddae8fa6a" 17592186405038] [17592186405546 1088 17592186405633 17592186405038] [17592186405547 1089 17592186049536 17592186405015] [17592186405547 1089 17592186049645 17592186405015] [17592186405547 1090 17592186405542 17592186405038] [17592186405547 1091 #uuid "9fece06b-e7a7-55f5-890a-4c1d9b372215" 17592186405038] [17592186405548 1089 17592186049768 17592186405015] [17592186405548 1089 17592186051091 17592186405015] [17592186405548 1090 17592186405312 17592186405038] [17592186405548 1091 #uuid "a02928b4-af79-5c8e-b095-b3c99d652102" 17592186405038] [17592186405549 1068 17592186406247 17592186405015] [17592186405549 1071 "Source question: 13.\n\nA thin wire forms the helix $x=6\\cos t,\\ y=6\\sin t,\\ z=t$. Find the mass M of four turns with constant density $\\rho$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405549 1072 #uuid "a0b0625f-905d-5d2b-80d5-9c8364e8d7e0" 17592186405038] [17592186405549 1074 "The speed is $\\sqrt{36\\sin^2t+36\\cos^2t+1}=\\sqrt{37}$. Four turns span 8π in t. Thus mass equals density times length: $\\rho\\sqrt{37}(8\\pi)$." 17592186405015] [17592186405550 1068 17592186406219 17592186405015] [17592186405550 1071 "**Question 2 — Part D**\n\n$y'(x)=\\dfrac{1}{x},\\quad y(1)=3$.\n\n$y(x)=$ {{field-1}}, on $x>0$." 17592186405015] [17592186405550 1072 #uuid "a14906b0-4d4b-5a16-b145-872851409e4e" 17592186405038] [17592186405550 1074 "Integrate to $\\ln|x|+C$. The interval containing the initial point $x=1$ is positive, so use $\\ln x$. At $1$, the logarithm is zero and $C=3$. The solution cannot cross the singular point $x=0$." 17592186405015] [17592186405551 1071 "**Question 1 — Part A**\n\nShow that its height is $\\lVert\\mathbf v\\rVert\\sin\\theta$." 17592186405038] [17592186405551 1072 #uuid "a17bf913-7f21-5bd1-ab10-c2d76180be2b" 17592186405038] [17592186405551 1074 "Drop a perpendicular from the tip of $\\mathbf v$ to the line along $\\mathbf u$. In the resulting right triangle, $\\sin\\theta=h/\\lVert\\mathbf v\\rVert$ for the acute angle shown. Thus $h=\\lVert\\mathbf v\\rVert\\sin\\theta$. For an obtuse included angle, the right triangle uses $\\pi-\\theta$ and gives the same result.\n\nThe height is the component of $\\mathbf v$ perpendicular to the base, not its full length. Identify the hypotenuse and opposite side in your triangle; $\\sin(\\pi-\\theta)=\\sin\\theta$ covers an obtuse included angle." 17592186405015] [17592186405552 1058 17592186405395 17592186405038] [17592186405552 1062 #uuid "a19a6ae5-44a9-5b49-85b0-fa030fced37c" 17592186405038] [17592186405552 1063 17592186405421 17592186405038] [17592186405553 1089 17592186048960 17592186405015] [17592186405553 1089 17592186049859 17592186405015] [17592186405553 1090 17592186405835 17592186405038] [17592186405553 1091 #uuid "a1e9759d-fc40-5f0e-a2c6-c17d15de281a" 17592186405038] [17592186405554 1058 17592186405775 17592186405038] [17592186405554 1062 #uuid "a23e4c72-cbd6-54c2-8d0a-76055109f351" 17592186405038] [17592186405554 1063 17592186405791 17592186405038] [17592186405555 1058 17592186405110 17592186405038] [17592186405555 1062 #uuid "a2596300-d6d9-5edb-b67a-d8e367612e2e" 17592186405038] [17592186405555 1063 17592186405225 17592186405038] [17592186405556 1085 17592186405512 17592186405038] [17592186405556 1085 17592186405656 17592186405038] [17592186405556 1085 17592186405831 17592186405038] [17592186405556 1086 "Source question: 6.\n\n**The 3D gradient is orthogonal to a level surface**\n\nLet $\\varphi(x,y,z)$ be a scalar function with level surface $\\varphi(x,y,z)=c$. From Problem 5, $\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}=-\\dfrac{\\varphi_x}{\\varphi_z}$ and $\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}=-\\dfrac{\\varphi_y}{\\varphi_z}$." 17592186405038] [17592186405556 1087 #uuid "a275c6a2-50ca-57bf-9cae-18b542d4439e" 17592186405038] [17592186405556 1088 17592186405656 17592186405038] [17592186405557 1062 #uuid "a2befd69-b641-58ff-a358-ba83948714b1" 17592186405038] [17592186405557 1063 17592186405345 17592186405038] [17592186405558 1089 17592186050295 17592186405015] [17592186405558 1089 17592186050866 17592186405015] [17592186405558 1090 17592186405072 17592186405038] [17592186405558 1091 #uuid "a2c08e18-dfdb-5bc7-aeb8-d0dc880d78d0" 17592186405038] [17592186405559 1068 17592186406170 17592186405015] [17592186405559 1071 "Source question: 10.\n\nFind the curl of $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)=\\langle3y+z,4z+9x,5z+6y+6x\\rangle$. \n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405559 1072 #uuid "a2e486ac-4d3a-5749-80c9-96a2d9a74db9" 17592186405038] [17592186405559 1074 "The curl components are $R_y-Q_z=6-4=2$, $P_z-R_x=1-6=-5$, and $Q_x-P_y=9-3=6$." 17592186405015] [17592186405560 1071 "**Question 4 — Part C**\n\nA curve that is not an integral curve." 17592186405038] [17592186405560 1072 #uuid "a3ad5074-7608-5a0b-b227-e249a8477dc7" 17592186405038] [17592186405560 1074 "Draw a short horizontal segment near $(0,0)$ and label it “not an integral curve.” The supplied field is nearly vertical in that neighborhood, so the segment visibly cuts across the direction marks.\n\nThe task only needs one counterexample. Choose a region where the mismatch between the segment’s tangent and the field is clear; no inferred differential equation is necessary." 17592186405015] [17592186405561 1089 17592186049487 17592186405015] [17592186405561 1089 17592186050537 17592186405015] [17592186405561 1089 17592186050866 17592186405015] [17592186405561 1090 17592186405622 17592186405038] [17592186405561 1091 #uuid "a5247ff6-ad16-5126-b1a0-b5705bcb9b50" 17592186405038] [17592186405562 1071 "**Question 3 — Part B**\n\nWhat does part (a) imply about $e^xy$?" 17592186405038] [17592186405562 1072 #uuid "a55f2244-ab74-5fa5-bc3c-af255c731687" 17592186405038] [17592186405562 1074 "The product $e^xy$ is constant: $e^xy=C$.\n\nA differentiable function with derivative zero throughout an interval is constant on that interval. That constant is determined by the initial condition, not assumed to be zero." 17592186405015] [17592186405563 1085 17592186405127 17592186405038] [17592186405563 1085 17592186405442 17592186405038] [17592186405563 1085 17592186405493 17592186405038] [17592186405563 1086 "Source question: 2(a)–(c), PDF page 3.\n\nLet $\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\mathbf r/|\\mathbf r|=\\langle x,y\\rangle/\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}$, $\\eta(x,y)=x^2+y^2$, and $C: x^2+y^2=a^2$, with $a>0$." 17592186405038] [17592186405563 1087 #uuid "a5643d60-a09c-5b5b-995a-c6199fd64e65" 17592186405038] [17592186405563 1088 17592186405442 17592186405038] [17592186405564 1085 17592186405458 17592186405038] [17592186405564 1085 17592186405794 17592186405038] [17592186405564 1086 "Source question: 6.\n\nConsider $x(t)=2\\sin t-3$ and $y(t)=2\\cos t+1$, with $0\\le t\\le\\pi$. Use exact values." 17592186405038] [17592186405564 1087 #uuid "a58eb7e9-eb11-5616-9332-9ba68bbd43cc" 17592186405038] [17592186405564 1088 17592186405794 17592186405038] [17592186405565 1089 17592186048152 17592186405015] [17592186405565 1089 17592186049536 17592186405015] [17592186405565 1090 17592186405376 17592186405038] [17592186405565 1091 #uuid "a59584ce-7ec6-5dfd-ab21-b06207738b61" 17592186405038] [17592186405566 1068 17592186406208 17592186405015] [17592186405566 1071 "Source question: 7.\n\nFind $\\oint_C\\frac{y\\,dx-x\\,dy}{x^2+y^2}$ about $C:(x-8)^2+(y+7)^2=16$. Enter DNE if it does not exist.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405566 1072 #uuid "a5c60846-91e6-5bc4-ba34-d07324603b64" 17592186405038] [17592186405566 1074 "The disk has center (8,−7) and radius 4. The origin is outside, since its distance from the center is √113>4. The field is smooth on the disk and $Q_x-P_y=0$ there, so Green’s theorem gives zero. This value is unchanged by reversing orientation." 17592186405015] [17592186405567 1068 17592186405998 17592186405015] [17592186405567 1071 "Source question: 7.\n\nFind the curl of $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)=\\langle e^{2x},3x^8y,6e^z\\rangle$. \n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405567 1072 #uuid "a603a5c6-eb6f-5dc3-998c-2c4eb901a1f2" 17592186405038] [17592186405567 1074 "The first two curl components vanish. The third is $Q_x-P_y=24x^7y-0$. The x derivative of the first component belongs to divergence, not curl." 17592186405015] [17592186405568 1068 17592186406332 17592186405015] [17592186405568 1071 "Source exercise: **1.2.2**, [printed page 42 (PDF page 54)](/api/source?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F341%2FKuttler-LinearAlgebra-AFirstCourse-2017A.pdf#page=54).\n\n**Question 5**\n\nFind the point of intersection of the two lines\n\n$$\n3x+y=3 \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad x+2y=1.\n$$\n\nEnter the exact ordered pair $(x,y)$: {{field-1}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405568 1072 #uuid "a7d59dcf-2026-53bb-9f01-204b0bb6cd64" 17592186405038] [17592186405568 1074 "Eliminate $y$ to find the point satisfying both equations. Twice the first equation is $6x+2y=6$; subtracting $x+2y=1$ gives $5x=5$. Hence $x=1$, and $y=3-3x=0$. The point $(1,0)$ satisfies $3x+y=3$ and $x+2y=1$. A zero coordinate is valid: it means this intersection lies on the x-axis." 17592186405015] [17592186405569 1058 17592186405059 17592186405038] [17592186405569 1062 #uuid "a841ee07-9a09-556f-8619-b004b9cd249f" 17592186405038] [17592186405569 1063 17592186405347 17592186405038] [17592186405570 1068 17592186406243 17592186405015] [17592186405570 1071 "Source question: 1.\n\nFind $\\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf F$ for $\\mathbf F=\\langle5x^2,4y^2,9z^6\\rangle$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405570 1072 #uuid "a9c6ae11-f1b3-5459-a01e-7d9e7b21330f" 17592186405038] [17592186405570 1074 "Divergence adds P_x+Q_y+R_z. Differentiate each component with respect to its matching coordinate: 10x, 8y, and 54z⁵." 17592186405015] [17592186405571 1071 "**Question 1 — Part B**\n\nExpress the following in terms of $y(x)$ and $y'(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{d}{dx}\\bigl(\\sin(x)\\,y(x)\\bigr).\n$$" 17592186405038] [17592186405571 1072 #uuid "aa053063-e2ae-5112-ac07-5d463e12648f" 17592186405038] [17592186405571 1074 "$\\frac{d}{dx}(\\sin x\\,y(x))=\\cos x\\,y(x)+\\sin x\\,y\\prime(x)$.\n\nTreat $y(x)$ as a differentiable function, not a constant. Differentiate each factor once and retain the undifferentiated partner in each term." 17592186405015] [17592186405572 1058 17592186405534 17592186405038] [17592186405572 1062 #uuid "aa1cb56f-f5cf-54cb-acd9-960b646cf6c1" 17592186405038] [17592186405572 1063 17592186405137 17592186405038] [17592186405573 1071 "**(c)** Find every point at which the field is orthogonal or tangent to the curve.\n\n$\\mathbf F=\\langle y,x\\rangle$, $C: x^2+y^2=4$." 17592186405038] [17592186405573 1072 #uuid "aa8cad15-5ba4-5bc8-8d05-7b4753877e98" 17592186405038] [17592186405573 1074 "**tangent-and-orthogonal:** Tangent: $(\\pm2,0),(0,\\pm2)$. Orthogonal: all four $(\\pm\\sqrt2,\\pm\\sqrt2)$ with signs independent.\n\nUse the nonzero tangent $T=(-y,x)$. Tangency requires the determinant $2xy=0$; intersect with the circle to get its four axis intercepts. Orthogonality requires $F\\cdot T=x^2-y^2=0$; then x²=y²=2." 17592186405015] [17592186405574 1058 17592186405761 17592186405038] [17592186405574 1062 #uuid "ab1c905b-da4f-5f94-b465-16a2a0f86f44" 17592186405038] [17592186405574 1063 17592186405066 17592186405038] [17592186405575 1043 17592186405115 17592186405038] [17592186405575 1043 17592186405173 17592186405038] [17592186405575 1043 17592186405190 17592186405038] [17592186405575 1043 17592186405244 17592186405038] [17592186405575 1043 17592186405287 17592186405038] [17592186405575 1043 17592186405356 17592186405038] [17592186405575 1043 17592186405380 17592186405038] [17592186405575 1043 17592186405428 17592186405038] [17592186405575 1043 17592186405496 17592186405038] [17592186405575 1043 17592186405539 17592186405038] [17592186405575 1043 17592186405663 17592186405038] [17592186405575 1043 17592186405677 17592186405038] [17592186405575 1043 17592186405745 17592186405038] [17592186405575 1044 17592186045461 17592186405038] [17592186405575 1045 "MTH 255 — OHW-9" 17592186405038] [17592186405575 1046 17592186405496 17592186405038] [17592186405575 1047 "2026-11-20T07:59:59.000Z" 17592186405038] [17592186405575 1048 17592186405043 17592186405038] [17592186405575 1053 #uuid "ab722c99-bf2e-580f-9476-1c1fa08b14a6" 17592186405038] [17592186405576 1058 17592186405326 17592186405038] [17592186405576 1062 #uuid "ab78baa4-4edf-5626-9603-a8f93b978292" 17592186405038] [17592186405576 1063 17592186405722 17592186405038] [17592186405577 1085 17592186405403 17592186405038] [17592186405577 1085 17592186405524 17592186405038] [17592186405577 1085 17592186405769 17592186405038] [17592186405577 1086 "[Open in WeBWorK](https://webwork2.science.oregonstate.edu/webwork2/MTH_256_020_F2026/WebWork_1/4)\n\nIn this problem you will solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\nxy'=9y-6x,\\qquad y(1)=-4.\n$$" 17592186405038] [17592186405577 1087 #uuid "abcf5322-e31b-58c5-acc9-c0a106bdc0b3" 17592186405038] [17592186405577 1088 17592186405403 17592186405038] [17592186405578 1085 17592186405297 17592186405038] [17592186405578 1085 17592186405474 17592186405038] [17592186405578 1085 17592186405727 17592186405038] [17592186405578 1086 "Source question: 3.\n\nThe direction field for\n\n$$\ny'=\\frac{x}{y}\n$$\n\nis provided below. On the direction field sketch and label the following:\n\n![Direction field for y prime equals x over y](images/2a/2ab36389693808aef75de9ab5f665a9956c672a1a6f12c850a380991b08abbd8.png)" 17592186405038] [17592186405578 1087 #uuid "ac83bc65-e101-5c37-ab8f-09ba2fd53da9" 17592186405038] [17592186405578 1088 17592186405297 17592186405038] [17592186405579 1058 17592186405776 17592186405038] [17592186405579 1062 #uuid "ad1d40c7-63e1-532d-87c0-327d358f19d5" 17592186405038] [17592186405579 1063 17592186405136 17592186405038] [17592186405580 1062 #uuid "ad51f6d5-f3a1-55f8-bbfd-4212852f9a31" 17592186405038] [17592186405580 1063 17592186405609 17592186405038] [17592186405581 1068 17592186406125 17592186405015] [17592186405581 1071 "[Open in WeBWorK](https://webwork2.science.oregonstate.edu/webwork2/MTH_256_020_F2026/WebWork_1/1)\n\n**Question 1**\n\nFind the function satisfying the differential equation\n\n$$\ny'-2y=4e^{4t}\n$$\n\nand $y(0)=0$.\n\n$y(t)=$ {{field-1}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405581 1072 #uuid "ae1c1214-2bed-5311-babc-b4998fb888c4" 17592186405038] [17592186405581 1074 "The integrating factor is $e^{-2t}$, so $(e^{-2t}y)\\prime=4e^{2t}$. Integrating gives $e^{-2t}y=2e^{2t}+C$. At $0$, $0=2+C$, hence $C=-2$. Differentiation gives $y\\prime-2y=4e^{4t}$." 17592186405015] [17592186405582 1058 17592186405447 17592186405038] [17592186405582 1062 #uuid "aee6cbf9-38ca-5896-b505-3b58a4677d71" 17592186405038] [17592186405582 1063 17592186405565 17592186405038] [17592186405583 1068 17592186406200 17592186405015] [17592186405583 1071 "Source question: 6.\n\nEvaluate $\\oint_C(4x^3-2x^2y)dx+9xy^2dy$ counterclockwise around the region bounded by $y=x^2$ and $x=y^2$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405583 1072 #uuid "af4dbefc-eb8c-5130-9503-7e037225ec74" 17592186405038] [17592186405583 1074 "$Q_x-P_y=9y^2+2x^2$. Integrate over 0≤x≤1, x²≤y≤√x. Symmetry under swapping x and y makes both second moments equal: $\\iint_Rx^2dA=\\int_0^1(x^{5/2}-x^4)dx=3/35$. Thus the result is (9+2)3/35." 17592186405015] [17592186405584 1058 17592186405310 17592186405038] [17592186405584 1062 #uuid "af718c4b-acb0-5ded-a1bf-76dfe8c68924" 17592186405038] [17592186405584 1063 17592186405138 17592186405038] [17592186405585 1089 17592186049465 17592186405015] [17592186405585 1090 17592186405179 17592186405038] [17592186405585 1091 #uuid "af78aa19-f731-572b-b2dc-5acb664c7e73" 17592186405038] [17592186405586 1062 #uuid "afdf84c3-31ae-5e42-94a2-ed8da2cc7685" 17592186405038] [17592186405586 1063 17592186405731 17592186405038] [17592186405587 1058 17592186405124 17592186405038] [17592186405587 1062 #uuid "b02a8366-e614-5e9f-b9de-f8ed09f0073c" 17592186405038] [17592186405587 1063 17592186405748 17592186405038] [17592186405588 1068 17592186405920 17592186405015] [17592186405588 1071 "Source question: 9.\n\nFind $\\int_C6x\\cos y\\,dx-8y\\sin x\\,dy$ along the segments (0,0) → (2,0) → (2,2) → (0,2), in that order.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405588 1072 #uuid "b054d67c-ee99-5f26-bc1c-b0e0b3cfe57b" 17592186405038] [17592186405588 1074 "The bottom segment contributes $\\int_0^2 6x dx=12$. The right segment contributes $\\int_0^2-8y\\sin2\\,dy=-16\\sin2$. The top, traversed right to left, contributes $\\int_2^0 6x\\cos2\\,dx=-12\\cos2$. Do not add a fourth segment." 17592186405015] [17592186405589 1089 17592186049114 17592186405015] [17592186405589 1089 17592186049138 17592186405015] [17592186405589 1089 17592186050155 17592186405015] [17592186405589 1090 17592186405527 17592186405038] [17592186405589 1091 #uuid "b0c40836-f4ac-508d-bc37-dd42df713496" 17592186405038] [17592186405590 1068 17592186406214 17592186405015] [17592186405590 1071 "Source question: 8.\n\nFind the curl of $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)=\\langle6\\cos(4x),5\\sin y,9e^{4xz}\\rangle$. \n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405590 1072 #uuid "b0fa39f3-551a-5650-910b-6aed3cf97e71" 17592186405038] [17592186405590 1074 "Only $R_x=36ze^{4xz}$ contributes. The middle component is $P_z-R_x=-36ze^{4xz}$; preserve that minus sign." 17592186405015] [17592186405591 1058 17592186405645 17592186405038] [17592186405591 1062 #uuid "b1436551-626c-52a0-8a00-9653fbe4b034" 17592186405038] [17592186405591 1063 17592186405269 17592186405038] [17592186405592 1089 17592186050149 17592186405015] [17592186405592 1089 17592186050875 17592186405015] [17592186405592 1090 17592186405802 17592186405038] [17592186405592 1091 #uuid "b1648d18-ae1f-5547-967d-ed7a00e139ca" 17592186405038] [17592186405593 1058 17592186405599 17592186405038] [17592186405593 1062 #uuid "b250dd60-1d85-501c-be34-9548247db64e" 17592186405038] [17592186405593 1063 17592186405739 17592186405038] [17592186405594 1085 17592186405223 17592186405038] [17592186405594 1085 17592186405252 17592186405038] [17592186405594 1085 17592186405628 17592186405038] [17592186405594 1086 "Source question: 12(a)–(c).\n\nFind $\\int_C e^x\\cos y\\,dx-e^x\\sin y\\,dy$ for each curve C." 17592186405038] [17592186405594 1087 #uuid "b2d2c0fa-8ad3-5edf-9d0d-eb348fd31800" 17592186405038] [17592186405594 1088 17592186405628 17592186405038] [17592186405595 1058 17592186405604 17592186405038] [17592186405595 1062 #uuid "b2ff52fb-f5ee-55b9-84f7-cf6baf4a2b9a" 17592186405038] [17592186405595 1063 17592186405128 17592186405038] [17592186405596 1089 17592186048259 17592186405015] [17592186405596 1090 17592186405625 17592186405038] [17592186405596 1091 #uuid "b302eafe-c25d-5f50-b350-312d5fc34a48" 17592186405038] [17592186405597 1058 17