7592186405038] [17592186405603 1074 "$L=\\int_0^{\\pi/3}5\\sec t\\,dt=5[\\ln|\\sec t+\\tan t|]_0^{\\pi/3}=5\\ln(2+\\sqrt3)$. The integrand is positive throughout the interval." 17592186405015] [17592186405604 1058 17592186405320 17592186405038] [17592186405604 1062 #uuid "b45f4134-201f-5b0b-b8dc-ddaa78ef91cd" 17592186405038] [17592186405604 1063 17592186405131 17592186405038] [17592186405605 1058 17592186405160 17592186405038] [17592186405605 1062 #uuid "b4ba0f26-9a50-5a38-83c7-af9509a8ce3b" 17592186405038] [17592186405605 1063 17592186405443 17592186405038] [17592186405606 1085 17592186405579 17592186405038] [17592186405606 1085 17592186405776 17592186405038] [17592186405606 1086 "Source question: 3.\n\nFor $x(t)=t-6$ and $y(t)=5t+1$, with $0\\le t\\le2$, eliminate the parameter. Give exact answers." 17592186405038] [17592186405606 1087 #uuid "b4baeb0c-f674-508b-b39a-7dfb18d20741" 17592186405038] [17592186405606 1088 17592186405579 17592186405038] [17592186405607 1058 17592186405283 17592186405038] [17592186405607 1062 #uuid "b52ab28e-ea5b-5852-9018-db7608132ecc" 17592186405038] [17592186405607 1063 17592186405659 17592186405038] [17592186405608 1058 17592186405247 17592186405038] [17592186405608 1062 #uuid "b545f6fb-318f-5799-8417-3e43953888d6" 17592186405038] [17592186405608 1063 17592186405479 17592186405038] [17592186405609 1089 17592186049465 17592186405015] [17592186405609 1090 17592186405121 17592186405038] [17592186405609 1091 #uuid "b583644b-9078-5138-aa06-57a170cc2de4" 17592186405038] [17592186405610 1085 17592186405352 17592186405038] [17592186405610 1085 17592186405461 17592186405038] [17592186405610 1086 "Source question: 3(a)–(b), PDF page 4.\n\nLet $\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\langle-y,x\\rangle/\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}$, $\\eta(x,y)=x^2+y^2$, and $C: x^2+y^2=a^2$, with $a>0$." 17592186405038] [17592186405610 1087 #uuid "b5ae2259-aa1c-5659-8da4-06e9ae82ee6b" 17592186405038] [17592186405610 1088 17592186405352 17592186405038] [17592186405611 1064 "**6.3 Conservative Vector Fields**\n\nDue: 2026-10-22\nSource: [Macmillan Achieve](https://achieve.macmillanlearning.com/ihub/assessment/f188d950-dd73-11e0-9572-0800200c9a66/5a1db2a7-4295-4a4c-9dbb-ceb8b174a282/3a627574-021f-4d0f-9969-54703a59bf12)\n\nStudy answers and explanations are authored additions, not publisher solutions.\n\n## Instructions\n\nGive exact answers, using symbolic notation and fractions where needed. Preserve K or C as requested for constants. Questions 1–4 explicitly require the Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals. Source: Sullivan 2e Calculus, W.H. Freeman. The platform allows unlimited attempts with no incorrect-attempt penalty; submissions up to 50 days late incur a 2% daily penalty." 17592186405015] [17592186405611 1066 #uuid "b6313513-b04c-512a-b61e-312237b52ce9" 17592186405038] [17592186405611 1067 "Assignment information" 17592186405038] [17592186405612 1089 17592186048225 17592186405015] [17592186405612 1089 17592186049050 17592186405015] [17592186405612 1090 17592186405075 17592186405038] [17592186405612 1091 #uuid "b653b3ca-9367-5ad2-93c1-b25f529d3151" 17592186405038] [17592186405613 1068 17592186406244 17592186405015] [17592186405613 1071 "**(b)** $\\mathbf F=\\frac{\\langle x,y\\rangle}{\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}}=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}}\\mathbf i+\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}}\\mathbf j$.\n\nPolar form: {{polar}}. Explain how the result agrees with the field plot." 17592186405015] [17592186405613 1072 #uuid "b656e2fd-9aeb-5597-87ec-b0770c4f9451" 17592186405038] [17592186405613 1074 "**interpretation:** Divide the result of (a) by r for r>0. It is radial outward with constant unit length and is undefined at the origin.\n\nDivide the result of (a) by r for r>0. It is radial outward with constant unit length and is undefined at the origin. Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$ and the basis identities from 4(b). Polar directions require r>0; in (a) and (c), the Cartesian field extends to the zero vector at the origin." 17592186405015] [17592186405614 1071 "**Question 2 — Part A**\n\nShow that the height of the box shown is $\\lVert\\mathbf w\\rVert\\cos\\theta$." 17592186405038] [17592186405614 1072 #uuid "b6678591-0a57-529e-afed-2a52cbbc4d11" 17592186405038] [17592186405614 1074 "The vector $\\mathbf u\\times\\mathbf v$ is normal to the base. With unit normal $\\mathbf n=(\\mathbf u\\times\\mathbf v)/\\lVert\\mathbf u\\times\\mathbf v\\rVert$, the projection of $\\mathbf w$ on that normal is $\\mathbf w\\cdot\\mathbf n=\\lVert\\mathbf w\\rVert\\cos\\theta$. For the acute angle shown, this projection is the height. For arbitrary orientation, geometric height is $\\lVert\\mathbf w\\rVert|\\cos\\theta|$.\n\nHere the angle is measured from the normal, so use cosine. The worksheet formula assumes the acute angle drawn; an obtuse angle gives a negative signed projection, whose absolute value is the physical height. Assume a nondegenerate base when defining its unit normal." 17592186405015] [17592186405615 1068 17592186406061 17592186405015] [17592186405615 1071 "[Open in WeBWorK](https://webwork2.science.oregonstate.edu/webwork2/MTH_256_020_F2026/WebWork_1/3)\n\n**Question 3**\n\nFind the particular solution of the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+y\\cos(x)=5\\cos(x)\n$$\n\nsatisfying the initial condition $y(0)=7$.\n\nYour answer should be a function of $x$.\n\n$y(x)=$ {{field-1}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405615 1072 #uuid "b6875448-22bc-5b6c-8e17-3448ca6a39e2" 17592186405038] [17592186405615 1074 "Let $u=y-5$. Then $u\\prime=-u\\cos x$, so $u=Ce^{-\\sin x}$. The initial value gives $C=2$. Check $y\\prime=-2\\cos x\\,e^{-\\sin x}$ and $y\\cos x=5\\cos x+2\\cos x\\,e^{-\\sin x}$; their sum is $5\\cos x$." 17592186405015] [17592186405616 1089 17592186050295 17592186405015] [17592186405616 1089 17592186050866 17592186405015] [17592186405616 1090 17592186405060 17592186405038] [17592186405616 1091 #uuid "b7801be6-3f36-592b-bed0-4dc1ed119e05" 17592186405038] [17592186405617 1068 17592186405981 17592186405015] [17592186405617 1071 "Source question: 6.\n\nFor $\\mathbf F=\\langle-y,x,z\\rangle$ on $S:x^2+y^2+z^2=49$, z≥0, find $\\iint_S\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,dS$ with outer unit normal n.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405617 1072 #uuid "b782860b-1eaa-5a7d-8c10-9856303f7e0b" 17592186405038] [17592186405617 1074 "On the radius-7 sphere, $n=(x,y,z)/7$, so F·n=z²/7. Use z=7cosφ and dS=49sinφ dφdθ, 0≤φ≤π/2: flux $=343(2\\pi)\\int_0^{\\pi/2}\\cos^2\\phi\\sin\\phi d\\phi=686\\pi/3$." 17592186405015] [17592186405618 1062 #uuid "b7b91c8d-1728-5471-b3ce-6eaefbf6e419" 17592186405038] [17592186405618 1063 17592186405261 17592186405038] [17592186405619 1089 17592186048225 17592186405015] [17592186405619 1089 17592186050488 17592186405015] [17592186405619 1090 17592186405102 17592186405038] [17592186405619 1091 #uuid "b8205605-60fb-5f48-9400-7ef18432e2c0" 17592186405038] [17592186405620 1089 17592186048225 17592186405015] [17592186405620 1089 17592186050488 17592186405015] [17592186405620 1090 17592186405366 17592186405038] [17592186405620 1091 #uuid "b8567fa8-6658-5601-be04-bf9eb64f4d61" 17592186405038] [17592186405621 1058 17592186405206 17592186405038] [17592186405621 1062 #uuid "b8c6bc9f-ff60-5b75-ae75-0bff643fe0ec" 17592186405038] [17592186405621 1063 17592186405226 17592186405038] [17592186405622 1068 17592186406006 17592186405015] [17592186405622 1071 "Source question: 7.\n\n**Question 7**\n\nFind the arc length for $x(t)=13t^2+1$, $y(t)=2t^3-1$, $0\\le t\\le2$. Give an exact answer.\n\n$s=$ {{field-1}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405622 1072 #uuid "b8fcb546-d6c9-50b5-a552-4ead01edb044" 17592186405038] [17592186405622 1074 "The speed is $\\sqrt{(26t)^2+(6t^2)^2}=2t\\sqrt{169+9t^2}$ on this interval. Substituting $u=169+9t^2$ gives $s=[2(169+9t^2)^{3/2}/27]_0^2=2(205^{3/2}-2197)/27$." 17592186405015] [17592186405623 1068 17592186405962 17592186405015] [17592186405623 1071 "Source question: 6.\n\nFor the conservative field $\\mathbf F=\\langle3x-\\cos y,x\\sin y\\rangle$, evaluate $\\int_C(3x-\\cos y)dx+x\\sin y\\,dy$ from $(1,\\pi/2)$ to $(2,\\pi)$ along a piecewise smooth curve.\n\nIntegral: {{integral}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405623 1072 #uuid "b91c6caf-20c7-506c-b577-b041551f1f6d" 17592186405038] [17592186405623 1074 "A potential is $f=3x^2/2-x\\cos y$. Its gradient is the stated field. The endpoint values are 8 and 3/2, giving 13/2." 17592186405015] [17592186405624 1071 "**Question 3 — Part A**\n\nAn integral curve that is a solution curve." 17592186405038] [17592186405624 1072 #uuid "b93c29c5-b86b-5ae9-8499-cc25a76c055c" 17592186405038] [17592186405624 1074 "Draw and label the upper hyperbola branch $y=\\sqrt{x^2+1/4}$. Draw the visible arc for $|x|<\\sqrt3/2$, within the supplied plotting window. The function is a solution for every real $x$: $y\\prime=x/\\sqrt{x^2+1/4}=x/y$. It stays above the singular line $y=0$.\n\n![Authored reference graph](images/7f/7f0bf50470f063b2555f5ac202e8bb1ef934ac29d9fc7bf4726d2d79de612da6.png)\n\nThe curve must be tangent to the field at each point and must be a single-valued differentiable graph on an interval. A branch with $y\ne0$ avoids the undefined right-hand side." 17592186405015] [17592186405625 1068 17592186406004 17592186405015] [17592186405625 1071 "Source question: 9.\n\nA fluid of constant mass density crosses $z=81-x^2-y^2$, z≥0, with velocity $\\mathbf F=\\langle x,y,11\\rangle$. Find the mass flowing outward in one unit of time.\n\n**Source gap:** The constant density has no numerical value. For this study answer, denote it by $\\rho_0$; report signed outward mass flow.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405625 1072 #uuid "b990240c-49ef-55fd-aa91-1ef51a672c6c" 17592186405038] [17592186405625 1074 "Outward relative to the solid beneath the paraboloid and above z=0 is upward: $n\\,dS=(2x,2y,1)dxdy$. The flux density is 2r²+11. Integrating over r≤9 gives $2\\pi\\int_0^9(2r^2+11)rdr=\\pi(9^4+11\\cdot9^2)=7452\\pi$. Multiply by ρ₀; with unit density omit that factor. No density value is supplied in the source." 17592186405015] [17592186405626 1089 17592186051076 17592186405015] [17592186405626 1090 17592186405566 17592186405038] [17592186405626 1091 #uuid "b9d66075-219a-55df-aac0-190ab6c99923" 17592186405038] [17592186405627 1068 17592186406015 17592186405015] [17592186405627 1071 "Source question: 11.\n\nFind the lateral area above the xy-plane and below $z=3-y^2$, formed by vertical segments from $C: x=\\sin(11t),\\ y=\\cos(11t),\\ 0\\le t\\le2\\pi/11$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405627 1072 #uuid "ba1a1155-634a-5359-b506-deabdb302e33" 17592186405038] [17592186405627 1074 "Area is height integrated over the base arc: $A=\\int_C(3-y^2)ds$. The speed is 11; u=11t gives $\\int_0^{2\\pi}(3-\\cos^2u)du=6\\pi-\\pi$." 17592186405015] [17592186405628 1058 17592186405223 17592186405038] [17592186405628 1062 #uuid "ba452181-552a-561c-b1ba-5ba84a9169c3" 17592186405038] [17592186405628 1063 17592186405174 17592186405038] [17592186405629 1058 17592186405151 17592186405038] [17592186405629 1062 #uuid "ba6d41c5-889a-5ce7-8037-aa4e69500dcb" 17592186405038] [17592186405629 1063 17592186405786 17592186405038] [17592186405630 1058 17592186405250 17592186405038] [17592186405630 1062 #uuid "ba8fb377-f0cf-51b6-af00-6b588cf882ed" 17592186405038] [17592186405630 1063 17592186405696 17592186405038] [17592186405631 1058 17592186405383 17592186405038] [17592186405631 1062 #uuid "bb26608b-5ad9-53fc-b32a-f7e39830b2c4" 17592186405038] [17592186405631 1063 17592186405299 17592186405038] [17592186405632 1089 17592186048832 17592186405015] [17592186405632 1089 17592186050196 17592186405015] [17592186405632 1090 17592186405404 17592186405038] [17592186405632 1091 #uuid "bb299914-611a-54e8-850f-f40bb23dda37" 17592186405038] [17592186405633 1058 17592186405495 17592186405038] [17592186405633 1062 #uuid "bb9e7a3f-341f-59b2-afbf-9bb1e95a5076" 17592186405038] [17592186405633 1063 17592186405100 17592186405038] [17592186405634 1068 17592186405919 17592186405015] [17592186405634 1071 "Source question: 9.\n\nThe field $\\mathbf F=\\langle ye^{9x},e^{9x}/9\\rangle$ is conservative. Find a potential f. Use K for the arbitrary constant and absorb into K as much as possible.\n\n$f(x,y)=$ {{potential}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405634 1072 #uuid "bbd61bdf-d27a-595a-baf4-a11edba76226" 17592186405038] [17592186405634 1074 "Integrate ye⁹ˣ with respect to x: $f=ye^{9x}/9+g(y)$. Its y derivative is e⁹ˣ/9+g′, so g′=0. Differentiating in x cancels the factor 1/9." 17592186405015] [17592186405635 1043 17592186405051 17592186405038] [17592186405635 1043 17592186405059 17592186405038] [17592186405635 1043 17592186405151 17592186405038] [17592186405635 1043 17592186405306 17592186405038] [17592186405635 1043 17592186405436 17592186405038] [17592186405635 1043 17592186405460 17592186405038] [17592186405635 1043 17592186405504 17592186405038] [17592186405635 1043 17592186405569 17592186405038] [17592186405635 1043 17592186405580 17592186405038] [17592186405635 1043 17592186405629 17592186405038] [17592186405635 1043 17592186405690 17592186405038] [17592186405635 1043 17592186405724 17592186405038] [17592186405635 1043 17592186405799 17592186405038] [17592186405635 1044 17592186045461 17592186405038] [17592186405635 1045 "MTH 255 — OHW-11" 17592186405038] [17592186405635 1046 17592186405504 17592186405038] [17592186405635 1047 "2026-12-04T07:59:59.000Z" 17592186405038] [17592186405635 1048 17592186405043 17592186405038] [17592186405635 1053 #uuid "bc02238e-6390-504d-a106-4d6c9d697203" 17592186405038] [17592186405636 1085 17592186405332 17592186405038] [17592186405636 1085 17592186405750 17592186405038] [17592186405636 1085 17592186405755 17592186405038] [17592186405636 1085 17592186405829 17592186405038] [17592186405636 1086 "Source question: 1.\n\nThe electrical voltage in some 3D region is given by\n\n$$\nV(x,y,z)=x^3-5xy+xyz.\n$$\n\nConsider $P(2,8,5)$ and $\\mathbf v=\\langle 1,1,-1\\rangle$." 17592186405038] [17592186405636 1087 #uuid "bc124f4a-6fac-5bac-8188-819201405a8d" 17592186405038] [17592186405636 1088 17592186405750 17592186405038] [17592186405637 1089 17592186050418 17592186405015] [17592186405637 1090 17592186405396 17592186405038] [17592186405637 1091 #uuid "bd551c10-e921-513b-bfba-3f9958869fab" 17592186405038] [17592186405638 1058 17592186405455 17592186405038] [17592186405638 1062 #uuid "bd6bd3b3-8038-5b75-9716-f87ef59b1f53" 17592186405038] [17592186405638 1063 17592186405679 17592186405038] [17592186405639 1068 17592186405972 17592186405015] [17592186405639 1071 "Source question: 8.\n\n**Question 8**\n\nChoose the graph of $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)=x\\mathbf i$." 17592186405015] [17592186405639 1072 #uuid "bda8ca51-ce46-5d6d-83ef-e11ad88fc671" 17592186405038] [17592186405639 1074 "These horizontal arrows point away from $x=0$, matching $\\mathbf F=\\langle x,0,0\\rangle$. The direction reverses with the sign of $x$, the length is $|x|$, and changing $y$ or $z$ does not change the vector." 17592186405015] [17592186405640 1089 17592186050514 17592186405015] [17592186405640 1090 17592186405170 17592186405038] [17592186405640 1091 #uuid "bdafa48d-2ccc-5663-a62e-ca5dd3f07050" 17592186405038] [17592186405641 1089 17592186048923 17592186405015] [17592186405641 1089 17592186049366 17592186405015] [17592186405641 1089 17592186050033 17592186405015] [17592186405641 1089 17592186050149 17592186405015] [17592186405641 1090 17592186405563 17592186405038] [17592186405641 1091 #uuid "bf010f14-eee4-5ffe-bd4d-12fbe79a20ab" 17592186405038] [17592186405642 1085 17592186405426 17592186405038] [17592186405642 1085 17592186405499 17592186405038] [17592186405642 1086 "Source question: 5(a)–(b), PDF page 6." 17592186405038] [17592186405642 1087 #uuid "bf09e225-dc63-563c-8b55-4c55af8a069a" 17592186405038] [17592186405642 1088 17592186405499 17592186405038] [17592186405643 1058 17592186405082 17592186405038] [17592186405643 1062 #uuid "bf287703-28d3-587f-88ea-a957822f9aed" 17592186405038] [17592186405643 1063 17592186405361 17592186405038] [17592186405644 1071 "**(a)** Draw F and C in the same coordinate axes." 17592186405038] [17592186405644 1072 #uuid "bf36cced-5fd5-5019-a7a0-eab3ed76260a" 17592186405038] [17592186405644 1074 "**drawing:** Draw the radius-a circle centered at the origin. At every nonzero point, draw an equal-length arrow pointing radially outward; no field vector exists at the origin. At (a,0), (0,a), (−a,0), (0,−a), arrows point right, up, left, down.\n\n![Authored radial field reference](images/a8/a81e47b17c286f2bbb3393045774604a075906fc99c8a42c6562c95605d6c64b.png)\n\nNormalization makes every field vector length 1. Its direction is the position vector (x,y). The reference diagram uses uniformly scaled arrows and labels distance in units of a." 17592186405015] [17592186405645 1058 17592186405519 17592186405038] [17592186405645 1062 #uuid "bf4417d9-13ba-5482-b7cb-95c2117db340" 17592186405038] [17592186405645 1063 17592186405429 17592186405038] [17592186405646 1089 17592186048960 17592186405015] [17592186405646 1089 17592186049366 17592186405015] [17592186405646 1089 17592186050142 17592186405015] [17592186405646 1090 17592186405477 17592186405038] [17592186405646 1091 #uuid "bfbe6d4f-83b5-54ce-ba13-ee27320a2076" 17592186405038] [17592186405647 1058 17592186405729 17592186405038] [17592186405647 1062 #uuid "c068b7ca-816a-5371-b8c9-66aca9347a80" 17592186405038] [17592186405647 1063 17592186405385 17592186405038] [17592186405648 1068 17592186405895 17592186405015] [17592186405648 1071 "Source question: 19.\n\nFind the work of $\\mathbf F=\\langle5x-2y,6xy\\rangle$ along $\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle2\\cos t,3\\sin t\\rangle$ from t=0 to t=π/2.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405648 1072 #uuid "c0adaf32-d2d9-57c8-8031-73b0c003a48c" 17592186405038] [17592186405648 1074 "The dot product is $-20\\sin t\\cos t+12\\sin^2t+108\\sin t\\cos^2t$. Its three integrals are −10, 3π, and 36, respectively." 17592186405015] [17592186405649 1058 17592186405801 17592186405038] [17592186405649 1062 #uuid "c115f30c-1944-5d54-9b55-d23f508d9139" 17592186405038] [17592186405649 1063 17592186405187 17592186405038] [17592186405650 1043 17592186405058 17592186405038] [17592186405650 1043 17592186405091 17592186405038] [17592186405650 1043 17592186405320 17592186405038] [17592186405650 1043 17592186405401 17592186405038] [17592186405650 1043 17592186405447 17592186405038] [17592186405650 1043 17592186405472 17592186405038] [17592186405650 1043 17592186405481 17592186405038] [17592186405650 1043 17592186405482 17592186405038] [17592186405650 1043 17592186405487 17592186405038] [17592186405650 1043 17592186405500 17592186405038] [17592186405650 1043 17592186405582 17592186405038] [17592186405650 1043 17592186405595 17592186405038] [17592186405650 1043 17592186405604 17592186405038] [17592186405650 1044 17592186045461 17592186405038] [17592186405650 1045 "MTH 255 — OHW-8" 17592186405038] [17592186405650 1046 17592186405482 17592186405038] [17592186405650 1047 "2026-11-13T07:59:59.000Z" 17592186405038] [17592186405650 1048 17592186405043 17592186405038] [17592186405650 1053 #uuid "c1fd6d1a-2ea0-5d67-9629-18bb38e71404" 17592186405038] [17592186405651 1068 17592186406095 17592186405015] [17592186405651 1071 "Source question: 4.\n\n**Question 4**\n\nChoose the graph of $\\mathbf F(x,y)=y\\mathbf i-7\\mathbf j$." 17592186405015] [17592186405651 1072 #uuid "c21e1b31-a23d-54a6-9aa3-8e08d261dff6" 17592186405038] [17592186405651 1074 "The component $y$ points right above the $x$-axis and left below it; the component $-7$ always points down. Along each horizontal row the arrows agree, matching this graph." 17592186405015] [17592186405652 1068 17592186406269 17592186405015] [17592186405652 1071 "Source question: 2.\n\nFind $\\nabla\\times\\mathbf F$ for $\\mathbf F=\\langle6xyz^9,y^2\\sin(6z),3xe^{6z}\\rangle$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405652 1072 #uuid "c28d5052-b40d-5f11-bc21-0d49dee27a1b" 17592186405038] [17592186405652 1074 "Use (R_y−Q_z,P_z−R_x,Q_x−P_y). The pairs of derivatives are (0,6y²cos(6z)), (54xyz⁸,3e⁶ᶻ), and (0,6xz⁹). The second component is P_z−R_x, so keep that subtraction order." 17592186405015] [17592186405653 1058 17592186405064 17592186405038] [17592186405653 1062 #uuid "c48d089b-9362-56a5-9e61-187732a48700" 17592186405038] [17592186405653 1063 17592186405809 17592186405038] [17592186405654 1068 17592186406160 17592186405015] [17592186405654 1071 "Source question: 5.\n\nEvaluate $\\oint_C(5x^4-5y^2)dx+(7y-4xy)dy$ counterclockwise around the region bounded by $y=x^2$ and $y=\\sqrt x$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405654 1072 #uuid "c582ed14-fd82-598f-bd67-b4d2b53d152a" 17592186405038] [17592186405654 1074 "The curves intersect at x=0,1 and x²≤y≤√x. Since $Q_x-P_y=-4y+10y=6y$, the integral is $\\int_0^1\\int_{x^2}^{\\sqrt x}6y\\,dy\\,dx=3\\int_0^1(x-x^4)dx=9/10$." 17592186405015] [17592186405655 1089 17592186048960 17592186405015] [17592186405655 1089 17592186049859 17592186405015] [17592186405655 1090 17592186405183 17592186405038] [17592186405655 1091 #uuid "c5cc2d38-02a1-5318-9681-fd4391f562cc" 17592186405038] [17592186405656 1058 17592186405831 17592186405038] [17592186405656 1062 #uuid "c5d522b7-2ff8-565a-a2d5-971b51d214a7" 17592186405038] [17592186405656 1063 17592186405501 17592186405038] [17592186405657 1089 17592186049465 17592186405015] [17592186405657 1089 17592186050540 17592186405015] [17592186405657 1090 17592186405089 17592186405038] [17592186405657 1091 #uuid "c60a9c40-57e8-5cc3-9a75-2a7fba0d95fd" 17592186405038] [17592186405658 1058 17592186405489 17592186405038] [17592186405658 1062 #uuid "c60b7f3d-1eb4-5010-a17a-dbab803a86b5" 17592186405038] [17592186405658 1063 17592186405096 17592186405038] [17592186405659 1068 17592186405860 17592186405015] [17592186405659 1068 17592186406174 17592186405015] [17592186405659 1071 "**(a)** $\\phi=e^x\\sin y$. Find its gradient field and the points excluded from its domain.\n\nGradient: {{gradient}}.\n\nExcluded points: {{excluded}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405659 1072 #uuid "c63ff802-33ef-5f29-8579-d5d62a98d3b5" 17592186405038] [17592186405659 1074 "Differentiate in x while holding y fixed, then in y holding x fixed. Exponential and trigonometric functions and their derivatives exist everywhere." 17592186405015] [17592186405660 1068 17592186405851 17592186405015] [17592186405660 1071 "Source question: 9.\n\nDetermine whether the following force is a conservative vector field.\n\n$\\mathbf F=\\langle6x,9y,0\\rangle$." 17592186405015] [17592186405660 1072 #uuid "c661bc94-62bf-5770-8a4a-56c5e6619ad9" 17592186405038] [17592186405660 1074 "The potential f=3x²+(9/2)y² has gradient F throughout space. Thus F is conservative; its curl is also identically zero." 17592186405015] [17592186405661 1058 17592186405255 17592186405038] [17592186405661 1062 #uuid "c6811841-e2af-5eb2-a3b8-b6700124c55f" 17592186405038] [17592186405661 1063 17592186405149 17592186405038] [17592186405662 1071 "**(c)** Conclude that at every point on C, F is orthogonal to C." 17592186405038] [17592186405662 1072 #uuid "c6899113-5e70-523e-b4e3-f94c34a9f739" 17592186405038] [17592186405662 1074 "**proof:** C is the level curve $\\eta=a^2$. Since $\\nabla\\eta\\ne0$ on C, it is normal to C. There $F=\\nabla\\eta/(2a)$, so F is also normal to C.\n\nThe condition a>0 excludes the origin. Multiplying a nonzero normal vector by the positive scalar 1/(2a) preserves its normal direction." 17592186405015] [17592186405663 1062 #uuid "c6ee17cf-bf8a-53d0-8f4e-295eec48745a" 17592186405038] [17592186405663 1063 17592186405535 17592186405038] [17592186405664 1068 17592186406318 17592186405015] [17592186405664 1071 "**(b)** Find $\\nabla\\eta(x,y)$. Notice that η is not a potential for F, but its gradient is parallel to F and points in the same direction.\n\nGradient: {{gradient}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405664 1072 #uuid "c70ca3f2-a964-523d-9c4f-6df57fea40bb" 17592186405038] [17592186405664 1074 "$\\eta_x=2x$ and $\\eta_y=2y$, so $\\nabla\\eta=2\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}\\,F$ at every nonzero point. The scalar is positive; it is not identically 1, so η is not a potential for F." 17592186405015] [17592186405665 1071 "**Question 1 — Part D**\n\nGive two orthogonal directions in which the rate of change of the voltage at $P$ is $0$." 17592186405038] [17592186405665 1072 #uuid "c727d611-c655-5cc6-b14c-49e9e6d8a48d" 17592186405038] [17592186405665 1074 "One pair is $\\mathbf u=\\langle0,1,0\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf v=\\langle4/5,0,-3/5\\rangle$. They are unit vectors, $\\mathbf u\\cdot\\mathbf v=0$, and each has dot product zero with $\\nabla V(P)=\\langle12,0,16\\rangle$. Other orthogonal unit pairs in this plane are valid.\n\nZero change means $12u_x+16u_z=0$. Choose two independent directions in that plane, then check their mutual orthogonality as well as their orthogonality to the gradient. Merely choosing a vector and its negative does not give an orthogonal pair." 17592186405015] [17592186405666 1089 17592186051091 17592186405015] [17592186405666 1090 17592186405414 17592186405038] [17592186405666 1091 #uuid "c7d73c74-4819-5800-ae90-b373e4fa2605" 17592186405038] [17592186405667 1089 17592186048259 17592186405015] [17592186405667 1090 17592186405753 17592186405038] [17592186405667 1091 #uuid "c84df452-16cc-5290-830e-3467cf5af313" 17592186405038] [17592186405668 1068 17592186405901 17592186405015] [17592186405668 1071 "**(d)** $\\mathbf F=\\frac{\\langle-y,x\\rangle}{\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}}=\\frac{-y}{\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}}\\mathbf i+\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}}\\mathbf j$.\n\nPolar form: {{polar}}. Explain how the result agrees with the field plot." 17592186405015] [17592186405668 1072 #uuid "c857505f-9e20-54e3-963b-ca064f545faa" 17592186405038] [17592186405668 1074 "**interpretation:** Divide (c) by r for r>0. The field follows increasing angle with unit length and is undefined at the origin.\n\nDivide (c) by r for r>0. The field follows increasing angle with unit length and is undefined at the origin. Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$ and the basis identities from 4(b). Polar directions require r>0; in (a) and (c), the Cartesian field extends to the zero vector at the origin." 17592186405015] [17592186405669 1043 17592186405081 17592186405038] [17592186405669 1043 17592186405106 17592186405038] [17592186405669 1043 17592186405117 17592186405038] [17592186405669 1043 17592186405206 17592186405038] [17592186405669 1043 17592186405260 17592186405038] [17592186405669 1043 17592186405276 17592186405038] [17592186405669 1043 17592186405280 17592186405038] [17592186405669 1043 17592186405392 17592186405038] [17592186405669 1043 17592186405621 17592186405038] [17592186405669 1043 17592186405674 17592186405038] [17592186405669 1043 17592186405758 17592186405038] [17592186405669 1043 17592186405774 17592186405038] [17592186405669 1043 17592186405811 17592186405038] [17592186405669 1044 17592186045461 17592186405038] [17592186405669 1045 "MTH 255 — OHW-10" 17592186405038] [17592186405669 1046 17592186405392 17592186405038] [17592186405669 1047 "2026-11-27T07:59:59.000Z" 17592186405038] [17592186405669 1048 17592186405043 17592186405038] [17592186405669 1053 #uuid "c88eeaa7-8847-586f-9523-864dcd1c27e5" 17592186405038] [17592186405670 1058 17592186405105 17592186405038] [17592186405670 1062 #uuid "c8c13483-7faa-568a-8c5b-3727a23f01b7" 17592186405038] [17592186405670 1063 17592186405166 17592186405038] [17592186405671 1068 17592186405903 17592186405015] [17592186405671 1071 "**Question 3 — Part C**\n\nIf $y(0)=y_0$, what is $e^0y(0)$ equal to?\n\n$e^0y(0)=$ {{field-1}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405671 1072 #uuid "c8c2dd09-f362-5d9d-ac5a-b26dfebc7a7e" 17592186405038] [17592186405671 1074 "Since $e^0=1$ and $y(0)=y_0$, the constant value of $e^xy$ is $y_0$." 17592186405015] [17592186405672 1068 17592186406177 17592186405015] [17592186405672 1068 17592186406328 17592186405015] [17592186405672 1071 "Source question: 8.\n\nFor $f(x,y)=3xe^y$, find its line integrals with respect to x and y along $x=e^{7y}$ from (1,0) to $(e^7,1)$.\n\n$\\int_C f\\,dx=$ {{dx}}.\n\n$\\int_C f\\,dy=$ {{dy}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405672 1072 #uuid "c8d35103-1c2b-5ee3-865c-2de5626ecae2" 17592186405038] [17592186405672 1074 "Parameterize by y from 0 to 1. Then f=3e^{8y} and dx=7e^{7y}dy. The dx integral is $\\int_0^1 21e^{15y}dy$; the dy integral is $\\int_0^1 3e^{8y}dy$." 17592186405015] [17592186405673 1043 17592186405065 17592186405038] [17592186405673 1043 17592186405160 17592186405038] [17592186405673 1043 17592186405331 17592186405038] [17592186405673 1043 17592186405344 17592186405038] [17592186405673 1043 17592186405372 17592186405038] [17592186405673 1043 17592186405387 17592186405038] [17592186405673 1043 17592186405395 17592186405038] [17592186405673 1043 17592186405519 17592186405038] [17592186405673 1043 17592186405552 17592186405038] [17592186405673 1043 17592186405591 17592186405038] [17592186405673 1043 17592186405605 17592186405038] [17592186405673 1043 17592186405645 17592186405038] [17592186405673 1043 17592186405789 17592186405038] [17592186405673 1043 17592186405825 17592186405038] [17592186405673 1044 17592186045461 17592186405038] [17592186405673 1045 "MTH 255 — OHW-5" 17592186405038] [17592186405673 1046 17592186405825 17592186405038] [17592186405673 1047 "2026-10-23T06:59:59.000Z" 17592186405038] [17592186405673 1048 17592186405043 17592186405038] [17592186405673 1053 #uuid "c9d189b4-509f-5aaf-acfa-28662c282986" 17592186405038] [17592186405674 1058 17592186405811 17592186405038] [17592186405674 1062 #uuid "c9ff89ed-bef8-5d7f-b959-fa93ea0a6171" 17592186405038] [17592186405674 1063 17592186405324 17592186405038] [17592186405675 1068 17592186405916 17592186405015] [17592186405675 1068 17592186406286 17592186405015] [17592186405675 1068 17592186406309 17592186405015] [17592186405675 1071 "**Question 3 — Part B**\n\nUse polar coordinates to evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_0^\\infty\\int_0^\\infty e^{-x^2-y^2}\\,dy\\,dx.\n$$\n\nWhat does this say about $\\displaystyle\\int_0^\\infty e^{-x^2}\\,dx$? How about $\\displaystyle\\int_{-\\infty}^\\infty e^{-x^2}\\,dx$?\n\nDouble integral: {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\int_0^\\infty e^{-x^2}\\,dx=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\int_{-\\infty}^\\infty e^{-x^2}\\,dx=$ {{field-3}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405675 1072 #uuid "ca6efe45-1e34-528b-98ca-b08851ed0a7e" 17592186405038] [17592186405675 1074 "The first quadrant becomes $0\\le\\theta\\le\\pi/2$, $r\\ge0$. The double integral is $\\int_0^{\\pi/2}\\int_0^\\infty e^{-r^2}r\\,dr\\,d\\theta=(\\pi/2)(1/2)=\\pi/4$. Because the nonnegative integrand factors as $e^{-x^2}e^{-y^2}$, this equals $I^2$ where $I=\\int_0^\\infty e^{-x^2}\\,dx>0$. Hence $I=\\sqrt\\pi/2$. Evenness doubles it on the real line." 17592186405015] [17592186405676 1068 17592186406194 17592186405015] [17592186405676 1071 "**Question 4 — Part A**\n\nPoints with horizontal tangents: {{field-1}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405676 1072 #uuid "cb479d44-fe4f-5635-98f3-5c844ff53e09" 17592186405038] [17592186405676 1074 "A horizontal tangent requires $y'=0$ and $x'\\ne0$. Here $y'=4t$ vanishes at $t=0$, while $x'=3t^2-144=-144$. The point is $(0,0)$." 17592186405015] [17592186405677 1058 17592186405663 17592186405038] [17592186405677 1062 #uuid "cbea8f04-cd79-511a-9ce8-c2ca3f4228a7" 17592186405038] [17592186405677 1063 17592186405830 17592186405038] [17592186405678 1085 17592186405167 17592186405038] [17592186405678 1085 17592186405277 17592186405038] [17592186405678 1085 17592186405367 17592186405038] [17592186405678 1085 17592186405468 17592186405038] [17592186405678 1086 "Source question: 3.\n\nThis problem will take you through the process of solving the initial value problem for the differential equation\n\n$$\ne^xy'+e^xy=0,\\qquad y(0)=y_0.\n$$" 17592186405038] [17592186405678 1087 #uuid "ccc1c766-56f3-5208-a546-5cbf31e2d92d" 17592186405038] [17592186405678 1088 17592186405277 17592186405038] [17592186405679 1089 17592186051076 17592186405015] [17592186405679 1090 17592186405827 17592186405038] [17592186405679 1091 #uuid "cd3c23e0-e779-5100-9cce-fb8fe05ea74b" 17592186405038] [17592186405680 1058 17592186405377 17592186405038] [17592186405680 1062 #uuid "cd5d3306-4537-5d68-96c5-9d7f7dae86ee" 17592186405038] [17592186405680 1063 17592186405282 17592186405038] [17592186405681 1058 17592186405726 17592186405038] [17592186405681 1062 #uuid "cd7cadbc-aff7-56e0-93f4-4e742a59c98c" 17592186405038] [17592186405681 1063 17592186405177 17592186405038] [17592186405682 1058 17592186405807 17592186405038] [17592186405682 1062 #uuid "cd885edb-7326-5878-8bdc-8482b923746a" 17592186405038] [17592186405682 1063 17592186405301 17592186405038] [17592186405683 1068 17592186406167 17592186405015] [17592186405683 1071 "Source question: 9.\n\n**Question 9**\n\nFind the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)=xe^{8y}$. Use component form or standard basis vectors, with exact values.\n\n$\\nabla f(x,y)=$ {{field-1}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405683 1072 #uuid "cdd9ac70-9a19-53e6-a0a5-26ae32b05fbc" 17592186405038] [17592186405683 1074 "The gradient lists partial derivatives. Holding $y$ fixed gives $f_x=e^{8y}$; holding $x$ fixed and using the chain rule gives $f_y=8xe^{8y}$. Thus $\\nabla f=\\langle e^{8y},8xe^{8y}\\rangle$." 17592186405015] [17592186405684 1089 17592186048191 17592186405015] [17592186405684 1090 17592186405125 17592186405038] [17592186405684 1091 #uuid "cedb1d93-2c07-5056-be4e-79abcd1dcb33" 17592186405038] [17592186405685 1058 17592186405792 17592186405038] [17592186405685 1062 #uuid "cf11b0b8-4f07-58fb-98a7-8548897d5184" 17592186405038] [17592186405685 1063 17592186405509 17592186405038] [17592186405686 1068 17592186405899 17592186405015] [17592186405686 1071 "Source question: 7.\n\nUse Stokes’ theorem to find $\\oint_C[2x\\,dx+(3x+y)dy+(6x+y+z)dz]$, where $\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle7\\cos t,7\\sin t,7\\rangle$, 0≤t≤2π, counterclockwise.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405686 1072 #uuid "cf20a424-ee80-53f7-a6d6-b5756b63d69b" 17592186405038] [17592186405686 1074 "The curl of (2x,3x+y,6x+y+z) is (1,−6,3). Use the horizontal radius-7 disk with upward normal. Only its third component contributes: 3·π·7²=147π." 17592186405015] [17592186405687 1058 17592186405157 17592186405038] [17592186405687 1062 #uuid "cf50a1b8-4bd9-5015-88e2-9777bbd91b90" 17592186405038] [17592186405687 1063 17592186405445 17592186405038] [17592186405688 1062 #uuid "cf68eb88-e289-5d7b-b25d-70ab43d638f7" 17592186405038] [17592186405688 1063 17592186405087 17592186405038] [17592186405689 1058 17592186405212 17592186405038] [17592186405689 1062 #uuid "cf9a956c-a3c0-5013-88d8-c23a69c30128" 17592186405038] [17592186405689 1063 17592186405145 17592186405038] [17592186405690 1058 17592186405580 17592186405038] [17592186405690 1062 #uuid "cf9bbf2b-0ec0-523b-95f0-77cede06aad7" 17592186405038] [17592186405690 1063 17592186405497 17592186405038] [17592186405691 1089 17592186051076 17592186405015] [17592186405691 1090 17592186405521 17592186405038] [17592186405691 1091 #uuid "d04819fe-1fd4-58fc-80dc-e7c819f1bef7" 17592186405038] [17592186405692 1058 17592186405453 17592186405038] [17592186405692 1062 #uuid "d06e5cfd-e454-54fb-9107-f37f55706ac1" 17592186405038] [17592186405692 1063 17592186405053 17592186405038] [17592186405693 1089 17592186050295 17592186405015] [17592186405693 1089 17592186050866 17592186405015] [17592186405693 1090 17592186405703 17592186405038] [17592186405693 1091 #uuid "d0e0182c-e4b0-5a55-9bf6-975c2ffeb6a1" 17592186405038] [17592186405694 1068 17592186406126 17592186405015] [17592186405694 1071 "Source question: 12.\n\nFind $\\iint_S\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,dS$ for $\\mathbf F=\\langle8z,3x,6y\\rangle$ on the hemisphere $z=\\sqrt{1-x^2-y^2}$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405694 1072 #uuid "d1053cb4-da92-53ac-af0a-f0cd8596195a" 17592186405038] [17592186405694 1074 "For the outward normal on the unit sphere, F·n=8xz+3xy+6yz. Each term integrates to zero by reflection symmetry: xz and xy change sign under x→−x; yz changes sign under y→−y. Thus flux is 0. The source omits orientation, but reversing the normal leaves this zero answer unchanged. A closed-surface check: div F=0 and the closing disk has flux ∫∫−6y dA=0." 17592186405015] [17592186405695 1058 17592186405513 17592186405038] [17592186405695 1062 #uuid "d158188b-039a-5da9-b4c8-2395d93ac7a2" 17592186405038] [17592186405695 1063 17592186405469 17592186405038] [17592186405696 1089 17592186049090 17592186405015] [17592186405696 1089 17592186049487 17592186405015] [17592186405696 1089 17592186051022 17592186405015] [17592186405696 1090 17592186405068 17592186405038] [17592186405696 1091 #uuid "d19d6430-b467-59eb-bb30-e7e16af7e002" 17592186405038] [17592186405697 1043 17592186405184 17592186405038] [17592186405697 1043 17592186405217 17592186405038] [17592186405697 1043 17592186405262 17592186405038] [17592186405697 1043 17592186405305 17592186405038] [17592186405697 1043 17592186405597 17592186405038] [17592186405697 1043 17592186405682 17592186405038] [17592186405697 1043 17592186405807 17592186405038] [17592186405697 1044 17592186045461 17592186405038] [17592186405697 1045 "MTH 255 — WHW-1" 17592186405038] [17592186405697 1046 17592186405184 17592186405038] [17592186405697 1047 "2026-10-02T06:59:59.000Z" 17592186405038] [17592186405697 1048 17592186405043 17592186405038] [17592186405697 1053 #uuid "d228b840-7895-5943-adef-4948618719ff" 17592186405038] [17592186405698 1089 17592186050149 17592186405015] [17592186405698 1089 17592186050891 17592186405015] [17592186405698 1090 17592186405728 17592186405038] [17592186405698 1091 #uuid "d2a32df3-b2b0-5fc1-99ff-ee4ea60f03f5" 17592186405038] [17592186405699 1058 17592186405536 17592186405038] [17592186405699 1062 #uuid "d2a56faf-af04-5ae6-8a28-c595bdefbae4" 17592186405038] [17592186405699 1063 17592186405134 17592186405038] [17592186405700 1058 17592186405630 17592186405038] [17592186405700 1062 #uuid "d2d0d0e6-045a-52e5-8404-4ef75dfab311" 17592186405038] [17592186405700 1063 17592186405327 17592186405038] [17592186405701 1089 17592186050149 17592186405015] [17592186405701 1089 17592186050875 17592186405015] [17592186405701 1090 17592186405634 17592186405038] [17592186405701 1091 #uuid "d3826e30-2e00-5141-b857-f124c244215e" 17592186405038] [17592186405702 1068 17592186406248 17592186405015] [17592186405702 1071 "Source question: 8.\n\nFind $\\iint_R7(x+y)\\sin(x-y)\\,dA$ on the triangle with vertices (−5,5), (5,5), (0,0). Use $u=x+y$, $v=x-y$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405702 1072 #uuid "d3dd8f6e-8215-53df-be23-e99770146c42" 17592186405038] [17592186405702 1074 "The inverse is $x=(u+v)/2$, $y=(u-v)/2$, so $|J|=1/2$. The transformed vertices are (0,−10),(10,0),(0,0), giving 0≤u≤10 and u−10≤v≤0. Hence $I=\\frac72\\int_0^{10}u[\\cos(u-10)-1]du=\\frac72[(1-\\cos10)-50]$. Angles are in radians." 17592186405015] [17592186405703 1068 17592186406289 17592186405015] [17592186405703 1071 "Source question: 10.\n\nA thin wire follows $x=9\\sin(2t),\\ y=9\\cos(2t),\\ 0\\le t\\le\\pi/4$ and has density $\\rho(x,y)=x+y+2$. Find its mass M.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405703 1072 #uuid "d3e54510-4687-590a-be32-120f1c624f18" 17592186405038] [17592186405703 1074 "Mass is $\\int_C\\rho ds$. Speed is 18, so $M=18\\int_0^{\\pi/4}[9\\sin2t+9\\cos2t+2]dt=18(9+\\pi/2)$." 17592186405015] [17592186405704 1058 17592186405836 17592186405038] [17592186405704 1062 #uuid "d4009f05-25ea-52df-b2b2-b8c53b974334" 17592186405038] [17592186405704 1063 17592186405079 17592186405038] [17592186405705 1089 17592186049462 17592186405015] [17592186405705 1089 17592186049494 17592186405015] [17592186405705 1089 17592186049678 17592186405015] [17592186405705 1090 17592186405325 17592186405038] [17592186405705 1091 #uuid "d451fcf0-e709-5f5b-bc5d-c3222d8e6001" 17592186405038] [17592186405706 1058 17592186405413 17592186405038] [17592186405706 1062 #uuid "d4755237-ab6e-5ec2-afe8-8e4c721bbd72" 17592186405038] [17592186405706 1063 17592186405676 17592186405038] [17592186405707 1089 17592186048191 17592186405015] [17592186405707 1090 17592186405617 17592186405038] [17592186405707 1091 #uuid "d48937b5-a399-52c7-b85d-88679065d110" 17592186405038] [17592186405708 1058 17592186405709 17592186405038] [17592186405708 1062 #uuid "d4f76116-cf49-59d7-ae0b-aac0e0858718" 17592186405038] [17592186405708 1063 17592186405056 17592186405038] [17592186405709 1058 17592186405339 17592186405038] [17592186405709 1062 #uuid "d5014aee-a4f7-5170-9825-f4993aecea71" 17592186405038] [17592186405709 1063 17592186405335 17592186405038] [17592186405710 1068 17592186406071 17592186405015] [17592186405710 1071 "**(c)** C consists of the line segment(s) joining $(7\\pi,0)\\to(7\\pi,1)\\to(8\\pi,1)$, in that order.\n\nIntegral: {{integral}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405710 1072 #uuid "d509aa3e-98a3-523f-b220-a79511ebf3d7" 17592186405038] [17592186405710 1074 "The potential is $f=e^{2y}\\sin x$. Both endpoint values vanish, because sin(7π)=sin(8π)=0. Each path therefore has integral 0; intermediate contributions cancel." 17592186405015] [17592186405711 1058 17592186405122 17592186405038] [17592186405711 1062 #uuid "d6f7492d-9674-5cca-9654-1ff0dfe7e99e" 17592186405038] [17592186405711 1063 17592186405172 17592186405038] [17592186405712 1068 17592186406236 17592186405015] [17592186405712 1071 "**Question 2 — Part A**\n\nComplete the rectangular equation: $y=$ {{field-1}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405712 1072 #uuid "d710ec0b-3410-5b66-9a71-891c2cf88b4c" 17592186405038] [17592186405712 1074 "Solve $x=8t+1$ for $t=(x-1)/8$. Substitution gives $y=(x-1)/8+8=(x+63)/8$." 17592186405015] [17592186405713 1068 17592186406072 17592186405015] [17592186405713 1071 "Source question: 6.\n\n**Question 6**\n\nFind the arc length for $x(t)=7t^3$, $y(t)=8t^2$, $0\\le t\\le2$. Give an exact answer.\n\n$s=$ {{field-1}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405713 1072 #uuid "d722ad10-0137-5a5f-a2db-bf50b5c530a9" 17592186405038] [17592186405713 1074 "Arc length integrates speed: $s=\\int_0^2\\sqrt{(21t^2)^2+(16t)^2}\\,dt=\\int_0^2t\\sqrt{441t^2+256}\\,dt$, since $t\\ge0$. With $u=441t^2+256$, this is $[(441t^2+256)^{3/2}/1323]_0^2=(2020^{3/2}-4096)/1323$." 17592186405015] [17592186405714 1089 17592186051091 17592186405015] [17592186405714 1090 17592186405382 17592186405038] [17592186405714 1091 #uuid "d758e0ad-d448-5694-826e-683c9e4dd7ad" 17592186405038] [17592186405715 1058 17592186405337 17592186405038] [17592186405715 1062 #uuid "d7d83b65-a0b1-5646-9386-8a554303845f" 17592186405038] [17592186405715 1063 17592186405048 17592186405038] [17592186405716 1043 17592186405114 17592186405038] [17592186405716 1043 17592186405146 17592186405038] [17592186405716 1043 17592186405275 17592186405038] [17592186405716 1043 17592186405430 17592186405038] [17592186405716 1043 17592186405492 17592186405038] [17592186405716 1043 17592186405574 17592186405038] [17592186405716 1043 17592186405761 17592186405038] [17592186405716 1044 17592186045461 17592186405038] [17592186405716 1045 "MTH 341 — WHW-1" 17592186405038] [17592186405716 1046 17592186405430 17592186405038] [17592186405716 1047 "2026-10-08T06:59:59.000Z" 17592186405038] [17592186405716 1048 17592186405045 17592186405038] [17592186405716 1053 #uuid "d7fc793d-feb8-5eb5-8b5f-dd45c5217485" 17592186405038] [17592186405717 1058 17592186405203 17592186405038] [17592186405717 1062 #uuid "d844ea27-8b17-5fbb-ad56-d4f9d28782f2" 17592186405038] [17592186405717 1063 17592186405245 17592186405038] [17592186405718 1062 #uuid "d92af706-c458-5b60-b8aa-6f131421e9b7" 17592186405038] [17592186405718 1063 17592186405073 17592186405038] [17592186405719 1068 17592186406209 17592186405015] [17592186405719 1071 "Source question: 1.\n\nEvaluate $\\oint_C3y\\,dx$ counterclockwise around the rectangle with vertices (0,0), (2,0), (2,6), (0,6).\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405719 1072 #uuid "d9765851-c4fa-5968-853c-7308afe7393b" 17592186405038] [17592186405719 1074 "$P=3y$, Q=0, so $Q_x-P_y=-3$. Green’s theorem gives −3 times the rectangle area 12, or −36. Directly, only the top horizontal side contributes: $\\int_2^0 18dx=-36$." 17592186405015] [17592186405720 1089 17592186048314 17592186405015] [17592186405720 1089 17592186050875 17592186405015] [17592186405720 1090 17592186405594 17592186405038] [17592186405720 1091 #uuid "d9d3e283-11b8-544e-9ae0-9c3d5b24f626" 17592186405038] [17592186405721 1043 17592186405339 17592186405038] [17592186405721 1043 17592186405448 17592186405038] [17592186405721 1043 17592186405681 17592186405038] [17592186405721 1043 17592186405708 17592186405038] [17592186405721 1043 17592186405709 17592186405038] [17592186405721 1043 17592186405726 17592186405038] [17592186405721 1043 17592186405772 17592186405038] [17592186405721 1044 17592186045461 17592186405038] [17592186405721 1045 "MTH 255 — OHW-1" 17592186405038] [17592186405721 1046 17592186405681 17592186405038] [17592186405721 1047 "2026-10-02T06:59:59.000Z" 17592186405038] [17592186405721 1048 17592186405043 17592186405038] [17592186405721 1053 #uuid "d9f7bb2a-5df2-5bda-8b57-d6041986e95e" 17592186405038] [17592186405722 1089 17592186048703 17592186405015] [17592186405722 1089 17592186050911 17592186405015] [17592186405722 1090 17592186405615 17592186405038] [17592186405722 1091 #uuid "da5939c4-e4df-53af-9400-a59c9098fbf1" 17592186405038] [17592186405723 1068 17592186405927 17592186405015] [17592186405723 1071 "Source question: 17.\n\nFind the work of $\\mathbf F=\\langle7y,3x\\rangle$ along $\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle t,t^2\\rangle$ from t=0 to t=1.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405723 1072 #uuid "da9aa18c-1981-5101-908b-64dab995c7b4" 17592186405038] [17592186405723 1074 "$F(r)=(7t^2,3t)$, $r\\prime=(1,2t)$. Thus $W=\\int_0^1(7t^2+6t^2)dt=13/3$." 17592186405015] [17592186405724 1058 17592186405690 17592186405038] [17592186405724 1062 #uuid "daa01da3-4450-5b38-881d-623798cc57ed" 17592186405038] [17592186405724 1063 17592186405319 17592186405038] [17592186405725 1089 17592186049465 17592186405015] [17592186405725 1090 17592186405742 17592186405038] [17592186405725 1091 #uuid "dabc4606-c882-5f7e-ac69-b2a6e88fc17b" 17592186405038] [17592186405726 1058 17592186405772 17592186405038] [17592186405726 1062 #uuid "daed8cc4-6f11-5459-a8b7-573118d05c4e" 17592186405038] [17592186405726 1063 17592186405705 17592186405038] [17592186405727 1062 #uuid "dc060909-fbde-54ab-bdf4-e877866a84a9" 17592186405038] [17592186405727 1063 17592186405171 17592186405038] [17592186405728 1068 17592186406090 17592186405015] [17592186405728 1071 "Source question: 10.\n\n**Question 10**\n\nFind the gradient vector field of $f(x,y,z)=x^6y+4xyz^8$. Use component form or standard basis vectors, with exact values.\n\n$\\nabla f(x,y,z)=$ {{field-1}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405728 1072 #uuid "dcd664ee-2da1-56d0-824b-dd7a1e2b4c71" 17592186405038] [17592186405728 1074 "Differentiate with respect to one variable at a time, holding the others fixed: $f_x=6x^5y+4yz^8$, $f_y=x^6+4xz^8$, and $f_z=32xyz^7$. These are the three gradient components." 17592186405015] [17592186405729 1058 17592186405465 17592186405038] [17592186405729 1062 #uuid "dd06ab9c-1378-5157-874c-2994ed23c88a" 17592186405038] [17592186405729 1063 17592186405626 17592186405038] [17592186405730 1068 17592186406275 17592186405015] [17592186405730 1071 "Source question: 3.\n\nEvaluate $\\oint_C8y(x^2+y^2)dx-8x(x^2+y^2)dy$ counterclockwise around the unit circle $x^2+y^2=1$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405730 1072 #uuid "dd087078-f11d-57cf-a82c-11a691c0bd23" 17592186405038] [17592186405730 1074 "$Q_x-P_y=-32(x^2+y^2)$. Over the unit disk the double integral is $-32\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\int_0^1r^3drd\\theta=-16\\pi$. On the circle, direct substitution also gives the constant −8 in the θ integrand." 17592186405015] [17592186405731 1071 "**Question 3 — Part C**\n\nDraw $x^2+y^2=1$ and a couple of tangent lines. Compare their slopes with the derivative from Part B." 17592186405038] [17592186405731 1072 #uuid "dd117d7e-f3a2-5134-9e0e-3ab8f8e2b9af" 17592186405038] [17592186405731 1074 "Draw the unit circle and, for example, tangents at $(0,1)$ and $(1/\\sqrt2,1/\\sqrt2)$. The first tangent is $y=1$ with slope $0=-0/1$. The second is $y=-x+\\sqrt2$ with slope $-1=-(1/\\sqrt2)/(1/\\sqrt2)$. Both agree with $dy/dx=-x/y$. Other correctly drawn and checked tangent points are valid.\n\n![Authored reference graph](images/73/730c9f459fe4f68e944d2fa043f43b145dd3b0b237ebb20533b13aefd6bdacad.png)\n\nLabel each chosen point and tangent. A tangent to a circle is perpendicular to its radius, which provides a geometric check on the slope. If you choose $(\\pm1,0)$, use a vertical tangent and explain why $-x/y$ is undefined there." 17592186405015] [17592186405732 1068 17592186405942 17592186405015] [17592186405732 1068 17592186406224 17592186405015] [17592186405732 1071 "**(c)** $\\phi=\\frac\\pi2-\\tan^{-1}(x/y)$. Find its gradient field and the points excluded from its domain.\n\nGradient: {{gradient}}.\n\nExcluded points: {{excluded}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405732 1072 #uuid "dd682f09-fbef-5c58-90e4-4d25459c6e1c" 17592186405038] [17592186405732 1074 "Differentiate the negative arctangent: $\\phi_x=-(1/y)/(1+x^2/y^2)$ and $\\phi_y=(x/y^2)/(1+x^2/y^2)$. The gradient agrees with part (b) where both potentials are defined, but this potential requires y≠0." 17592186405015] [17592186405733 1058 17592186405330 17592186405038] [17592186405733 1062 #uuid "de7015b4-415c-5196-aff3-b73808ad2dde" 17592186405038] [17592186405733 1063 17592186405826 17592186405038] [17592186405734 1089 17592186048152 17592186405015] [17592186405734 1089 17592186049536 17592186405015] [17592186405734 1090 17592186405812 17592186405038] [17592186405734 1091 #uuid "def8ce76-1a65-508f-b960-7f88c9b503e1" 17592186405038] [17592186405735 1089 17592186048495 17592186405015] [17592186405735 1089 17592186048703 17592186405015] [17592186405735 1090 17592186405578 17592186405038] [17592186405735 1091 #uuid "df010457-65d2-5af9-b384-977b8ae9816a" 17592186405038] [17592186405736 1089 17592186048451 17592186405015] [17592186405736 1089 17592186048684 17592186405015] [17592186405736 1089 17592186048832 17592186405015] [17592186405736 1090 17592186405531 17592186405038] [17592186405736 1091 #uuid "df38b813-7b1f-58a1-b938-d0468354bf9e" 17592186405038] [17592186405737 1089 17592186049462 17592186405015] [17592186405737 1089 17592186049494 17592186405015] [17592186405737 1089 17592186049678 17592186405015] [17592186405737 1090 17592186405317 17592186405038] [17592186405737 1091 #uuid "df687ff5-4d56-5697-a482-53619d2295c0" 17592186405038] [17592186405738 1058 17592186405557 17592186405038] [17592186405738 1062 #uuid "dfa44c60-0cb7-5441-abdb-da153f2b5342" 17592186405038] [17592186405738 1063 17592186405156 17592186405038] [17592186405739 1089 17592186051091 17592186405015] [17592186405739 1090 17592186405209 17592186405038] [17592186405739 1091 #uuid "dfb256d5-f9ef-5345-9e8f-358967b9d46a" 17592186405038] [17592186405740 1068 17592186406152 17592186405015] [17592186405740 1071 "Source question: 16.\n\nFind $\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot d\\mathbf r$ for $\\mathbf F=\\langle e^x,e^{-y},e^{2z}\\rangle$, $\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle5t,t^2,t^3\\rangle$, $0\\le t\\le1$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405740 1072 #uuid "dfb3a1bb-8c72-53d7-a353-5ad1f2728b94" 17592186405038] [17592186405740 1074 "The dot product is $5e^{5t}+2te^{-t^2}+3t^2e^{2t^3}$. An antiderivative is $e^{5t}-e^{-t^2}+e^{2t^3}/2$. Evaluate at 1 and 0; the endpoint potential $e^x-e^{-y}+e^{2z}/2$ gives the same check." 17592186405015] [17592186405741 1058 17592186405593 17592186405038] [17592186405741 1062 #uuid "dfb76180-f78c-590f-be3a-d84cb65c6965" 17592186405038] [17592186405741 1063 17592186405265 17592186405038] [17592186405742 1068 17592186406127 17592186405015] [17592186405742 1071 "Source question: 4.\n\nFind the divergence of $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)=\\langle7xy^2,7x^2y,7xyz\\rangle$. \n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405742 1072 #uuid "dfff358c-84da-515d-b874-fde14bf747d6" 17592186405038] [17592186405742 1074 "The derivatives are $P_x=7y^2$, $Q_y=7x^2$, $R_z=7xy$. There is no z in the final divergence." 17592186405015] [17592186405743 1089 17592186049639 17592186405015] [17592186405743 1089 17592186050866 17592186405015] [17592186405743 1090 17592186405408 17592186405038] [17592186405743 1091 #uuid "e00bb879-453c-5a62-96bd-014e25c89fa0" 17592186405038] [17592186405744 1089 17592186050168 17592186405015] [17592186405744 1089 17592186050537 17592186405015] [17592186405744 1090 17592186405049 17592186405038] [17592186405744 1091 #uuid "e0351426-14cb-5d1b-bfe0-bbacecf95ca1" 17592186405038] [17592186405745 1058 17592186405244 17592186405038] [17592186405745 1062 #uuid "e04c1268-8c34-59a6-b273-db1d8d17b5a8" 17592186405038] [17592186405745 1063 17592186405667 17592186405038] [17592186405746 1089 17592186048923 17592186405015] [17592186405746 1089 17592186050418 17592186405015] [17592186405746 1090 17592186405384 17592186405038] [17592186405746 1091 #uuid "e0595952-f3d8-54aa-96f3-f80615b8dabb" 17592186405038] [17592186405747 1089 17592186049889 17592186405015] [17592186405747 1090 17592186405161 17592186405038] [17592186405747 1091 #uuid "e063da91-f0ea-537c-8fc8-fee89a39aef6" 17592186405038] [17592186405748 1064 "Date imported: 2026-09-30\nDue: 2026-10-02\n\nSource: [WeBWorK — WebWork 1](https://webwork2.science.oregonstate.edu/webwork2/MTH_256_020_F2026/WebWork_1/)\n\nSix problems, each worth 1 point. The equations and initial conditions below are transcribed from the assigned problem versions.\n\nStudy answers, explanations, and reference graphics are authored additions to the linked source." 17592186405015] [17592186405748 1066 #uuid "e0c6591e-de96-5883-91c4-67813091a34a" 17592186405038] [17592186405748 1067 "Assignment information" 17592186405038] [17592186405749 1068 17592186405839 17592186405015] [17592186405749 1068 17592186405865 17592186405015] [17592186405749 1068 17592186405867 17592186405015] [17592186405749 1068 17592186405925 17592186405015] [17592186405749 1068 17592186405926 17592186405015] [17592186405749 1068 17592186405936 17592186405015] [17592186405749 1068 17592186405965 17592186405015] [17592186405749 1068 17592186406019 17592186405015] [17592186405749 1068 17592186406151 17592186405015] [17592186405749 1068 17592186406241 17592186405015] [17592186405749 1068 17592186406252 17592186405015] [17592186405749 1068 17592186406285 17592186405015] [17592186405749 1071 "Source question: 1, PDF page 2.\n\nMatch plots A–F to equations (a)–(f) and flow curves (i)–(vi).\n\n![Vector field plots A–F](images/59/59baa4c104233f8ce590e72bb194a56ec065ff68a1220ab92f2e138f39ee43b2.png)\n\n| Equation | Vector field | Curve | Parameterization |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| (a) | $\\langle0,x\\rangle$ | (i) | $\\langle1,t\\rangle$ |\n| (b) | $\\langle y,x\\rangle$ | (ii) | $\\langle\\cos t,\\sin t\\rangle$ |\n| (c) | $\\langle x,y-x\\rangle$ | (iii) | $\\langle\\tan t,\\sec t\\rangle$ |\n| (d) | $\\langle1,2\\rangle$ | (iv) | $\\langle2t,t\\rangle$ |\n| (e) | $\\langle x,y\\rangle$ | (v) | $\\langle t,2t\\rangle$ |\n| (f) | $\\langle-y,x\\rangle$ | (vi) | $\\langle e^t,e^t-te^t\\rangle$ |\n\nEnter the original equation letters and curve Roman numerals.\n\nPlot A: equation {{eq-a}}; curve {{curve-a}}.\n\nPlot B: equation {{eq-b}}; curve {{curve-b}}.\n\nPlot C: equation {{eq-c}}; curve {{curve-c}}.\n\nPlot D: equation {{eq-d}}; curve {{curve-d}}.\n\nPlot E: equation {{eq-e}}; curve {{curve-e}}.\n\nPlot F: equation {{eq-f}}; curve {{curve-f}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405749 1072 #uuid "e0f837df-c792-5a57-9571-6a1dd942cb26" 17592186405038] [17592186405749 1074 "A is radial, B is constant northeast, C rotates counterclockwise, D is vertical with sign set by x, E has components (x,y-x), and F has components (y,x). Thus A–e–iv, B–d–v, C–f–ii, D–a–i, E–c–vi, F–b–iii.\n\nFor (i), (ii), (v), and (vi), direct differentiation gives $\\mathbf r'=\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r)$. For (iii), $\\mathbf r'=\\sec t\\,\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r)$ on its domain. For (iv), $\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r)=t\\mathbf r'$. Thus the last two are geometrically tangent parameterizations, not solutions of $\\mathbf r'=\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r)$ as written. Curve (iv) passes through the equilibrium at t=0 and follows the field orientation only on t>0; treat its two rays separately. On intervals where sec t<0, curve (iii) has reversed orientation. The supplied matching uses geometric flow paths." 17592186405015] [17592186405750 1058 17592186405755 17592186405038] [17592186405750 1062 #uuid "e16d8b4e-203f-5b3d-957a-5b0ffe692774" 17592186405038] [17592186405750 1063 17592186405798 17592186405038] [17592186405751 1089 17592186050168 17592186405015] [17592186405751 1089 17592186050537 17592186405015] [17592186405751 1090 17592186405080 17592186405038] [17592186405751 1091 #uuid "e1b0357d-422a-51bd-bceb-eda4e1f43c8d" 17592186405038] [17592186405752 1089 17592186048225 17592186405015] [17592186405752 1089 17592186050711 17592186405015] [17592186405752 1090 17592186405787 17592186405038] [17592186405752 1091 #uuid "e1c6f0a7-fc26-5a85-9ec3-4f18892c7a13" 17592186405038] [17592186405753 1068 17592186406191 17592186405015] [17592186405753 1071 "Source question: 8.\n\nA fluid of constant mass density crosses $z=x^2+y^2$, z≤49, with velocity $\\mathbf F=\\langle-x,-y,-z\\rangle$. Find the mass flow in one unit of time in the direction of the outer normal.\n\n**Source gap:** The constant density has no numerical value. For this study answer, denote it by $\\rho_0$; report signed outward mass flow.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405753 1072 #uuid "e1c86b2f-9af3-50b9-8209-64644c12c39a" 17592186405038] [17592186405753 1074 "Interpret outward relative to the solid inside the bowl, capped at z=49. On the curved surface the outward vector area is $(2x,2y,-1)dxdy$, so F·dS=−(x²+y²)dxdy. Its integral over r≤7 is $-2\\pi\\int_0^7r^3dr=-2401\\pi/2$. Multiply by the constant density ρ₀. This is signed outward mass flow; the positive mass entering is 2401πρ₀/2. With unit density, omit ρ₀. The source supplies no density value and does not distinguish signed flow from positive mass." 17592186405015] [17592186405754 1089 17592186049465 17592186405015] [17592186405754 1089 17592186050540 17592186405015] [17592186405754 1090 17592186405314 17592186405038] [17592186405754 1091 #uuid "e20ae9c8-1515-56fd-85af-a2886f8918d1" 17592186405038] [17592186405755 1058 17592186405829 17592186405038] [17592186405755 1062 #uuid "e2e50cce-a68f-55c9-ba58-a9eebf4278ac" 17592186405038] [17592186405755 1063 17592186405224 17592186405038] [17592186405756 1071 "**(b)** Confirm that the given curve is a flow curve for the field.\n\n$\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle e^{3t},1/t,\\ln t\\rangle$ for $t>0$, $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)=\\langle3x,-y^2,y\\rangle$." 17592186405038] [17592186405756 1072 #uuid "e318db23-2661-528d-a7d7-06a3c8e1754d" 17592186405038] [17592186405756 1074 "**verification:** $\\mathbf r\\prime(t)=\\langle3e^{3t},-1/t^2,1/t\\rangle=\\mathbf F(e^{3t},1/t,\\ln t)$ for t>0.\n\nDifferentiate each coordinate of r and separately substitute r into F. Equality of all three components proves $\\mathbf r\\prime=\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r)$ on the stated domain." 17592186405015] [17592186405757 1058 17592186405047 17592186405038] [17592186405757 1062 #uuid "e34002aa-9e7d-5b65-a7a7-d20b02cb6aeb" 17592186405038] [17592186405757 1063 17592186405090 17592186405038] [17592186405758 1058 17592186405117 17592186405038] [17592186405758 1062 #uuid "e36e8ade-b01c-5ffa-841e-00d206708172" 17592186405038] [17592186405758 1063 17592186405454 17592186405038] [17592186405759 1085 17592186405377 17592186405038] [17592186405759 1085 17592186405536 17592186405038] [17592186405759 1085 17592186405680 17592186405038] [17592186405759 1085 17592186405699 17592186405038] [17592186405759 1086 "Source question: 1.\n\nThis problem will review the product and chain rule for derivatives, focusing on aspects that will be useful in this class." 17592186405038] [17592186405759 1087 #uuid "e3b2ca69-7c50-54ff-8b72-5198a7d1095c" 17592186405038] [17592186405759 1088 17592186405699 17592186405038] [17592186405760 1089 17592186050338 17592186405015] [17592186405760 1090 17592186405730 17592186405038] [17592186405760 1091 #uuid "e43f5dc3-1672-5878-bba4-6c06d49f6eb1" 17592186405038] [17592186405761 1058 17592186405492 17592186405038] [17592186405761 1062 #uuid "e46c570e-ace6-5ba1-8571-1c0f50802370" 17592186405038] [17592186405761 1063 17592186405632 17592186405038] [17592186405762 1089 17592186048960 17592186405015] [17592186405762 1089 17592186049859 17592186405015] [17592186405762 1090 17592186405740 17592186405038] [17592186405762 1091 #uuid "e4bd6deb-abd2-5263-980d-aef60891cd91" 17592186405038] [17592186405763 1089 17592186048651 17592186405015] [17592186405763 1089 17592186050866 17592186405015] [17592186405763 1090 17592186405219 17592186405038] [17592186405763 1091 #uuid "e671770b-a69c-5c34-be91-bc21955614af" 17592186405038] [17592186405764 1062 #uuid "e78bbd3e-686a-51a0-9360-51f589e73e6b" 17592186405038] [17592186405764 1063 17592186405544 17592186405038] [17592186405765 1068 17592186405994 17592186405015] [17592186405765 1071 "Source question: 4.\n\nEvaluate $\\oint_C(x^2-13y^3)dx+(x^2+y^2)dy$ counterclockwise around the unit circle $x^2+y^2=1$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405765 1072 #uuid "e7bdcb25-186c-54e2-bf5f-34faaeed962b" 17592186405038] [17592186405765 1074 "$Q_x-P_y=2x+39y^2$. The odd term integrates to zero over the disk; $\\iint y^2dA=\\pi/4$, yielding 39π/4." 17592186405015] [17592186405766 1058 17592186405364 17592186405038] [17592186405766 1062 #uuid "e878eaca-e204-54ad-8ae4-99bb9c46dadc" 17592186405038] [17592186405766 1063 17592186405732 17592186405038] [17592186405767 1062 #uuid "e8aa47a9-f820-5e46-b015-e8fc4c816e0b" 17592186405038] [17592186405767 1063 17592186405402 17592186405038] [17592186405768 1043 17592186405052 17592186405038] [17592186405768 1043 17592186405124 17592186405038] [17592186405768 1043 17592186405326 17592186405038] [17592186405768 1043 17592186405341 17592186405038] [17592186405768 1043 17592186405502 17592186405038] [17592186405768 1043 17592186405576 17592186405038] [17592186405768 1043 17592186405587 17592186405038] [17592186405768 1044 17592186045461 17592186405038] [17592186405768 1045 "MTH 256 — OHW-1" 17592186405038] [17592186405768 1046 17592186405587 17592186405038] [17592186405768 1047 "2026-10-03T06:59:59.000Z" 17592186405038] [17592186405768 1048 17592186405044 17592186405038] [17592186405768 1053 #uuid "e8df8bb1-2a91-5bc7-81ff-b15e99ad4fb7" 17592186405038] [17592186405769 1062 #uuid "e92fea9e-4905-5db2-b39a-dcb875de387b" 17592186405038] [17592186405769 1063 17592186405420 17592186405038] [17592186405770 1068 17592186406093 17592186405015] [17592186405770 1071 "Source question: 6.\n\nFind $\\partial(x,y,z)/\\partial(u,v,w)$ for $x=3(u+v)$, $y=-5u+7w$, $z=6u^5-11w^{10}$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405770 1072 #uuid "e9d22828-f010-55f3-bd94-50509105365c" 17592186405038] [17592186405770 1074 "The matrix is $\\begin{pmatrix}3&3&0\\\\-5&0&7\\\\30u^4&0&-110w^9\\end{pmatrix}$. Expand along the second column: $-3(550w^9-210u^4)=630u^4-1650w^9$." 17592186405015] [17592186405771 1068 17592186405837 17592186405015] [17592186405771 1071 "**(a)** C consists of the line segment(s) joining $(7\\pi,0)\\to(8\\pi,1)$, in that order.\n\nIntegral: {{integral}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405771 1072 #uuid "ea6177a7-b2e1-504a-9207-ace3b3fc5bc8" 17592186405038] [17592186405771 1074 "The potential is $f=e^{2y}\\sin x$. Both endpoint values vanish, because sin(7π)=sin(8π)=0. Each path therefore has integral 0; intermediate contributions cancel." 17592186405015] [17592186405772 1058 17592186405708 17592186405038] [17592186405772 1062 #uuid "eb1a5654-98dd-5670-8962-582143bf2688" 17592186405038] [17592186405772 1063 17592186405228 17592186405038] [17592186405773 1058 17592186405374 17592186405038] [17592186405773 1062 #uuid "eb299a4e-6c71-5fa0-bf75-fca98801c704" 17592186405038] [17592186405773 1063 17592186405133 17592186405038] [17592186405774 1058 17592186405276 17592186405038] [17592186405774 1062 #uuid "ebf94632-64c3-5585-b994-fbb68450f5cb" 17592186405038] [17592186405774 1063 17592186405714 17592186405038] [17592186405775 1062 #uuid "ec773a7f-03c6-5243-8e45-f3ed66d38daf" 17592186405038] [17592186405775 1063 17592186405550 17592186405038] [17592186405776 1062 #uuid "ecb98044-8e28-5a2e-891e-0f813e1caca2" 17592186405038] [17592186405776 1063 17592186405369 17592186405038] [17592186405777 1071 "**Question 1 — Part D**\n\nExpress the following derivative in terms of $p(x)$ and an antiderivative:\n\n$$\n\\frac{d}{dx}\\exp\\left(\\int p(x)\\,dx\\right).\n$$" 17592186405038] [17592186405777 1072 #uuid "ed1748cc-4512-50d3-a9cc-9588a21891c6" 17592186405038] [17592186405777 1074 "Let $P(x)$ be any antiderivative of $p(x)$. Then $\\frac{d}{dx}e^{P(x)}=P\\prime(x)e^{P(x)}=p(x)e^{P(x)}=p(x)\\exp(\\int p(x)\\,dx)$.\n\nApply the exponential chain rule and then the definition $P\\prime=p$. The additive constant in $P$ only changes a constant multiplicative factor in the exponential; the displayed derivative identity remains valid." 17592186405015] [17592186405778 1089 17592186048225 17592186405015] [17592186405778 1089 17592186050488 17592186405015] [17592186405778 1090 17592186405702 17592186405038] [17592186405778 1091 #uuid "ee1bd779-0028-505a-947f-385fe4ddd1cd" 17592186405038] [17592186405779 1068 17592186406041 17592186405015] [17592186405779 1071 "Source question: 15.\n\nFind $\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot d\\mathbf r$ for $\\mathbf F=\\langle16x^2,xy\\rangle$, $\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle\\sin t,-4\\cos t\\rangle$, $0\\le t\\le\\pi$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405779 1072 #uuid "ee65c84c-2102-5bc7-9a35-fadda3ae61dc" 17592186405038] [17592186405779 1074 "$F(r)=(16\\sin^2t,-4\\sin t\\cos t)$ and $r\\prime=(\\cos t,4\\sin t)$. Their dot product is identically zero. This checks the integral without evaluating an antiderivative." 17592186405015] [17592186405780 1089 17592186048575 17592186405015] [17592186405780 1089 17592186049152 17592186405015] [17592186405780 1090 17592186405581 17592186405038] [17592186405780 1091 #uuid "ee8e996b-8a4e-5d5a-bad4-5e805d0799f2" 17592186405038] [17592186405781 1089 17592186049465 17592186405015] [17592186405781 1089 17592186050540 17592186405015] [17592186405781 1090 17592186405237 17592186405038] [17592186405781 1091 #uuid "eebe1f82-0161-57e2-bbb2-93e6adda5141" 17592186405038] [17592186405782 1071 "**(a)** Draw F and C in the same coordinate axes." 17592186405038] [17592186405782 1072 #uuid "eecbf7f9-2e6a-5d87-8343-589852d93d37" 17592186405038] [17592186405782 1074 "**drawing:** Draw the radius-a circle and equal-length arrows circulating counterclockwise around the origin. At (a,0), (0,a), (−a,0), (0,−a), arrows point up, left, down, right. The field is undefined at the origin.\n\n![Authored tangential field reference](images/5c/5c6173bdda7a93fc42142645d1013b63a881e47510dcde4ea6ae142354a2b7d9.png)\n\n$(-y,x)$ is (x,y) rotated 90 degrees counterclockwise. Its magnitude is $\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}$, so normalization gives unit arrows." 17592186405015] [17592186405783 1089 17592186049487 17592186405015] [17592186405783 1089 17592186050537 17592186405015] [17592186405783 1089 17592186050866 17592186405015] [17592186405783 1090 17592186405713 17592186405038] [17592186405783 1091 #uuid "eee91690-0a9b-5f65-9410-0f39a05d774d" 17592186405038] [17592186405784 1058 17592186405154 17592186405038] [17592186405784 1062 #uuid "ef0febbf-6151-596b-8605-d426f7d4fb85" 17592186405038] [17592186405784 1063 17592186405378 17592186405038] [17592186405785 1089 17592186048575 17592186405015] [17592186405785 1089 17592186049487 17592186405015] [17592186405785 1089 17592186050614 17592186405015] [17592186405785 1090 17592186405577 17592186405038] [17592186405785 1091 #uuid "ef25a050-05d7-5a1b-9216-560cd97b753c" 17592186405038] [17592186405786 1089 17592186049465 17592186405015] [17592186405786 1089 17592186050540 17592186405015] [17592186405786 1090 17592186405452 17592186405038] [17592186405786 1091 #uuid "f01b9730-4bf4-56db-849b-ec52e051f3be" 17592186405038] [17592186405787 1068 17592186406050 17592186405015] [17592186405787 1071 "Source question: 12.\n\nUse a change of variables to find the area enclosed by $x+y=4$, $x+y=-7$, $y=7x$, and $y=7x+7$. Give the area in square units.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405787 1072 #uuid "f02b8cf2-3664-5402-abdc-628228493f50" 17592186405038] [17592186405787 1074 "Choose u=x+y and v=y−7x. The new rectangle has −7≤u≤4 and 0≤v≤7. Inverting gives x=(u−v)/8,y=(7u+v)/8 and $|J|=1/8$. The area is 11·7/8=77/8 square units." 17592186405015] [17592186405788 1085 17592186405586 17592186405038] [17592186405788 1085 17592186405649 17592186405038] [17592186405788 1085 17592186405801 17592186405038] [17592186405788 1086 "Source question: 3.\n\n**Implicit differentiation — single variable**\n\nLet $f(x,y)$ be a scalar function with level curve $f(x,y)=c$. Regard $y$ as a function of $x$, so the dependence tree is:\n\n![f depends on x and y, and both branches depend on x](images/c9/c90c3e7d7c66ca010b15db00d492aa6278a31550025ce0353a2647a7412c4fa6.png)" 17592186405038] [17592186405788 1087 #uuid "f035b023-8e26-5b16-b106-3d3ee958a249" 17592186405038] [17592186405788 1088 17592186405649 17592186405038] [17592186405789 1058 17592186405387 17592186405038] [17592186405789 1062 #uuid "f05a8180-6025-50f9-b69c-b42f6bffd256" 17592186405038] [17592186405789 1063 17592186405486 17592186405038] [17592186405790 1058 17592186405241 17592186405038] [17592186405790 1062 #uuid "f061ed43-a27c-5162-b084-d6a172142cd8" 17592186405038] [17592186405790 1063 17592186405693 17592186405038] [17592186405791 1068 17592186406078 17592186405015] [17592186405791 1071 "**Question 2 — Part C**\n\n$y'(x)=5\\sin(x),\\quad y(0)=10$.\n\n$y(x)=$ {{field-1}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405791 1072 #uuid "f06eea79-3c3d-5368-935f-83b346a40f7f" 17592186405038] [17592186405791 1074 "An antiderivative of $5\\sin x$ is $-5\\cos x$. Setting $-5+C=10$ gives $C=15$, and $y\\prime=5\\sin x$." 17592186405015] [17592186405792 1058 17592186405554 17592186405038] [17592186405792 1062 #uuid "f07e17ad-26f9-5b56-86d8-0353ce3eeff6" 17592186405038] [17592186405792 1063 17592186405350 17592186405038] [17592186405793 1058 17592186405246 17592186405038] [17592186405793 1062 #uuid "f08aca7c-b062-580e-b874-dc9d45aa927a" 17592186405038] [17592186405793 1063 17592186405832 17592186405038] [17592186405794 1058 17592186405458 17592186405038] [17592186405794 1062 #uuid "f0a454e6-7ee4-5e68-9a6e-7ecfbaf54446" 17592186405038] [17592186405794 1063 17592186405143 17592186405038] [17592186405795 1058 17592186405711 17592186405038] [17592186405795 1062 #uuid "f0b08bfd-b17f-5b46-82c2-2d3419b4e400" 17592186405038] [17592186405795 1063 17592186405698 17592186405038] [17592186405796 1064 "**5.7 Change of Variables in Multiple Integrals**\n\nDue: 2026-11-12\nSource: [Macmillan Achieve](https://achieve.macmillanlearning.com/ihub/assessment/f188d950-dd73-11e0-9572-0800200c9a66/5a1db2a7-4295-4a4c-9dbb-ceb8b174a282/d5d2efff-1127-4196-836e-e717fd7546a3)\n\nStudy answers and explanations are authored additions, not publisher solutions.\n\n## Instructions\n\nGive exact answers using symbolic notation and fractions. Use the stated substitutions in Questions 7–9 and 11; use a change of variables in Question 12. Source: Sullivan 2e Calculus, W.H. Freeman. Unlimited attempts, no incorrect-attempt penalty; up to 50 days late with a 2% daily penalty." 17592186405015] [17592186405796 1066 #uuid "f0cfc042-4690-5376-9d94-c198873fcc47" 17592186405038] [17592186405796 1067 "Assignment information" 17592186405038] [17592186405797 1043 17592186405064 17592186405038] [17592186405797 1043 17592186405122 17592186405038] [17592186405797 1043 17592186405140 17592186405038] [17592186405797 1043 17592186405208 17592186405038] [17592186405797 1043 17592186405354 17592186405038] [17592186405797 1043 17592186405371 17592186405038] [17592186405797 1043 17592186405386 17592186405038] [17592186405797 1043 17592186405394 17592186405038] [17592186405797 1043 17592186405415 17592186405038] [17592186405797 1043 17592186405435 17592186405038] [17592186405797 1043 17592186405653 17592186405038] [17592186405797 1043 17592186405711 17592186405038] [17592186405797 1043 17592186405795 17592186405038] [17592186405797 1044 17592186045461 17592186405038] [17592186405797 1045 "MTH 255 — OHW-3" 17592186405038] [17592186405797 1046 17592186405415 17592186405038] [17592186405797 1047 "2026-10-09T06:59:59.000Z" 17592186405038] [17592186405797 1048 17592186405043 17592186405038] [17592186405797 1053 #uuid "f14361d7-1ae5-5b0d-b048-a3fafce13685" 17592186405038] [17592186405798 1068 17592186406196 17592186405015] [17592186405798 1071 "**Question 1 — Part A**\n\nFind the rate of change of the voltage at $P$ in the direction of $\\mathbf v$.\n\nRate: {{field-1}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405798 1072 #uuid "f15adcb9-fdf3-562e-a671-9c4b91b63f9d" 17592186405038] [17592186405798 1074 "$\\nabla V=\\langle3x^2-5y+yz,-5x+xz,xy\\rangle$, so $\\nabla V(P)=\\langle12,0,16\\rangle$. Normalize the given direction: $\\mathbf u=\\langle1,1,-1\\rangle/\\sqrt3$. Then $D_{\\mathbf u}V=\\nabla V(P)\\cdot\\mathbf u=(12-16)/\\sqrt3=-4/\\sqrt3$. The negative sign means voltage decreases in that direction." 17592186405015] [17592186405799 1058 17592186405460 17592186405038] [17592186405799 1062 #uuid "f16dc292-d9e5-5e5b-aff4-a6ff6fb35864" 17592186405038] [17592186405799 1063 17592186405657 17592186405038] [17592186405800 1058 17592186405764 17592186405038] [17592186405800 1062 #uuid "f16f2b01-b52d-5f68-9717-f4c02611825d" 17592186405038] [17592186405800 1063 17592186405194 17592186405038] [17592186405801 1058 17592186405586 17592186405038] [17592186405801 1062 #uuid "f18de478-6a29-5826-9a70-d241aefb98a9" 17592186405038] [17592186405801 1063 17592186405484 17592186405038] [17592186405802 1068 17592186405961 17592186405015] [17592186405802 1071 "Source question: 8.\n\nThe field $\\mathbf F=\\langle\\ln y+12x,x/y\\rangle$ is conservative. Find a potential f. Use K for the arbitrary constant and absorb into K as much as possible.\n\n$f(x,y)=$ {{potential}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405802 1072 #uuid "f1c08da6-fd73-585c-82ce-6fe086b59b48" 17592186405038] [17592186405802 1074 "For y>0, integration in x gives $f=x\\ln y+6x^2+g(y)$. Then $f_y=x/y+g′(y)$, so g′=0. The domain condition y>0 comes from ln y." 17592186405015] [17592186405803 1089 17592186050168 17592186405015] [17592186405803 1089 17592186050537 17592186405015] [17592186405803 1090 17592186405508 17592186405038] [17592186405803 1091 #uuid "f2b5cff3-213f-53ca-8ea3-859ef8ee41ba" 17592186405038] [17592186405804 1089 17592186049465 17592186405015] [17592186405804 1090 17592186405295 17592186405038] [17592186405804 1091 #uuid "f2e054bd-a748-518f-94f0-1f970d7b36c6" 17592186405038] [17592186405805 1068 17592186405941 17592186405015] [17592186405805 1068 17592186406010 17592186405015] [17592186405805 1071 "**(d)** $\\phi=\\frac{\\ln(x^2+y^2+z^2)}2$. Find its gradient field and the points excluded from its domain.\n\nGradient: {{gradient}}.\n\nExcluded points: {{excluded}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405805 1072 #uuid "f374f30e-0227-5a2c-bc70-67159330b8cb" 17592186405038] [17592186405805 1074 "The logarithm requires $x^2+y^2+z^2>0$. Differentiating gives $\\phi_x=x/(x^2+y^2+z^2)$ and analogous y,z components. No additional points are lost." 17592186405015] [17592186405806 1085 17592186405110 17592186405038] [17592186405806 1085 17592186405555 17592186405038] [17592186405806 1086 "Source question: 4.\n\nThe temperature in a room is given by the function\n\n$$\nw=f(x,y,z)=x^2+y^2+z^2.\n$$" 17592186405038] [17592186405806 1087 #uuid "f3be8c40-72a0-544b-a839-8210545fba3b" 17592186405038] [17592186405806 1088 17592186405555 17592186405038] [17592186405807 1058 17592186405217 17592186405038] [17592186405807 1062 #uuid "f48c3124-ece4-5231-8cf9-009a2667451b" 17592186405038] [17592186405807 1063 17592186405416 17592186405038] [17592186405808 1089 17592186048960 17592186405015] [17592186405808 1089 17592186049138 17592186405015] [17592186405808 1089 17592186050033 17592186405015] [17592186405808 1089 17592186050037 17592186405015] [17592186405808 1089 17592186050164 17592186405015] [17592186405808 1090 17592186405556 17592186405038] [17592186405808 1091 #uuid "f4af1ee3-e144-57c6-bb2c-049453871f1b" 17592186405038] [17592186405809 1089 17592186048923 17592186405015] [17592186405809 1089 17592186050418 17592186405015] [17592186405809 1090 17592186405833 17592186405038] [17592186405809 1091 #uuid "f5474d71-16e8-5da7-8d3c-b7911aaa5cc3" 17592186405038] [17592186405810 1068 17592186406155 17592186405015] [17592186405810 1071 "Source question: 1.\n\nFind the divergence of $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)=\\langle9x^2,9y^2,3z^4\\rangle$. \n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405810 1072 #uuid "f5c6335f-9942-5a9b-8c8b-2470acaea4f5" 17592186405038] [17592186405810 1074 "The three same-coordinate derivatives are 18x, 18y, and 12z³. Add them; divergence is a scalar." 17592186405015] [17592186405811 1058 17592186405774 17592186405038] [17592186405811 1062 #uuid "f5f22eaa-bd88-510e-85bd-716da395dcea" 17592186405038] [17592186405811 1063 17592186405216 17592186405038] [17592186405812 1068 17592186406169 17592186405015] [17592186405812 1071 "Source question: 4.\n\nFind $\\partial(x,y,z)/\\partial(u,v,w)$ for $x=2u+8v+4w$, $y=13u+4v+6w$, $z=14u+6v+12w$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405812 1072 #uuid "f5f5c12b-f816-5791-87b5-0b525e4d3b75" 17592186405038] [17592186405812 1074 "The determinant of $\\begin{pmatrix}2&8&4\\\\13&4&6\\\\14&6&12\\end{pmatrix}$ expands to $2(48-36)-8(156-84)+4(78-56)=24-576+88=-464$." 17592186405015] [17592186405813 1089 17592186048314 17592186405015] [17592186405813 1089 17592186050149 17592186405015] [17592186405813 1090 17592186405389 17592186405038] [17592186405813 1091 #uuid "f60ce69c-0541-52d0-9cdd-c539f3f72088" 17592186405038] [17592186405814 1089 17592186049465 17592186405015] [17592186405814 1089 17592186050540 17592186405015] [17592186405814 1090 17592186405517 17592186405038] [17592186405814 1091 #uuid "f6399e6e-d636-57aa-ac64-8c4d2db6dab8" 17592186405038] [17592186405815 1068 17592186406110 17592186405015] [17592186405815 1071 "**Question 4 — Part B**\n\nChoose the graph with the correct curve and direction." 17592186405015] [17592186405815 1072 #uuid "f78a3700-20e8-5028-af24-59a66949243e" 17592186405038] [17592186405815 1074 "The circle has center $(0,0)$ and radius $5$. At $t=0$, the point is $(0,5)$ and the velocity is $(5,0)$, so it moves clockwise." 17592186405015] [17592186405816 1089 17592186050149 17592186405015] [17592186405816 1089 17592186050891 17592186405015] [17592186405816 1090 17592186405683 17592186405038] [17592186405816 1091 #uuid "f7921811-246a-59af-9fa5-9597d633a6ea" 17592186405038] [17592186405817 1058 17592186405687 17592186405038] [17592186405817 1062 #uuid "f7a24688-854b-5cbb-9056-5c4d15b3ef99" 17592186405038] [17592186405817 1063 17592186405258 17592186405038] [17592186405818 1089 17592186050891 17592186405015] [17592186405818 1090 17592186405423 17592186405038] [17592186405818 1091 #uuid "f8bfadfc-e70d-52fa-9c9d-58ff3e231e6b" 17592186405038] [17592186405819 1058 17592186405357 17592186405038] [17592186405819 1062 #uuid "f92702c4-b208-593b-9e6c-248e50d43140" 17592186405038] [17592186405819 1063 17592186405077 17592186405038] [17592186405820 1089 17592186048923 17592186405015] [17592186405820 1089 17592186050418 17592186405015] [17592186405820 1090 17592186405651 17592186405038] [17592186405820 1091 #uuid "f9e859c6-a85c-5a15-83fe-b2b68f853143" 17592186405038] [17592186405821 1085 17592186405204 17592186405038] [17592186405821 1085 17592186405618 17592186405038] [17592186405821 1086 "Source question: 2.\n\nFor $x(t)=8t+1$ and $y(t)=t+8$, with $-\\infty<t<\\infty$, eliminate the parameter. Use exact values and fractions where needed." 17592186405038] [17592186405821 1087 #uuid "fa0d4f6b-d818-5b0f-9ba8-7888c2a3101c" 17592186405038] [17592186405821 1088 17592186405204 17592186405038] [17592186405822 1071 "**Question 4 — Part A**\n\nAn integral curve that is a solution curve." 17592186405038] [17592186405822 1072 #uuid "fa3e5d36-38b5-5c37-97cd-541aae5d3cdb" 17592186405038] [17592186405822 1074 "In the upper-left part of the supplied field, trace the upper arc of a small closed trajectory surrounding approximately $(-2,0)$. Use only the portion above the $x$-axis between its left and right vertical turns, excluding those endpoints. Label this arc “solution curve.” It follows the local directions and gives one $y$ for each $x$ on that open interval.\n\nUse the displayed field to trace the arc; no exact equation is supplied. A solution graph must be single-valued and have finite derivative on its interval, so stop before the vertical turns rather than including a complete loop." 17592186405015] [17592186405823 1089 17592186048960 17592186405015] [17592186405823 1089 17592186049859 17592186405015] [17592186405823 1090 17592186405439 17592186405038] [17592186405823 1091 #uuid "fad67b1b-8827-5dd4-8516-ee49051ec6d6" 17592186405038] [17592186405824 1068 17592186405885 17592186405015] [17592186405824 1068 17592186406122 17592186405015] [17592186405824 1071 "Source question: 10.\n\nDetermine whether $\\mathbf F=\\langle x^2+y^{10},10xy^9-\\sin y\\rangle$ is conservative. Then find a potential f, using C for the arbitrary constant; enter DNE if it is not conservative.\n\n$f(x,y)=$ {{potential}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405824 1072 #uuid "fae0d756-b749-5dd5-85b8-a109dcf0482d" 17592186405038] [17592186405824 1074 "Integrate P in x: $f=x^3/3+xy^{10}+g(y)$. Matching Q yields g′=−sin y, hence g=cos y+C. Its gradient checks both components." 17592186405015] [17592186405825 1058 17592186405605 17592186405038] [17592186405825 1062 #uuid "fb18f137-d2a4-5245-adb9-9f6aa5f1befb" 17592186405038] [17592186405825 1063 17592186405611 17592186405038] [17592186405826 1064 "Date: 2026-09-29\nDue: 2026-09-29\n\nSource: [Original worksheet](/api/source?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F255%2FR1%2F255-R1.pdf)\n\n## Instructions\n\nRecitation worksheets are graded for completeness. Complete the worksheet on the printed pages, digitally on the PDF, or on your own paper with the same problem layout. Submit a single PDF to Gradescope under **Recitation Worksheet 1**, with the cover page followed by one page per problem, including blank problems. If a solution spans multiple pages, merge those pages into a single page for that problem. See the original PDF for the full submission instructions.\n\nStudy answers, explanations, and reference graphics are authored additions to the linked source." 17592186405015] [17592186405826 1066 #uuid "fb2e578a-cebf-5e22-aee7-ab14b49bddf4" 17592186405038] [17592186405826 1067 "Assignment information" 17592186405038] [17592186405827 1068 17592186406225 17592186405015] [17592186405827 1071 "Source question: 2.\n\nEvaluate $\\oint_C2x\\,dy$ counterclockwise around the rectangle with vertices (0,0), (6,0), (6,2), (0,2).\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405827 1072 #uuid "fb2f69a0-f906-521b-9a80-545470b72fa5" 17592186405038] [17592186405827 1074 "$Q_x-P_y=2$. The enclosed area is 6·2=12, giving 24. The right side contributes $\\int_0^2 12dy=24$, and the left side contributes zero." 17592186405015] [17592186405828 1068 17592186405897 17592186405015] [17592186405828 1071 "Source question: 11.\n\nFind $\\oint_C y^3dx-x^3dy$, where C is the boundary of the region between $x^2+y^2=4$ and $x^2+y^2=1$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 17592186405015] [17592186405828 1072 #uuid "fb56bd98-3f47-577d-b704-676611da0785" 17592186405038] [17592186405828 1074 "Using the standard positive boundary orientation (outer circle counterclockwise, inner circle clockwise), $Q_x-P_y=-3r^2$. Thus $I=-3\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\int_1^2r^3drd\\theta=-45\\pi/2$. The source does not explicitly specify orientation; reversing the full boundary changes the sign." 17592186405015] [17592186405829 1058 17592186405332 17592186405038] [17592186405829 1062 #uuid "fba15544-dc64-5de9-ae9a-da36e00a9978" 17592186405038] [17592186405829 1063 17592186405525 17592186405038] [17592186405830 1089 17592186048259 17592186405015] [17592186405830 1090 17592186405333 17592186405038] [17592186405830 1091 #uuid "fbf749c0-f12d-5644-9b91-f7abc1f1a583" 17592186405038] [17592186405831 1058 17592186405512 17592186405038] [17592186405831 1062 #uuid "fc723400-8f4d-5c38-9d2f-415e7016e56d" 17592186405038] [17592186405831 1063 17592186405213 17592186405038] [17592186405832 1089 17592186050537 17592186405015] [17592186405832 1089 17592186050866 17592186405015] [17592186405832 1090 17592186405104 17592186405038] [17592186405832 1091 #uuid "fd9f92de-e279-504a-8168-89f6490bdab8" 17592186405038] [17592186405833 1068 17592186405846 17592186405015] [17592186405833 1071 "Source question: 3.\n\n**Question 3**\n\nChoose the graph of $\\mathbf F(x,y)=x\\mathbf i+y\\mathbf j$." 17592186405015] [17592186405833 1072 #uuid "fdf61de5-34fd-5dfa-b31c-3ada3d574e7d" 17592186405038] [17592186405833 1074 "The field $\\langle x,y\\rangle$ equals the position vector. Arrows point radially outward, with equal horizontal and vertical components where $x=y$, as shown." 17592186405015] [17592186405834 1058 17592186405424 17592186405038] [17592186405834 1062 #uuid "fe0c9275-4782-5f6d-97ee-e7597710e77d" 17592186405038] [17592186405834 1063 17592186405290 17592186405038] [17592186405835 1068 17592186406273 17592186405015] [17592186405835 1071 "Source question: 20.\n\nFind the work of $\\mathbf F=\\langle4x,7y\\rangle$ along $\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle4\\cos t,5\\sin t\\